{"id": "212815", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=212815", "title": "Maclean", "text": "Maclean is a town of 2,800 (2021) in the Northern Rivers region of New South Wales. It's a nice riverside town along the Clarence River and a very short detour from the M1 Pacific Mwy between Sydney and Brisbane.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nMaclean is on the M1 Pacific Mwy, about 115 km north of Coffs Harbour, 90 km north of Woolgoolga and 95 km south of Ballina.\nFrom Grafton, it is 46 km northeast via the B76 Big River Way.\nBy coach and bus.\nLocal buses connect Maclean to Grafton, Yamba and Iluka.\nThere is also a once-daily coach between Grafton and Byron Bay via Ballina, which connects with the Casino XPT headed northbound or the Brisbane XPT headed southbound."}
{"id": "212820", "revid": "1789", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=212820", "title": "Diving the Cape Peninsula and False Bay/South Island Reef", "text": "The dive site South Island Reef is an offshore rocky reef east of the Whittle Rock area on the central part of False Bay, near Cape Town in the Western Cape province of South Africa.\nUnderstand.\nFound during a multibeam sonar survey of False Bay by Wreckless Marine in about 2022, and first dived in September 2025 by Wreckless Divers. South Island Reef is one of the largest of the Off-Whittle Eastern Reefs.\nFairly large and quite deep reef, pleasant, with varied structure. \n S34\u00b015.1773' E18\u00b036.4963', \nPosition.\nEast of Whittle Rock, southwest of the SATS \"General Botha\" wreck, about halfway across False Bay between Simon's Town and Gordon's Bay. A granite outcrop, rising from about 50\u00a0m on the sand to about 31\u00a0m at the top of the biggest outcrop ridge. This site is not in a Marine Protected Area, and a permit is not required.\nName.\nThe name \"South Island Reef\" was chosen because it is an island of rock in a sea of sand, with another similarly sized reef to the north across a fairly narrow sandy gap, which will be called North Island Reef.\nDepth.\nMaximum depth is about 50\u00a0m on the sand to the south, and the top of the big outcrop is about 31\u00a0m. (Average depth of a dive is likely to be about 40\u00a0m, though there is enough area to stay above 40\u00a0m if you want.)\nVisibility.\nVisibility is likely to be much like at the reefs of the Whittle Rock area of similar depth. It is deep enough to usually be below the algal bloom and associated heavy plankton layer which often develops after a sunny period. The first dive was in about 15\u00a0m visibility, but quite dark as there was a red tide developing in the top 6\u00a0m.\nTopography.\nA fairly large granite outcrop, quite flat on top, but with some high profile areas near the southern edge. There are several low-lying sandy patches within the perimeter of the reef.\nGeology:\nPrecambrian granite of the \"Peninsula\" pluton, surrounded by sand. Possibly the south-easternmost exposed part of the Peninsula pluton in the bay.\nConditions.\nThe site is exposed to wind and waves from all directions, so should be dived in low swells and light winds, and is likely to be at its best in winter but there will also be opportunities during the rest of the year. This is an area which has a thermocline in late summer, and the visibility may be better at depth when there is an algal bloom in the surface water, caused by strong sunshine in summer, resulting in a top layer of poor visibility with clearer but dark water below.\nGet in.\nThe site is only accessible by boat. It is about ??\u00a0km from the slipway at Miller's Point, but boats may also leave from Simon's Town jetty or Gordon's Bay. On a good day in a fast boat it is a bit more than a half hour run from Simon's Town Jetty, a distance of about ??\u00a0km. Occasionally boats may leave from Gordon's Bay Old Harbour or Harbour Island marina, which are both about ??\u00a0km away.\nDo.\nRoutes.\nNo known routes. \nSee.\nFeatures.\nThe occasional high profile areas towards the edges of the reefs, which tend to support more varied invertebrate cover.\nPhotography.\nMacro with artificial lighting. and wide angle with natural lighting if you are lucky with visibility and natural illumination.\nStay safe.\nHazards.\nThere are no known site-specific hazards other than the depth. Much of the site is within the depth range usually accepted as suitable for deep recreational diving, but not for emergency swimming ascent. Surface currents due to wind are likely and may take the divers away from the descent area during an ascent with decompression stops.\nSkills.\nThe competence to follow suitable decompression procedures is strongly recommended. This includes carrying and using appropriate gases and deploying a decompression buoy to mark the position of the divers during ascent.\nEquipment.\nAdequate and reliable alternative breathing gas supply, as the depth is beyond the range for which emergency swimming ascent is a reasonable response. A decompression buoy is strongly recommended, and may be required by the service provider. A fully redundant emergency gas supply is recommended. Use of breathing gas mixtures appropriate to the depth is recommended.\nNearby.\n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"TMNPMPA\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.470833,-34.109833],[18.556883,-34.109833],\n[18.556883,-34.407400],[18.250000,-34.407400],\n[18.250000,-33.901250],[18.400617,-33.901250,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Table Mountain National Park Marine Protected Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ffff00\",\n \"stroke-width\":3\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"KRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.364117,-33.979283],[18.296617,-33.979283],\n[18.296617,-34.061000],[18.337533,-34.061000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Karbonkelberg Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"COGHRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.369900,-34.209517],[18.369900,-34.294833],\n[18.391667,-34.306650],[18.404300,-34.306650,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Cape of Good Hope Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"PRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.474400,-34.275817],[18.483333,-34.275817],\n[18.483333,-34.295733],[18.467000,-34.295733,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Paulsberg Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"CRRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.475133,-34.235000],[18.488333,-34.235000],\n[18.488333,-34.258000],[18.472400,-34.258000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Castle Rock Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"BRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.446033,-34.192783],[18.453267,-34.176350],\n[18.463017,-34.176350],[18.463017,-34.211750,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Boulders Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"SJRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.459467,-34.118717],[18.459467,-34.126117],[18.450833,-34.126117,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"St James Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt;\nOther offshore dive sites of False Bay:\nBack to the Alphabetical list of sites, or list of reef dive sites in the Whittle Rock and surrounds offshore area\nOther regional dive sites:"}
{"id": "212822", "revid": "1789", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=212822", "title": "Diving the Cape Peninsula and False Bay/Bill Liltved's Reef", "text": "The dive site Bill Liltved's Reef is an offshore rocky reef in False Bay, near Cape Town in the Western Cape province of South Africa.\nUnderstand.\nBill Liltved's Reef is an area of reef to the northwest of Whittle Rock. It is the furthest north reef in the Off-Whittle Eastern Reefs area. The highest point is at about ??\u00a0m depth, and the surrounding sand is at about ??\u00a0m. The reef has been surveyed by multibeam sonar in 2022, but high resolution images are not yet published It is considerably smaller than the main reef at Whittle Rock, but still quite large and there may eventually have more than one dive site identified by drop point coordinates.\n S34\u00b014.0238' E18\u00b035.2632'. Northeast of Whittle Rock.\nPosition.\nThis site is not in a Marine Protected Area. A permit is not required.\nName.\nThe name \"Bill Liltved's Reef\" is in recognition of William Rune Liltved, after whom the stalked cauliflower soft coral \"Pseudonephthea liltvedi\" (Verseveldt &amp; Williams, 1988) is named. There are quite a lot of these corals at the reef, some unusually large.\nDepth.\nMaximum depth is probably deeper than 32\u00a0m at the sand, and the top of the ridge is about ??\u00a0m. Average depth of a dive is likely to be about 27\u00a0m, excluding ascent.\nVisibility.\nVisibility may be better than on the inshore reefs, but as with any False Bay reef, it is not very predictable, and there may be better visibility at the bottom below a dirty surface layer of plankton, which can follow a period of strong sunshine. Visibility can exceed 20\u00a0m on rare and unpredictable occasions. The colour of the surface water is not a reliable indicator of the visibility at depth. \nAt times the variation in visibility between sites can be surprising. There have been days when visibility at the \"Fleur\" has been less than 2\u00a0m, and very dark below 30\u00a0m, while only 6.5\u00a0km away at Whittle Rock, visibility was over 15\u00a0m at 30\u00a0m depth, and brightly illuminated by sunlight.\nTopography.\nThis fairly extensive area of reef includes some high profile areas. The topography is rugged in the area visited. High profile granite reef. Large boulders and pinnacle outcrops with gullies with smaller boulders and a few small sandy patches.\nGeology:\nGranite corestone outcrop of the Pre-Cambrian \"Peninsula\" pluton, closely jointed in places.\nConditions.\nThe site is exposed to wind and waves from all directions, and swells from the southwest, however it is quite deep, so short period waves will not affect conditions on the bottom greatly. Low short swell and light wind is best. The prevailing long period swell is from the southwest, and if long enough can cause surge even on the deep reef. Other waves are generally short period wind waves and chop, but the reef is far enough offshore for a vicious chop to develop in a strong wind, which can be very uncomfortable on the ride back to Simon's Town. The area is protected from north-westerly swell, and to a lesser extent south-westerly swell by the Cape Peninsula, but longer period south-westerly swell will refract round Cape Point, and though attenuated, will reach this area. \nSurface currents have been measured at up to about 1.5 kilometers per hour, caused by recent wind, and in a similar direction, offset in an anticlockwise Ekman spiral by the Coriolis effect (the current is offset more at greater depth, but proportionately weaker). The skipper should drop divers off a bit up-current of the shotline, which may not be quite the same as upwind of it, and the drift direction of a boat with significant windage is not quite the same as the drift direction of an almost completely immersed diver. Occasionally the current will extend right down to the deep reef, but usually it is shallow. \nWater temperature may vary with depth. A thermocline develops in midsummer, gets deeper in autumn, and dissipates in winter. The visibility may also change significantly below the thermocline. The surface at Whittle Rock nearby can be 18 or 19\u00b0C with 10 or 11\u00b0C at the bottom, but the difference is more likely to be 5\u00b0C or less. Conditions at depth are not easily predictable, and may be better or worse than near the surface. There can be a plankton bloom in the surface layers and a sudden improvement in visibility from 3\u00a0m or less to over 10\u00a0m in the cold bottom water, or, less often, fairly clean surface water, but dirty at depth. This dirty bottom water is more common at deeper offshore sites in False Bay, such as the wrecks of the \"Fleur\" (40\u00a0m), \"General Botha\" (54\u00a0m), and \"Bloemfontein\" (57\u00a0m). The depth of the thermocline is also not very predictable, but has been known to be between 12 and 20\u00a0m in late summer. \nIn winter the water may be the same temperature from top to bottom, and as there is less sunlight to power the phytoplankton blooms, the visibility and natural illumination can be better even though there is less light. There is no specific time of year for diving this site, you just have to wait for low swell and light winds, and take your chances with visibility. \nA long period swell may produce significant surge at depth, depending on the local topography. The reef is far enough to the west of False Bay that the diffraction of south westerly waves around Cape Point and refraction over Rocky Bank can dissipate a fair amount of the wave energy that would otherwise cross the reef, but the amount is not easily predictable, though the shoreline break on the Strandfontein coast can give some indication.\nGet in.\nThis site is only accessible by boat. It is about 15.6\u00a0km from Simon's Town Jetty, or 10\u00a0km from Miller's Point slipway. On a good day in a fast boat it is about a half hour run from Simon's Town Jetty.\nAnchoring is not recommended. The holding ground is unreliable, and there is a good chance that the anchor will either drag or foul, possibly both, and a rope rode may chafe through during a dive. This is a site where only the suicidally foolhardy would dive from an unattended boat, and besides dragging or fouling, the anchor tackle will do undesirable and unnecessary ecological damage. Leave the boat in the charge of a competent person who will be able to pick up stray divers and call for help in an emergency.\nDo.\nDive at one of the drop points listed and explore the vicinity, or choose another point and see what you find. Most of the reef is unexplored, and though it has been surveyed by multibeam sonar, the images have not yet been published. Depth does not usually vary much within a reasonable distance as the reef is not very steep overall and profile is moderate to low in most places.\nSee.\nMarine life.\nThere are some magnificent specimens of white stalked cauliflower soft corals \"Pseudonephthea liltvedi\" (Verseveldt &amp; Williams, 1988)on the tops of several small ridges and high profile outcrops. There were large shoals of juvenile strepie and moderate shoals of hottentot seabream, puffadder shysharks, redfingers, Good variety of invertebrates, including gorgonian sea fans and cauliflower soft corals, and a few species of nudibranch, with strawberry anemones and various sponges and ascidians.\nPhotography.\nThe topography is quite scenic, but the most photogenic subjects are the sessile invertebrates, so close-up and macro photography with artificial lighting are most likely to produce good results.\nStay safe.\nHazards.\nThere are no known site-specific hazards.\nNearby.\n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"TMNPMPA\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.470833,-34.109833],[18.556883,-34.109833],\n[18.556883,-34.407400],[18.250000,-34.407400],\n[18.250000,-33.901250],[18.400617,-33.901250,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Table Mountain National Park Marine Protected Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ffff00\",\n \"stroke-width\":3\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"KRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.364117,-33.979283],[18.296617,-33.979283],\n[18.296617,-34.061000],[18.337533,-34.061000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Karbonkelberg Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"COGHRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.369900,-34.209517],[18.369900,-34.294833],\n[18.391667,-34.306650],[18.404300,-34.306650,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Cape of Good Hope Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"PRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.474400,-34.275817],[18.483333,-34.275817],\n[18.483333,-34.295733],[18.467000,-34.295733,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Paulsberg Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"CRRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.475133,-34.235000],[18.488333,-34.235000],\n[18.488333,-34.258000],[18.472400,-34.258000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Castle Rock Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"BRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.446033,-34.192783],[18.453267,-34.176350],\n[18.463017,-34.176350],[18.463017,-34.211750,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Boulders Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"SJRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.459467,-34.118717],[18.459467,-34.126117],[18.450833,-34.126117,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"St James Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt;\nOther offshore dive sites of False Bay:\nBack to the Alphabetical list of sites, or list of reef dive sites in the Whittle Rock and surrounds offshore area\nOther regional dive sites:"}
{"id": "212836", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=212836", "title": "Zona Volc\u00e0nica de la Garrotxa Natural Park", "text": "Zona Volc\u00e0nica de la Garrotxa Natural Park is in Girona (province), the best example of volcanic landscape on the Iberian Peninsula. It has about forty volcanic cones and more than 20 basaltic lava flows. None of its many volcanoes are still active.\nUnderstand.\nThere are 3 main parts on the park\nThe park is in La Garrotxa county, which has an entrepreneurial spirit that is reflected in its culture and social network, in its traditions, and also in its economy and gastronomy.\nIn La Garrotxa, a deep-rooted tradition with long experience in artistic teaching stands out, related to the centuries-old religious imagery industry. This spirit is also reflected in Garrotxa society, with an associative network with many entities \u2014 there are more than 350 socio-cultural entities in the natural park \u2014 and where popular festivals and traditions are well-rooted and very participatory. The industry and services of the region are mostly run by local companies with great drive. Many have expanded beyond the boundaries of the region. Gastronomy has always been a pillar of the region and has known how to take advantage of a quality pantry.\nHistory.\nThe Parliament of Catalonia unanimously approved the creation of the volcanic area of \u200b\u200bLa Garrotxa, declaring the area a Natural Site of National Interest, with the aim of preserving its flora, its geomorphological constitution and its special beauty, and in view of the unique character of the territory. \nIt also declared twenty integral reserves of geobotanical interest, with the aim of avoiding any action that could lead to the destruction, deterioration, transformation or disfigurement of its geomorphology or flora.\nThe first Special Protection Plan for the natural park was approved in 1994 and the current one was approved in 2010.\nLandscape.\n40 catalogued volcanoes, with 10 well-preserved phreatomagmatic explosion craters and 23 well-preserved scoria cones resulting from Strombolian activity (Croscat, Montsacopa, Bisaroques, Rocanegra\u2026), More than 20 lava flows, one of which is the best-preserved block flow in the Iberian Peninsula (Fageda d'en Jord\u00e0)\nThe volcanoes age is between 8,000 years and 700,000 years.\nClimate.\nThe park has a humid, mild Mediterranean mountain climate, with abundant rainfall and cool summers.The area experiences rain well distributed throughout the year, although winter is the driest season. Frequent showers and cloud cover moderate summer temperatures, while the proximity of the Pyrenees brings cold winters with occasional frosts. This climate supports lush vegetation, including holm oak, oak and beech forests, which change with altitude and exposure. Overall, the park\u2019s climate creates green landscapes, comfortable summer hiking conditions and marked seasonal contrasts in temperature and humidity.\nFlora and fauna.\nThe flora represented in this space is extremely rich, due to its location and biogeographic history, both quantitatively (some 1,125 species of higher plants have been identified) and qualitatively (there are several very rare plants in the Catalan flora as a whole), with a clear predominance of Mediterranean and Central European species. Almost 65% of the natural park is occupied by forest formations, especially holm oak, oak and beech forests (\"fageda\" in Catalan), but also mixed forests and oak forests.\nGet in.\nThe protected area is made up of the territory of 11 municipalities and has different entrances.\nBy train.\nNo easy train connections. Closest trains are in Girona and Ripoll.\nBy bus.\nTake the Bus lines that from Figueres, Girona, Ripoll, Vic, or Barcelona reach the towns of the Natural Park, from the Teisa company.\nRUMBUS is a public transport service operated by TEISA that runs along the park main attractions. The route is Les Preses - centre of Olot - Fageda d\u2019en Jord\u00e0 - \u00c0rea de Santa Margarida - Santa Pau runs on weekends, bank holidays, public holidays and other dates.\nThe ticket allows, for only \u20ac2 (Sep 2025), an unlimited number of trips throughout the day and thus discover, at your own pace, all the attractions of the central area of the Garrotxa Volcanic Zone Natural Park.\nYou can purchase the ticket online in advance on the TEISA website, or on the bus on the same day.\nBy car.\nThe main park entrance is near Olot in road \u00a0GI-524\u00a0 \nTo access Sant Feliu de Pallerols use \u00a0C-63\u00a0 from \u00a0AP-7\u00a0 (\u00a0E15\u00a0)\nTo access Santa Pau follow \u00a0GI-524\u00a0 from Olot or \u00a0GI-524\u00a0 from Banyoles\nFees and permits.\nIt's free to enter. \nOrganized groups (school and university groups, hikers, cultural, leisure groups, etc.) who want to access the natural reserves of the Croscat volcano, the Fageda d'en Jord\u00e0 and the Santa Margarida volcano must request authorization to visit.For more information you can contact the Park management team pnzvg@gencat.cat\nDrink.\nLook for bars and cafeterias in the villages around the park.\nSleep.\nThere are several Hotels in Olot"}
{"id": "212837", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=212837", "title": "Sulej\u00f3wek", "text": "Sulej\u00f3wek is a town in Mazowieckie, close to Warsaw. It is best known as the birthplace of Polish national hero J\u00f3zef Pi\u0142sudski.\nUnderstand.\nSulej\u00f3wek was once a private village owned by Polish nobility. \nBuy.\nUlica Dworcowa near the train station has the standard range of shops you might expect to find in a small Polish town.\nSleep.\nSulej\u00f3wek has a variety of hotels, many taking advantage of its tree-lined surroundings to provide a relaxing atmosphere."}
{"id": "212843", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=212843", "title": "Hapcheon", "text": "Hapcheon is a county in northwestern South Gyeongsang, South Korea. It is best known for Haeinsa, a UNESCO World Heritage temple, and Gayasan National Park, which surrounds it. Hapcheon also features scenic mountains, rivers, and rural landscapes, making it ideal for hiking, temple stays, and experiencing traditional Korean culture.\nUnderstand.\nHapcheon is a city located in the northwest of Gyeongsangnam-do and is widely known as Gayasan National Park and Haeinsa Temple. In particular, Haeinsa Temple is considered one of the centers of Buddhist culture as it stores the Palman Daejanggyeong, which is registered as a UNESCO World Heritage Site. In addition, tourists visit all four seasons thanks to its beautiful natural scenery such as Hwangmaesan Azalea and Hapcheonho Lake. It is popular as a day trip destination because it is close to Daegu, and it is a place where you can enjoy Korean traditional culture and nature at the same time.\nHistory.\nThe Hapcheon area belonged to the Gaya culture before the Three Kingdoms period, and was later incorporated into Silla. Due to the geographical characteristics of many mountainous areas, it was formed as an area centered on temples and rural areas rather than developing into large-scale cities. During the Goryeo Dynasty, Haeinsa Temple, located at the foot of Gayasan Mountain, became the center of Buddhist culture. The Palman Daejanggyeong, made against Mongol invasions in the 13th century, was kept here, To this day, it is regarded as the most complete Buddhist woodblock print in the world. Hapcheon remained a relatively quiet inland area until the Joseon Dynasty and modern times, Temple culture and traditional rural life continued steadily. In the late 20th century, Haeinsa Janggyeong Panjeon and Palman Daejanggyeong were listed as UNESCO World Heritage Sites, Hapcheon became widely known as a travel destination representing Korean Buddhist culture and tradition.\nClimate.\nHapcheon is located in the inland of Gyeongsangnam-do, so it is generally dry and has a large daily temperature difference.\nIn winter (December-February), the average minimum temperature drops to -5\u00b0C, and it is very cold in the morning and evening.\nIn summer (June-August), the average maximum temperature is around 30\u00b0C, and the humidity is high.\nIn particular, it rains frequently during the rainy season from July to August, so raincoats or waterproof shoes are needed.\nApril-May and September-October are the best times for outdoor tourism such as Haeinsa Temple and Gayasan Mountain, as the temperature is pleasant and there are many clear days."}
{"id": "212856", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=212856", "title": "Spanish Florida", "text": "Prior to its annexation by the United States, Florida was a colony of Spain. From the founding of St. Augustine in 1565 to the collapse of their empire in the 1820s, the Spanish built forts, missions, and attempted to settle the subtropical, humid peninsula despite competition from other European powers.\nUnderstand.\nThe history of Spanish colonial interests in Florida can be separated into two distinct periods: 1513-1763 and 1783-1821. During the first Spanish period, Florida was one territory ruled through the Captaincy General of Cuba, while during the second Spansh period, it was divided into West and East Florida with capitals in Pensacola and St. Augustine respectively.\nThe first European explorer of Florida was Ponce de Le\u00f3n. Various sites in the state have been named after him, such as DeLeon Springs, Ponce Inlet, and the Ponce de Le\u00f3n Fountain of Youth in St. Augustine. However, despite his and others' explorations across the Southeastern U.S., the Spanish took little interest in the region due to its small Native population and lack of natural resources. Spanish settlement did not begin in earnest until 1565, when Admiral Pedro Men\u00e9ndez de Avil\u00e9s founded the colony of St. Augustine on the Florida Peninsula's eastern coast. The admiral and his settlers established Mission Nombre de Dios and, subsequently, missions throughout what is now Florida, Georgia, and South Carolina. However, apart from Nombre de Dios, these missions were established as temporary structures. Once the British began colonizing Georgia and South Carolina in earnest, the Spanish abandoned their northernmost missions and they vanished entirely. The original capital of Spanish Florida, Santa Elena near modern Beaufort, South Carolina, was abandoned in the late 16th century and nothing remains of the site above ground.\nSpanish efforts in today's state of Florida, though, were more successful. In the 17th century, the Spanish established a second colony in Pensacola and built a fort \u2014 Castillo de San Marcos \u2014 in St. Augustine. This came at a cost, as the Spanish used their remaining missions between the two colonies to recruit indigenous tribes for labor. Contact with these tribes led to their eradication by infectious diseases. Seminoles moved in from the north and settled Florida in their place, becoming the dominant population of Florida outside of Spanish settlements and runaway African slaves. By the 18th century, the decline of the Spanish Empire, combined with failed attempts at settlement and war against the British, led Spain to neglect Florida. In 1763, Spain handed over Florida to Britain in a peace treaty that concluded the Seven Years' War. The British divided Spanish Florida into two colonies, West and East Florida, but did little with these colonies during their twenty years in control.\nAfter the American War of Independence, the British returned Florida to Spain. The Spanish maintained the division of the two colonies, but this time they took a more active role in settlement. Realizing the Americans had their eyes on the region, the Spanish aimed to establish clear control. They built the St. Augustine Basilica, founded Fernandina Beach, and presided over rapid population growth in the previously underpopulated Pensacola. Their fortunes were short-lived, though, as American General Andrew Jackson used the pretext of the Seminoles to lead incursions into Florida. With the Napoleonic Wars in Europe and wars for independence taking root across Latin America, the Spanish handed over both its Florida colonies to the United States and focused on holding onto its crumbling empire.\nAlthough most of Spain's rural missions were lost to history, the Spanish district of St. Augustine was preserved. A few other sites survived in part, including a couple of houses built in Pensacola during the second Spanish period and some underwater shipwrecks off the coast. Museums in St. Augustine and the McLarty Treasure Museum farther south contain artifacts of Spanish colonization. In the late 20th and early 21st centuries, historians began reconstructing Spanish colonial sites in Florida. Examples include Gracia Real de Santa Teresa de Mose (Fort Mose) and Mission San Luis in Tallahassee.\nFurther reading.\nA bilingual guidebook, \"Florida's Spanish Colonial Heritage Trail\" (\"Herencia Colonial Espa\u00f1ola de Florida\") details the history of the Spanish colonial period and lists historical sites and museums across the state. It is available from the Florida Department of State.\nGo next.\nSpanish colonial architecture became popular in Florida in the early 20th century when the state was firmly a part of the U.S. Examples of Spanish architecture built during the late 19th and early 20th centuries are abundant in Winter Park, Naples, and Miami. is one of the most architecturally significant examples of the Spanish style in Winter Park, and the nearby and in Orlando bear resemblance to the St. Augustine Basilica. In Miami stands Florida's oldest structure of European origin, the stunning, which was shipped in pieces from Spain in the 20th century but had been built in the 12th century.\nAn American site with strong connections to Spanish Cuba is, a massive fort in the Dry Tortugas Islands west of the Florida Keys."}
{"id": "212865", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=212865", "title": "Pakur", "text": "Pakur (Hindi: \u092a\u093e\u0915\u0941\u0921\u093c \"P\u0101ku\u1e5b\", Bengali: \u09aa\u09be\u0995\u09c1\u09a1\u09bc \"P\u0101ku\u1e5b\") is a mining town in the Santhal Parganas region of Jharkhand, close to Birbhum and Murshidabad districts of West Bengal."}
{"id": "212866", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=212866", "title": "Karmatanr", "text": "Karmatanr (Hindi: \u0915\u0930\u092e\u093e\u091f\u093e\u0902\u0921\u093c \"Karm\u0101\u1e6d\u0101n\u1e5b\", Bengali: \u0995\u09be\u09b0\u09ae\u09be\u099f\u09be\u0981\u09a1\u09bc \"K\u0101rm\u0101\u1e6d\u0101n\u1e5b\") is a hamlet in the Santhal Parganas region of Jharkhand, associated with the later life of Bengali writer Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar.\nUnderstand.\nIshwar Chandra Vidyasagar came to Karmatanr in 1873 and spent the last 18 years there. There, he established a girls' school and an adult night school on the premises of his residence named \"Nandan K\u0101nan\" (Hindi: \u0928\u0902\u0926\u0928 \u0915\u093e\u0928\u0928, Bengali: \u09a8\u09a8\u09cd\u09a6\u09a8\u0995\u09be\u09a8\u09a8), literally the \"garden of delight\". He also opened a free homoeopathy clinic to provide some alternative therapy to the unprivileged tribes.\nAfter his death, his son sold Nandan Kanan to the Mallick family of Calcutta (present-day Kolkata). It later came under the Bihar Bengalee Association on 29 March 1974. The girls' school was reopened in 1976 as Vidyasagar Balika Vidyalaya and closed in 2014. The homoeopathy clinic is still active."}
{"id": "212877", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=212877", "title": "Tecumseh", "text": "Tecumseh is a city in Lenawee County inside Southeast Michigan. no population data for this page\nGet around.\nDriving is the best way to get around town."}
{"id": "212926", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=212926", "title": "Ak\u00e7aabat", "text": "Ak\u00e7aabat is a city in the Eastern Karadeniz region on Turkey's Black Sea coast, to the west of the city of Trabzon. Ak\u00e7aabat is known for its local soccer team Ak\u00e7aabat Sebatspor, its kofta dish Ak\u00e7aabat k\u00f6fte, and the Ak\u00e7aabat Horonu dance. Ak\u00e7aabat has hosted an international folklore festival since 1990. In 2022 the population was 129,000. \nUnderstand.\nThis port shares the history of Trabzon, in being settled by Hellenistic people (who called it Pl\u00e1tanos, \u03a0\u03bb\u03ac\u03c4\u03b1\u03bd\u03bf\u03c2, for its plane trees) then forming part of the Pontic kingdom then Roman and Byzantine empires, falling to the Ottomans in 1461. It was always subsidiary to Trabzon because inland access here is blocked by the mountains, with the valley roads petering out into goat tracks. It nevertheless burgeoned in modern times as mountain-dwellers moved into town, and migrant workers in Germany returned home.\nPerhaps the most entertaining character to come out of Ak\u00e7aabat, if you didn't have to share a room with him, was the writer Kadir M\u0131s\u0131ro\u011flu (1933 \u2013 2019). He opposed Atat\u00fcrk's secular reforms and yearned to restore a caliphate, which he reckoned would get US backing. He claimed that Stalin ordered the Red Army to read the Quran in order to repel the Nazis, that William Shakespeare was a secret Muslim, and that Karl Marx's \"Das Kapital\" was dictated by demons.\nGet in.\nTrabzon Airport () is 10 km east of that city. It has hourly flights from Istanbul (IST and SAW) and at least daily from Ankara, Izmir, Adana and Ercan. Take the bus downtown for transport out to Ak\u00e7aabat.\nHighway D010 follows almost the length of the Black Sea coast, west through Giresun, Ordu and Samsun, and east to Trabzon (15 km), Rize and Hopa near the border with Georgia.\nRoads inland all dead-end in the mountains. Go via Trabzon for E97 across to G\u00fcm\u00fc\u015fhane and into Anatolia.\nBuses from Samsun take 5 hours; they're hourly round the clock, heading for Trabzon. Some start from Istanbul, and continue to Batumi in Georgia.\nBus operators include Metro Turizm, Flixbus and Ulusoy. \n the bus station is by the marina.\nGet around.\nDolmu\u015fes and taxis play the main streets.\n Ak\u00e7aabat Sebatspor play down in the amateur leagues, though they were in S\u00fcper Lig until 2005. Their Fatih stadium (capacity 6200) also hosts athletics.\nBuy.\nK\u00fclt\u00fcr Park is a shopping centre next to the bus station.\nLots of small stores, such as Asya Market by Town Mosque at \u0130n\u00f6n\u00fc Cd 175, open daily 07:30-22:30.\nDrink.\nA few cafes serve beer.\nConnect.\nAk\u00e7aabat and the coast highway have 4G from all Turkish carriers. As of Sep 2025, 5G has not rolled out in Turkey."}
{"id": "212929", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=212929", "title": "Delta de l'Ebre Natural Park", "text": "Delta de l'Ebre Natural Park is in Tarragona (province) of Catalonia. One of the most emblematic birds of the Delta are the flamingos, which are present all year round. The Ebro Way of St. James starts in the park which is inside the UNESCO Biosphere Reserve \"Terres de l\u2019Ebre, Catalonia\".\nUnderstand.\nThe Ebro Delta covers 320 km\u00b2: 20% of which are natural areas (the current natural park), 75% are farmland and the rest is urban.\nHistory.\nIt was declared a protected area in 1983, and was later expanded in 1986 to cover 7,736 hectares. The protection of this area had a precedent in 1962 when, at the request of the scientific community, the Ebro Delta was included in the classification of Euro-African wetlands of the Bureau MAR with the category of urgent priority. In accordance with a directive of the European Economic Community on the conservation of birds, it was declared a special protection area. The protection was extended in March 1993, by which the park was included in the list of wetlands of international importance (R\u0101msar Convention), especially as a habitat for waterfowl. In 2013, UNESCO declared the Terres de l'Ebre Biosphere Reserve, which included the Ebro Delta Natural Park, in addition to other areas.\nLandscape.\nThe Ebro River brings the materials torn up along its course to deposit them here, in conjunction with the Mediterranean Sea. The amount of sedimented materials have created an extensive alluvial plain, which projects nearly 22 km into the sea in the shape of a wedge, and in which numerous habitats have formed. The large hydroelectric power plants have created large dams that have slowed the growth of the delta and even caused it to retreat due to lack of sediment.\nClimate.\nThe Parc enjoys a Mediterranean climate with mild temperatures (annual average ~17\u00b0C), high humidity (~70%), and low rainfall (~400 mm/year), concentrated in spring and autumn. Summers are warm and sunny (highs ~28\u00b0C), ideal for birdwatching amid rice paddies, while winters stay cool (lows ~8\u00b0C) with windy \"vent de dalt\" from the northwest.\nPersistent levante winds bring occasional storms, shaping lagoons like Encanyissada and Ribera de la Tancada. Climate change threatens via sea-level rise and erosion, yet the delta's buffered microclimate sustains flamingos, rice cycles, and wetlands year-round.\nFlora and fauna.\nThe lands of this delta have a notable salinity gradient that ranges from salty soils to fertile lands that are considerably suitable for cultivation, and this generates a variety of vegetation of great interest. For this reason, the plant mass of these places offers notable particularities. The reed beds and reed beds are of extreme importance.\nThe vegetation that occupies the banks of the river corresponds to the only forest communities of the delta, that is, the well-known riparian forests, among which the white poplar (Populus alba), especially present on the Illa de Gr\u00e0cia, stands out.\nThe humidity of the Ebro delta (including the park) is of international importance for eight species of plants and 69 of vertebrate fauna, most of which are birds. There are nests of 95 species, where they winter, rest and feed during the migrations of many other species of birds. The Ebro Delta has about 325 species of birds out of the 600 existing in Europe.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nTo get to Amposta\nand to the park\nDo.\nBirdwatching.\nL'Encanyissada\nLa Tancada\nBassa de l'Alfacada\nGarxao Gran\nLes olles\nPunta de la Banya\nBuy.\nAround the park there are rice crops and high quality salt producers. Buy at the local shops in the villages around the park.\nDrink.\nThere are bar &amp; cafeterias in the villages around the parc"}
{"id": "212930", "revid": "1516949", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=212930", "title": "Coast Starlight", "text": "The Coast Starlight is an Amtrak train line that goes up and down the West Coast of the United States, from Seattle to Los Angeles via Portland and the San Francisco Bay Area.\nSee rail travel in the United States for further information about Amtrak and other train lines.\nUnderstand.\nAlthough the moniker is \"Coast Starlight\", the coast is visible for only a short while between Los Angeles and San Luis Obispo, however the route does also pass through some pine forests with excellent vistas of mountain peaks in Oregon and Washington. Service is daily and takes 35 hours to complete. The train crosses most of three states: Washington, Oregon, and California.\nThe train is scheduled to reach most stations (except the sparsely populated northern third of California) at somewhat reasonable hours, assuming no delays. Going south, the Coast Starlight leaves Seattle around mid-morning and crosses the Oregon-California border in the middle of the night, to reach Sacramento early in the morning and the Bay Area an hour or two later, then roll into Los Angeles in the evening. Going north, it leaves Los Angeles in the mid-morning, hits the Bay Area in the evening, Sacramento in the middle of the night, crosses the Oregon border early in the morning, and reaches Portland in the late afternoon and Seattle in the evening.\nCoach and private sleeper accommodations are available. Meals in the dining car are included for sleeper car passengers but cost extra for coach passengers. Food is also available from the cafe car.\nGet in.\nYou can start and end at any of the stations; major cities on the way include Los Angeles, Santa Barbara, San Jose, Oakland, San Francisco (via Emeryville), Sacramento, Portland, and Seattle. It's also feasible to connect to or from another train, in particular the Cascades from Vancouver, Canada; the Empire Builder from Chicago to Seattle and Portland; the California Zephyr from Chicago, which connects with the Coast Starlight in Emeryville; the Southwest Chief from Chicago to Los Angeles; and the Pacific Surfliner from San Diego. \"Thruway\" buses also connect some stations to nearby destinations. If you want to make any of these connections, you should book it all as one ticket with Amtrak.\nStations.\nFrom north to south:\nCalifornia.\nDunsmuir, Redding, and Chico are reached in the wee hours both directions.\nAlternatives.\nOn some segments of the line, the Coast Starlight shares tracks with other Amtrak trains that run more often and with more stops \u2013 consider these options too if your travel plans are within one of these well-travelled corridors (if relevant, they should show up as options in Amtrak's booking system):"}
{"id": "212934", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=212934", "title": "Colfax (California)", "text": "Colfax is a town in Placer County, California.\nGet in.\nBy train.\nAmtrak's California Zephyr stops at once per day each direction, on its way between Chicago and the Bay Area, with stops in between including Sacramento, Truckee, Reno, and Denver. The train is scheduled to stop in Colfax at 12:50pm going west and 11:49am going east, but delays are common.\nAmtrak also provides a connecting bus (Route 20) to reach Colfax from the \"Gold Runner\" train (Stockton, Merced, Fresno, Bakersfield, etc.).\nBy bus.\nPlacer County Transit provides limited weekday commuter bus service.\nBy car.\nColfax is on I-80.\nSleep.\nColfax has a couple of hotels."}
{"id": "212965", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=212965", "title": "Farallones de Cali National Natural Park", "text": "Farallones de Cali National Natural Park is a protected natural area in the Pacifica region of western Colombia. It is a popular destination for Hiking and Birdwatching, particularly given its proximity to Cali. \nUnderstand.\nThe park covers an area of more than 196,400 hectares.\nHistory.\nThe park was declared as part of the Colombian national parks system in 1968.\nLandscape.\nThe word \"farallones\" means \"cliffs\", and it's an apt description for much of the park, which features craggy Andean peaks, just west of the city of Cali, and the western slopes of the Andes as they drop away and yield to the lowland plains of the Pacific coast. The transitional area between mountain peaks and lowlands is called the \"paramo\", and it features valleys with rushing rivers, and short grassy plateaus amid sheer cliffs. \nRivers flow down both sides of the mountain range with at least 7 rivers flowing into the Caucau River to the east. On the west side, rivers flow into the Pacific Ocean. The Dagua and Anchicay\u00e1 are the major rivers on the west side of the park. The Anchicaya is especially noted for the large numbers of amphibians and birds that live in its river basin.\nThere are several mountain peaks within the park. The highest is 4,080 meters. The lowest elevation in the park is 200 meters.\nFlora and fauna.\nThe park is famous for its biodiversity, especially its birds, with more than 300 species nesting and feeding in its jungles and rocky hideaways. Birds that can be found in the park include the multicolored tanager, crested ant-tanager, chestnut wood-quail and Andean cock-of-the-rock.\nThe park is home to dozens of species of mammals including cats, large and small, like the jaguar and the tigrillo, as well as spectacled bears, and a charismatic little guy called the \"olinguito\" which was only identified as a species in 2013. Perhaps most famous are the park's large colonies of monkeys. At least five species of monkey lurk in the jungle tree tops. Some are elusive while some, like the howler monkeys, will loudly make their presence known.\nGet in.\nForeign visitors will find numerous commercial flights available to Cali's \"Alfonso Bonilla Arag\u00f3n International Airport\" (). \nThe park entrance is a short 30-minute drive from downtown Cali. A taxi will take about 50 minutes and cost about US$15.\nSeveral buses depart from the Cali bus terminal to various park entry points, depending on what you plan to see and do. The most popular general interest entry point is the Recreation Center. From the bus terminal, ask a local for the bus to \"Pance de empresa Recreativos\".\nFees and permits.\nAdmission fees depend on your age and nationality.\nNote that park visitors are required to attend a briefing on park rules and safety advisories.\nReservations are required for visitors who plan to visit the Anchicay\u00e1 Canyon or Cerro Pico de Loro. Visit Farallones Ecotourism page[dead link].\nGet around.\nHike.\nSleep.\nCamping.\nCamping is allowed in several areas of the park.\nBackcountry.\nSome of the hiking trails to mountain peaks can take more than a day to hike up and back. Plan for a backcountry camping night to hike these routes. A local guide may be advisable. Local guides can be recommended through the park ranger station."}
{"id": "212988", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=212988", "title": "Roman Catholic Church", "text": "The Roman Catholic Church is the largest denomination of Christianity, with around 1.3 billion adherents.\nUnderstand.\nThe Catholic Church defines its faith through the Nicene Creed. It claims that it is the \"one, holy, catholic and Apostolic Church\" founded by Jesus Christ in his Great Commission, that the Pope is the successor of Saint Peter, and that its bishops are successors of Christ's apostles.\nChristianity spread within the late Roman Empire and became the Empire's official religion, and as the Western Roman Empire collapsed in the 5th century AD, the Church remained as a unifying factor for Latin-speaking Europe, in some degree up to the 21st century.\nThe Great Schism of the 11th century separated Catholicism from Orthodox Christianity.\nThe Protestant Reformation that started in the 15th century led to several Protestant denominations breaking with the Pope.\nThe Roman Catholic Church has spread overseas mainly through colonization; in particular the Spanish Empire, the Portuguese Empire and the French Colonial Empire contained territories whose inhabitants are mostly at least nominally Catholic today.\nThe Catholic Church has seven sacraments: that is, sacred liturgical acts held to be instituted by Jesus Christ. Holy Communion is the foremost of these; they also include baptism, confirmation, absolution, matrimony, holy orders, and the anointing of the sick. While most Protestant and Orthodox denominations also carry out these acts, only Communion and baptism tend to have status as sacraments.\nPilgrimage.\nSince ancient times, pilgrimage is a well-known Catholic rite. The Via Maria and Camino de Santiago are famous routes."}
{"id": "213002", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213002", "title": "Reisa National Park", "text": "Reisa National Park (Norwegian: \"Reisa nasjonalpark\") is a protected area in Nordreisa municipality in Troms county, Norway. The park covers the upper parts of the Reisadalen valley and protects a vast and pristine mountain and river landscape. It is known for its dramatic canyons, spectacular waterfalls, and the rich biodiversity of its boreal forests and mountain plateaus.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nReisa National Park was established in 1986 to protect the beautiful and varied landscape, and its unique flora and fauna. The area has a long history of use by the Sami and Kven people, and traces of their traditional life and culture can be found throughout the valley.\nLandscape.\nThe park is dominated by the Reisa River, which has carved a deep, forested canyon through the landscape. The valley is surrounded by high plateaus and mountains. Key features include the dramatic waterfalls, such as Mollisfossen, and the narrow, winding river.\nFlora and fauna.\nThe park is a meeting point for different botanical zones, with rich pine forests in the valley and more sparse mountain vegetation higher up. It is home to diverse wildlife, including Eurasian elk, wolverine, and lynx. Birdwatching is popular, with golden eagles and gyrfalcons nesting in the cliffs. The Reisa River is also an important habitat for salmon and Arctic char.\nClimate.\nThe climate is subarctic, with long, cold winters and short, cool summers. The best time to visit for hiking is from July to August, when the snow has melted and the weather is most stable.\nGet in.\nThe main access point to the park is the village of Storslett, in Nordreisa municipality.\nBy car.\nThe most flexible way to get to the park is by car. From Storslett, follow route 865 to the village of Saraelv, which is the main starting point for trips into the valley. Note that the final section is a gravel road.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest airports are in Troms\u00f8 (') and Alta ('). From there, you can rent a car or take a bus to Storslett. A smaller local airport, S\u00f8rkjosen (), has daily connections to Troms\u00f8 and Alta.\nBy public transit.\nBus service is available from Troms\u00f8 and Alta to Storslett. From Storslett, local buses may operate to points closer to the park entrance, but schedules can be limited.\nFees and permits.\nThere are no entrance fees for Reisa National Park.\nPermits: Fishing and hunting require a valid permit. Special permits may be needed for certain activities.\nGet around.\nHiking: The park has marked trails, including a section of the long-distance Arctic Trail (Nordkalottruta). Be prepared for challenging terrain.\nRiverboat: A traditional way to experience the valley is by hiring a local riverboat guide. This is a popular option for visiting waterfalls like Mollisfossen, as it can be difficult to reach on foot.\nPaddling: The Reisa River is well-suited for packrafting or canoeing.\nSee.\nMollisfossen: A stunning, 269-m waterfall with a 140-m vertical drop. It is one of the park's most famous sights and is often visited by riverboat.\nImofossen: A pair of waterfalls where the Reisa River plunges into a narrow, dramatic canyon.\nRock art: The park contains ancient Sami rock carvings at the Sieimma site, an important cultural and historical landmark.\nDo.\nHiking: Explore the network of trails, from short day hikes around the Ovi Raishiin visitor point to multi-day treks on the Arctic Trail. There is a visitor center available near Halti:\nFishing: The Reisa River is a well-known salmon river, though fishing regulations and permits are strict.\nKayaking and canoeing: Paddle the tranquil sections of the Reisa River for a unique perspective of the valley.\nWinter activities: In winter, the park is accessible for cross-country skiing and dog sledding.\nBuy.\nThe nearest towns for supplies are Storslett and S\u00f8rkjosen, where you can find grocery stores, sports equipment, and lodging.\nEat.\nThere are no restaurants or services within the national park itself. Visitors must bring their own food and drinks. The closest options are in Storslett, where you can find grocery stores and a few local eateries.\nSleep.\nCamping: You are allowed to camp almost anywhere in the park, as long as you follow the principles of leave no trace.\nCabins: There are several open and reservable cabins (hytter) maintained by the Norwegian Trekking Association (DNT) and the national park authorities.\nNearby lodging: Hotels and guesthouses are available in Storslett and other towns in the region.\nStay safe.\nBe prepared: The weather can change quickly, even in summer. Bring warm, waterproof clothing, food, and a map and compass. Mobile phone reception is unreliable.\nWildlife: Be aware of wildlife, including elk and wolverines.\nRiver crossings: The Reisa River can be fast-flowing and dangerous, especially during spring and early summer. Exercise extreme caution when crossing."}
{"id": "213013", "revid": "2206172", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213013", "title": "Wulong", "text": "Wulong is a district in Chongqing Municipality outside the central urban area. Wulong is famous of its limestone karst structures, several of which form part of the South China Karst World Heritage Site. \nGet around.\nThe Fairy Mountain Star Tourist Bus (\u4ed9\u5973\u5c71\u661f\u65c5\u6e38\u4e13\u7ebf) runs between Wulong South Railway Station and the Fairy Mountain National Forest Park with intermediate stops in the Wulong urban area and the Fairy Mountain National Tourist Resort. The first bus leaves at 7:00 and the last bus leaves at 20:00. The full fare is \u00a510."}
{"id": "213022", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213022", "title": "Menongue", "text": "Menongue, formerly Serpa Pinto, is a municipality and the capital of Cubango Province in Angola. The municipality had a population of 320,914 in 2014. It is one of the four municipalities in Angola whose inhabitants are predominantly Mbunda.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nBy train.\nMenongue is linked to Lubango by a daily train (except Sunday). It starts at 07:00. The cost in \"Special\" is 8.500 Kz (September 2025). The journey is about 12 hours.\nFrom Namibia.\nBy bush taxi.\nIf you come from Namibia and cross the border at Katwitwi, you will first need to take a moto-taxi from the border to Hotel Katuitui, which is about 7 km away (1000 Kwanza, September 2025). From there, you can catch a bush taxi to Menongue (18.000 Kwanza, September 2025). Bush taxis are limited, with at least one departing daily in the evening between 17:00 and 19:00 (but can be full. At least you can sleep at Hotel Katuitui). The journey is quite long and challenging due to poor road conditions. Only 4WD vehicles can manage the route, which takes around 9 hours: approximately 7 hours on a very rough bush track followed by 2 hours on a paved road. For this reason, crossing at Santa-Clara is usually faster and simpler. There is an ATM at the border that takes both International Visa and Mastercards. You can also change Namibian dollars around Hotel Katuitui on black market (ask local people).\nGet around.\nThe city center is quiet compact. You can easily walk."}
{"id": "213033", "revid": "2440688", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213033", "title": "Anseong", "text": "Anseong (Korean: \uc548\uc131; Korean pronunciation: [an.s\u028c\u014b]) is a city in Gyeonggi-do Province, South Korea, 80 km south of Seoul. Anseong promotes itself as \"The City of Masters\". It is known for producing brassware and arts and crafts. The agriculture of Anseong is centered around Asian pears, grapes, ginseng, and rice, while Hanwoo (Korean cow) is considered to be the most famous and high-quality.\nUnderstand.\nAnseong is best known as a center of traditional Korean performing arts, particularly 'Namsadang nori', a form of itinerant folk performance that once traveled the country. The city hosts the annual 'Anseong Baudeogi Festival', named after Baudeogi, remembered as Korea\u2019s first female clown and a symbol of the free-spirited performers who once gathered here. During the festival season, Anseong briefly transforms from a quiet provincial city into a lively stage for traditional music, dance, and street performances.\nAs part of Korea\u2019s \u201csemiconductor belt\u201d in southern Gyeonggi, Anseong is also developing new industrial complexes for logistics, electronics, and high-tech manufacturing.\nThe name \u201cAnseong matchum\u201d (literally \u201cperfectly made in Anseong\u201d) originates from the city\u2019s reputation for high-quality Anseong yugi, traditional Korean brassware. Over time, the expression entered everyday Korean language to mean something that fits perfectly, making Anseong one of the few cities whose name lives on as a common phrase. (The railway was closed in 1990, and it is to be revived in 2028\u20132032.)\nno population data for this page.\nModern history.\nIn 1963, Gosam town of Yongin was incorporated by Anseong, which was still a county at the time. In 1998, Anseong was upgraded from county to city status.\nLocated in the southernmost part of Gyeonggi Province and adjacent to Chungcheong Province, Anseong serves as a gateway between the Seoul metropolitan area and the rest of the country. For this reason, in the past, it was counted as one of three major commercial centers in Korea with Daegu and Jeonju. At that time, Anseong was a bustling city. In particular, the 5-day market held on the 2nd and 7th of every month was one of the three largest markets in Joseon.\nIn addition, Anseong served as a railroad transportation hub, and it has emerged as a transportation hub thanks to the expansion of the road network and the connection of highways and highways.\nClimate.\nAnseong has a temperate continental climate with four distinct seasons.\nThe city experiences hot and humid summers influenced by the East Asian monsoon and cold, dry winters due to the Siberian air mass.\nSpring (March to May) is mild but can bring sudden temperature changes and yellow dust from the deserts of China.\nSummer (June to August) is typically hot, with average daytime temperatures often exceeding 30 \u00b0C (86 \u00b0F) and heavy rainfall during the monsoon period in July.\nAutumn (September to November) is the most pleasant season, featuring clear skies, crisp air, and vibrant foliage.\nWinter (December to February) is cold, with temperatures frequently dropping below \u22125 \u00b0C (23 \u00b0F) at night and occasional snowfall.\nBecause 'Anseong' is located inland on the southern edge of Gyeonggi-do Province, it tends to have greater daily temperature differences and less humidity than coastal cities.\nOverall, its climate represents a typical inland Korean weather pattern\u2014distinct seasonal shifts, humid summers, and cold, dry winters.\nThe wind is an area affected by the seasonal wind like all of Korea. The seasonal wind in winter is mainly the northwest seasonal wind that blows from the continental side, which is characterized by being very cold and dry, bringing clear weather, while accompanied by severe cold.\nGet in.\nAnseong is in the southern part of Gyeonggi-do, between Seoul and the Chungcheong area, so it has good access to transportation. It is about 80 km from Seoul (70-80 min) and 40 km from Suwon (approx. 50 min), and can be easily reached by car, express bus, and intercity bus.\nBy train.\nThe nearest KTX station is Cheonan Asan Station or Pyeongtaek Station.\nIt takes about 30 to 50 minutes by bus or taxi from each station to Anseong.\nBy car.\nAnseong is a major transportation hub in southern Gyeonggi, connected to numerous highways. It is connected to all highways in the country, and there are nine interchanges, the most in South Korea, connecting the Gyeongbu Expressway, Jungbu Expressway, and Pyeongtaek\u2013Jecheon Expressway.\nGyeongbu Expressway: Enter Anseong IC or Anseong Rest Area (Smart Toll).\nJungbu Expressway: accessible Iljuk IC or Hobub JC.\nIt takes about an hour and a half by car from Seoul and about an hour from Daejeon.\nGet around.\nAnseong City is relatively small, and the main tourist attraction is about 10 to 20 minutes by car from the main street. Public transportation has city buses, but the interval between buses can be long, so it is convenient to use private cars or taxis for efficient movement.\nCity buses and taxis in Anseong accept 'T-money' and 'Cashbee' transportation cards, which can be purchased and topped up at convenience stores. Cash payment is also accepted, though having small bills is recommended.\nOn foot.\nWhile central Anseong is walkable in short stretches, most attractions are spread out and not well connected by pedestrian-friendly routes. Travelers planning to visit multiple sites in one day will find walking impractical.\nBy bus.\nThere is a city bus route that connects Anseong city and the outside area. You can check the real-time bus location and timetable through this site.\nThe city buses also have good routes to Pyeongtaek, so there is no difficulty in using SRT, general railroads, and wide-area trains at Pyeongtaek Station and Pyeongtaek Jije Station.\nBus stop announcements and onboard displays are primarily in Korean. Using a navigation app such as 'Naver Map' or 'KakaoMap' is strongly recommended, as Google Maps provides limited bus information in the area.\nCity buses connect most neighborhoods and nearby cities, but services can be infrequent, especially outside peak hours and in the evenings.\nBy taxi.\nYou can call a taxi by referring to the phone numbers below.\nTaxis use meters and do not require negotiation. Credit cards and transportation cards are widely accepted. Showing the destination name in Korean, either written or on a smartphone screen, makes communication much easier.\nBy bike.\nAnseong is relatively flat terrain and rural character make cycling a feasible way to explore areas outside the compact city center. While dedicated bike lanes are limited, riding along Anseongcheon provides a calm route connecting residential districts with parks and open farmland.\nIn the outskirts, quiet agricultural roads around Anseong Farmland and the surrounding countryside allow for longer, scenic rides with minimal traffic, though riders should be aware that these roads are shared with farm vehicles.\nBy hiking.\nHiking in Anseong is generally low-intensity and suitable for casual walkers rather than experienced mountaineers. Chiljangsan, home to Chiljangsa Temple, is the most commonly referenced hiking destination in the area. The mountain features well-maintained trails with gentle elevation gain, allowing visitors to combine a short hike with a cultural visit to the temple.\nOther walking options include landscaped paths and forested trails around Anseong Matchum Land and nearby reservoirs, which function more as extended walking routes than traditional hikes.\nDo.\nFestivals.\nThere are several regular festivals in Anseong.\nEat.\nRegional products.\nRice, pears and grapes grown in this region are particularly tasty, and the pears and grapes are particularly sweet. Anseong is also renowned for its premium Hanwoo(Korean cow), appreciated for its rich flavor and tenderness. The fertile farmland supports the production of ginseng, mushrooms, and quality soybeans, and the city has a long tradition of dairy farming, offering fresh milk and locally made cheese. Seasonal fruits and farm products can be enjoyed at local markets or through farm-visit experiences.\nLearn.\nThere are several universities in Anseong."}
{"id": "213034", "revid": "113846", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213034", "title": "Hadong", "text": "Hadong (\ud558\ub3d9) is a county in South Gyeongsang Province, South Korea, well known for its scenic landscapes and traditional tea culture. Nestled between the Seomjin River and the Jirisan Mountains, Hadong offers a slower pace of life compared to the bustling cities, with picturesque villages, lush green tea fields, and historic temples. The area is especially famous for its Hadong green tea, considered one of the oldest and most prestigious in Korea, with a history dating back over a thousand years. Visitors can enjoy hiking in Jirisan National Park, strolling along the Seomjin River, exploring traditional markets, or experiencing Korea\u2019s tea heritage through tea festivals and hands-on tea-making programs.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nHadong has long been at the heart of Korea\u2019s tea culture. Historical records say that tea seeds were first brought from China and planted here during the Silla Dynasty in the 9th century, beginning a tradition that has continued for more than a millennium. The area also preserves strong Confucian and Buddhist traditions, with historic temples and pavilions scattered across its hills and valleys. Despite gradual modernization, Hadong still keeps much of its traditional charm, offering visitors a glimpse of Korea\u2019s serene rural past. Its tea fields, ancient villages, and riverside markets together reflect a slower, harmonious rhythm of life that continues to define the region today.\nOrientation.\nHadong is located in the southwestern part of South Gyeongsang Province. The Seomjin River forms its western boundary, while the eastern side rises into the rugged Jirisan National Park. The county is made up of small towns and villages surrounded by tea fields, rice paddies, and forested hills. Hadong-eup is the county seat and main town, serving as the base for exploring the surrounding attractions. \nClimate.\nHadong enjoys a temperate climate with four distinct seasons. Spring and autumn are especially pleasant, bringing fresh mountain air, blooming wildflowers, and brilliant fall colors along the Seomjin River. Summers are hot and humid, influenced by the East Asian monsoon, while winters are cold but relatively mild compared to northern Korea. The tea fields reach their most vibrant green from late spring to early summer, making this the most scenic time to visit.\nGet in.\nHadong is a rural county in South Gyeongsang Province and is most easily reached by intercity bus or by car. There is no high-speed rail service, but regional trains and frequent buses connect Hadong with nearby cities such as Jinju, Suncheon, and Busan. The main arrival points are Hadong Bus Terminal, Hadong Station on the Gyeongjeon Line, and the Hadong interchange on the Namhae Expressway.\nIntercity and express buses run to Hadong Bus Terminal from major cities such as Seoul (about 4 hours), Busan (about 2 hours 30 minutes), Daegu, and Jinju. The terminal is located in the downtown area within walking distance of most accommodations.\nFrom Busan, the easiest way to reach Hadong is by intercity bus, with direct services departing from Busan Central Bus Terminal and taking about 2\u00bd hours. An alternative is to travel by train from Busan to Jinju, then transfer to a regional train on the Gyeongjeon Line to Hadong Station.\nFrom Gwangju, travelers can reach Hadong by intercity bus via Gurye or Suncheon, with total travel times typically around 2\u20132\u00bd hours. Another option is to take a train from Gwangju to Suncheon and transfer to a regional train on the Gyeongjeon Line to Hadong.\nHadong Station is on the Gyeongjeon Line, with regional trains running from Jinju (about 1 hour) and Suncheon (about 50 minutes). From the station, local buses or taxis can take you to Hadong-eup and nearby attractions.\nHadong is accessible via the Namhae Expressway (Route 10). Take the Hadong IC exit and continue on local roads to reach the town center. National Route 19 also passes through Hadong, connecting it with Namhae, Gurye, and the Seomjin River valley.\nThe Seomjin River Bike Path provides a scenic cycling approach to Hadong from nearby areas such as Gurye and Suncheon. The route is mostly flat and paved and is best suited for travelers already in the region rather than long-distance arrivals.\nDo.\nHadong offers a wide range of outdoor and cultural activities year-round \u2014 from scenic rail bike rides and thrilling ziplines to vibrant local festivals.\nFestivals.\nThroughout the year, Hadong celebrates its tea heritage, river culture, and rural beauty with lively local festivals. From tea ceremonies in spring to flower fields in autumn, each season offers something festive and distinctly Hadong.\nCope.\nHadong is a small rural county, and most practical services are concentrated in Hadong-eup, the county seat. Travelers will find basic public services, police assistance, and administrative offices within walking distance of the bus terminal. English is not widely spoken outside tourist facilities, so having addresses written in Korean can be helpful when seeking assistance.\nPublic services.\nThe main administrative office of Hadong County, located in Hadong-eup. Visitors can ask for local information, maps, and assistance with general administrative matters.\nThe principal law enforcement office for the region. In emergencies, dial 112 for police assistance."}
{"id": "213059", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213059", "title": "Serra de Montsant Natural Park", "text": "Serra de Montsant Natural Park is in Tarragona province. The unique landscape of this park, characterized by the footprint of human activity and a steep orography of cliffs, ravines and gorges, offers us a rich natural and cultural heritage linked to the religious history in the park. Montsant can be translated from Catalan as \"Holy Mountain\". There are over 2000 Rock climbing routes in the park and pre-park areas.\nUnderstand.\nThe park is around 10 km to the west of Poblet Natural Park\nTourist office.\nSouthEast\nEast\nSouth\nHistory.\nThe Parliament of Catalonia approved its declaration as a natural park in 2002 with the aim of protecting its geological, biological, landscape and cultural values, respecting the sustainable development of its uses. The peak of Roca Corbatera at 1,163 metres is the highest point in the park.\nLandscape.\nA diverse and rich landscape, born from a complex orography, the confluence of several climatic regions and traditional agricultural uses. On the southern slope, the imposing rocky walls of the Cingle Major stand out powerfully. Above these, the mountain continues to ascend gently until reaching the Serra Major.\nThe Serra Major is a rocky area with little or no vegetation. It is accessed via the graus (most accesses to the Serra Major are made through what are known as graus, since there are several places where you have to overcome the difference in altitude by going over a step or a difficult step).\nOn the northern slope there is an area of wild forests and deep ravines, which extends from the Serra Major towards the north. The western Montsant is a sector of deep ravines and scrub with pine forests that descend from the cliffs towards the Montsant river. The valleys of the Montsant and Siurana rivers are an area of high ecological and aesthetic value. A unique and beautiful landscape.\nClimate.\nThe park, located in the northeast Iberian Peninsula, experiences a Mediterranean climate marked by dry summers and mild winters with occasional cold snaps. \nIts complex terrain and varied elevations foster diverse microclimates, where summer drought dominates and winter freezes occur, especially in valleys due to thermal inversion. \nTemperatures peak at 40\u00b0C on western slopes in summer, while winter lows are notable for frequent frosts. Precipitation is uneven, with most rainfall in spring and autumn, and pronounced dry periods in summer and winter; annual rainfall averages 450\u2013600 mm. Snowfall is occasional, and fog can be persistent.\nFlora and fauna.\nThe Serra de Montsant Natural Park hosts a remarkable diversity of flora and fauna, shaped by its Mediterranean climate with sub-Mediterranean and eurosiberian influences. Its vegetation is dominated by holm oak and kermes oak forests, while small patches of oak groves and extensive pinewoods (Aleppo, Scots, and black pine) mark different elevations. Dense scrublands of rosemary, rockrose, and thyme, as well as juniper and boxwood communities, thrive on drier slopes and windy ridges. Humid valleys and ravines sustain riparian woodlands of poplars, willows, and ashes, together with lush undergrowth of ivy, bramble, and horsetail. Rocky cliffs and screes harbour specialised casmophytic vegetation, including numerous rare and endemic plants adapted to fissures and limestone outcrops. \nThe park\u2019s fauna is equally rich, with one of the most diverse assemblages in Catalonia. Cliffs serve as nesting grounds for golden eagles, Bonelli\u2019s eagles, peregrine falcons, and eagle owls, alongside swifts, swallows, and rock thrushes. Mammals such as wild boar, badger, genet, wildcat, and red fox occupy the forests and scrublands, while occasional Iberian ibexes roam the rocky areas. Amphibians like the salamander and common toad, reptiles including Montpellier snake and ocellated lizard, and endemic invertebrates in caves highlight the ecological variety. Rivers and wetlands shelter water voles, kingfishers, aquatic snakes, native fish, and the increasingly rare white-clawed crayfish. Agricultural landscapes further enrich this mosaic, attracting species adapted to human-altered habitats.\nGet around.\nThe park is surrounded by a loop of local roads:\nAmong the tracks that enters the park:\nSee.\nNice places around the park.\nSouth East\nSouth west\nWest\nNorth East\nDo.\nClimbing.\nThe Montsant mountain range and Natural Park encompass a vast region that extends far beyond the specific climbing zones popular with rock climbers. While Margalef's renowned crags lie within Montsant's boundaries, the \"Montsant climbing area\" conventionally describes the dramatic limestone escarpments tracing the range's primary ridgeline. This awe-inspiring stretch, beginning near Albarca, unfurls nearly 20 km westward-southwest, offering world-class sport climbing amid rugged peaks and sweeping vistas.\nRoutes here mirror Margalef's signature style: powerful, pocket-dominated walls interspersed with cobblestone features that demand creative crimping and dynamic movement. This creates a thrilling counterpoint to the technical, finger-intensive edging challenges of Siurana across the valley. Montsant's exposed ridges deliver sustained, pumpy lines across grades 6b-8c+, ideal for intermediate-to-expert climbers seeking adventure in Catalonia's premier limestone playground. Whether chasing sun-drenched afternoons or shaded morning sessions, the area's length ensures endless variety year-round.\nClimbing is only allowed in designated areas. Check park website: Climbing areas\nVia Ferrata.\nThere is a via ferrata located around the town of La Morera de Montsant, where you can enjoy a safe adventure in the middle of nature.\nTechnical characteristics\n*Difficulty: medium (k3)\n*Access time from the parking lot: 45 min\n*Equipped section time: 1 hr\n*Return time to the parking lot: 1 hr\n*Total elevation gain: 250 m\n*Bridges: 2 bridges of 4 and 15 m\n*Escape routes: there are no escape routes during the equipped itinerary.\n*Others: route can be secured with a rope system.\n*Minimum mandatory approved equipment: helmet, harness and anchor kit with dissipator for via ferratas.\nEat.\nLa Morera de Montsant:\nUlldemolins:\nLa Vilella Alta:\nDrink.\nCornudella de Montsant:\nCabac\u00e9s:\nMargalef:\nSleep.\nUlldemolins:\nCornudella de Montsant:\nLa Morera de Montsant:\nEscaladei:"}
{"id": "213067", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213067", "title": "Kochi/Goshree", "text": "The Goshree Islands are the islands north of Old Kochi, including Bolgatty, Vallarpadom and Vypin. Vypin is the outermost of the three islands. Vypin Island is one of the most densely populated islands in the world with numerous fishing villages, tourist villages and the popular Cherai Beach."}
{"id": "213068", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213068", "title": "Kochi/Thrippunithura", "text": "Thrippunithura, the southern suburb of Kochi and erstwhile capital of the Kingdom of Cochn, is a major arts centre, famous for its historical palaces, museums and temples."}
{"id": "213069", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213069", "title": "Kochi/Kalamassery", "text": "Kalamassery (\u0d15\u0d33\u0d2e\u0d36\u0d4d\u0d36\u0d47\u0d30\u0d3f \"Ka\u1e37amashsh\u0113ri\") is the eastern suburb of Kochi, famous for the large Vamana Temple, the focal centre of Onam, the national festival of Kerala. Also the home to Cochin University and the city's IT hub Infopark."}
{"id": "213095", "revid": "2327406", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213095", "title": "Lincolnshire (Illinois)", "text": "Lincolnshire is a village in the Northwest Suburbs region of Illinois.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nLincolnshire is situated along I-94, as well as Illinois Route 22, US Route 41, and Illinois Route 21."}
{"id": "213100", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213100", "title": "Ichikikushikino", "text": "Ichikikushikino (\u3044\u3061\u304d\u4e32\u6728\u91ce) is a city of 25,579 people (2025) in Kagoshima prefecture. The city is also renowned nationwide as the \"Town of Tuna,\" boasting the largest fleet of deep-sea tuna vessels in Japan. For travelers, this translates into an opportunity to eat exceptionally fresh, high-quality tuna at local restaurants, such as the famous Maguro no Yakata (Tuna Hall).\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nThe history and identity of Ichikikushikino are best understood through its three main pillars: gold, tuna, and its pivotal role in Japan's modernization.\nFor centuries, this area was one of Japan's leading gold-mining towns. While the original Kushikino Mine closed in 1966, its history is now a major tourist attraction. The old mine tunnels have been repurposed by the Satsuma Kinzangura, a unique distillery where visitors can take a tram deep into the cool, dark shafts to see where \"shochu\" (a Japanese spirit) is aged.\nHistorically, the city's most significant event occurred in 1865 at Hashima Port. This was the secret departure point for the \"Satsuma Students\", 19 young men sent to Europe in defiance of national policy. They went on to become key leaders in Japan's modernization, and this pivotal story is now told at the Satsuma Students Museum located right at the port.\nWhile the area was historically ruled by various samurai clans and the modern city was formed by a merger in 2005, it is these tangible legacies of industry and modernization that define the travel experience today.\nClimate.\nIchikikushino has a mild, subtropical climate due to the warm Kuroshio Current, which keeps winters exceptionally mild with temperatures rarely dropping below 5\u00b0C (41\u00b0F). However, travelers must be aware of the distinct rainy season in June, which is extremely intense; the city often receives record-breaking downpours of over 500 mm (20 in) in this single month alone. This is immediately followed by a very hot and humid midsummer (July-August), with temperatures regularly exceeding 32\u00b0C (90\u00b0F). Furthermore, from late summer into autumn (approx. August-September), the city lies directly on the main path for typhoons and can be affected by strong storms.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThere is no airport in Ichikikushikino. The nearest airport is Kagoshima Airport, and you can take the airport bus from Kagoshima Airport to Satsumasendai Station and take the subway.\nBy train.\nThe Kagoshima Main Line passes through Ichiki Station, Kushikino Station, and Kamimura-Kakuenmae Station.\nBy car.\nAs for the highway, the Minami-Kyushu Western Circulation Expressway passes, and there are Kushikino IC and Ichiki IC in the city. The Minami-Kyushu Western Circulation Expressway runs from Kumamoto to Kagoshima, and the Kyushu Expressway runs to Kitakyushu.\nNational road 3 and 270 pass.\nBy bus.\nThe express bus connecting Kagoshima-shi and Satsumasendai passes through. The stop is as follows.\nGet around.\nIchikikushiki is small enough to travel by bus or train. The fare for the city bus is \u00a5200 yen for adults. Taxis are waiting in front of Kushikino and Ichiki stations. However, public transportation does not cover all of Ichikikushikino, so it is most convenient to travel by car in areas outside the city.\nConnect.\nInternet.\nThe 'Japan Wi-Fi auto connect' application allows you to automatically access free Wi-Fi across Japan.\nMobile.\nMost Japanese SIMs include the option of Internet-only, so it is common to buy on an Internet site. To make a phone call, it is better to make a phone call by roaming. There are many mountains around, so there are cases where the connection is disconnected when you go to the mountain area.\nPostal Service.\nThere is a post office around Kushiki no Station, and the postcode is \u3012 896-XXXX."}
{"id": "213103", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213103", "title": "Busan/Gadeok Island", "text": "Busan/Gadeok Island (\uac00\ub355\ub3c4) is a large island located in the far west of Busan, South Korea. Known for its beautiful, rugged scenery and scenic hiking trails, the island is also home to a number of historical sites, including a famous lighthouse and old fortifications.\nUnderstand.\nWhile it is known for its scenic hiking trails and natural beauty, the island's future is a major topic of discussion as it is the planned site for a new airport that will serve as a key transportation hub for the entire region.\nGet in.\nGadeok Island is well-connected to both Busan and Geoje, making it accessible by car and public transportation.\nBy bus.\nSeveral public bus routes from Busan's Gangseo-gu district connect to Gadeok Island, offering an affordable way to travel. The buses run from early morning to late evening, connecting key areas of the island.\nBy ferry.\nSmall ferries operate from Daehang-dong on the island, primarily serving as a connection to nearby islands. They are generally not used for direct access from the mainland.\nSee.\nGadeok Island offers a number of scenic and historical sites, perfect for hiking and exploration.\nEat.\nGadeok Island is a great place to enjoy fresh seafood. Many local restaurants specialize in dishes made with ingredients from the surrounding waters."}
{"id": "213105", "revid": "2440922", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213105", "title": "Yeoncheon", "text": "Yeoncheon (\uc5f0\ucc9c) is a county in northern Gyeonggi Province, South Korea, bordering North Korea along the Demilitarized Zone (DMZ). \nThe county is surrounded by other notable areas, including Paju to the south, Cheorwon to the north, and Dongducheon to the east. Known for its rich prehistoric heritage, including the famous Jeongok-ri Paleolithic site, Yeoncheon combines tranquil rural landscapes with important historical and cultural attractions. \nThe area is accessible via national highways and the Gyeongwon railway line, offering visitors a blend of natural beauty and a glimpse into Korea\u2019s border history.\nUnderstand.\nYeoncheon is well-known for its significant prehistoric heritage\u2014the Jeongok-ri Paleolithic site is considered a landmark of early human culture in East Asia and hosts the annual Yeoncheon Paleolithic Festival.\nYeoncheon is defined by uniquely scenic landscapes, from the Hantangang and Imjingang rivers to striking columnar joints and waterfalls created by ancient volcanic activity. The area\u2019s quiet towns, rural villages, and easy access to the outdoors make it a hidden gem for travelers interested in history, nature, and Korea\u2019s borderlands. \nDespite its remote atmosphere, Yeoncheon is reachable via the Gyeongwon railway line and major national highways, and offers visitors a chance to experience both cultural attractions and tranquil rural life. \nHistory.\nYeoncheon has a history of shifting between ancient kingdoms, known as Gongseong-hyeon under Silla and later as Yeoncheon from 1309 during the Goryeo Dynasty. Due to its location near the 38th parallel, the region experienced fierce battles during the Korean War\u2014today, parts of Yeoncheon remain near the Demilitarized Zone, preserving traces of both its ancient and modern past. \nClimate.\nYeoncheon experiences a humid continental climate with four distinct seasons. Spring (March to May) is mild and dry, with temperatures rising gradually and flowers blooming across the countryside. Summers (June to August) are hot and humid; daytime highs often exceed 30\u00b0C and rainfall is frequent during the East Asian monsoon, with occasional thunderstorms. Autumn (September to November) is noted for clear skies, cool temperatures, and vibrant foliage, making it one of the best times to visit. Winters (December to February) are cold and dry, with temperatures often dropping below \u201310\u00b0C and regular snowfall. The climate mirrors much of northern Gyeonggi Province but can be slightly colder due to Yeoncheon\u2019s proximity to the border and its rural landscape.\nGet in.\nBy train.\nYeoncheon is served by the Gyeongui\u2013Jungang Line, with trains to and from central Seoul (Yongsan Station) and Uijeongbu. The journey from Seoul takes about 2 hours. From Yeoncheon Station, local buses and taxis are available to reach destinations within the county, but some rural attractions may require a car or additional transfers.\nBy bus.\nIntercity buses run several times daily between Seoul\u2019s Dong Seoul and Sangbong Bus Terminals and Yeoncheon, with travel times around 2 hours. Buses also connect from nearby cities such as Uijeongbu, Paju, and Pocheon.\nBy car.\nYeoncheon can be accessed by car via National Route 3 or the Seoul\u2013Yangyang Expressway. The drive from central Seoul takes approximately 1.5\u20132 hours depending on traffic. Most major attractions offer parking, but rural roads can be narrow.\nGet around.\nNeither Yeoncheon Station nor Yeoncheon Bus Terminal is within walking distance of most major sights. A taxi or local bus is usually the best way to reach destinations throughout the county. \nBy Bus.\nSeveral local bus routes operate within Yeoncheon County, connecting transport hubs such as Yeoncheon Station, the bus terminal, and key locations including county offices and tourist sites. Buses to smaller villages run less frequently, so check schedules in advance. As Yeoncheon is quite rural, cycling is possible for short trips, but public transport or taxi is more convenient for sightseeing. \nBy taxi.\nTaxis are a practical choice for getting around Yeoncheon, especially for visiting rural areas or historic sites like Jeongok-ri Prehistoric Museum or Horogoru Fortress. The basic fare is typically \u20a94,000 for the first 2 km, with extra charges for travel outside the main urban area or late at night (22:00\u201304:00). It is recommended to arrange return trips in advance if traveling to remote spots. \nConnect.\nInternet and Wi-Fi.\nPublic Wi-Fi.\nFree public Wi-Fi is available in some public facilities such as community centers, libraries, and bus terminals in Yeoncheon. Networks may appear under names operated by Gyeonggi-do or local government services. Coverage can be uneven in rural areas, so do not rely on it everywhere.\nCafes and Restaurants.\nMany cafes and some restaurants in Jeongok-eup and Yeoncheon-eup provide Wi-Fi for customers. Small, independent places may not always display the password, but you can usually ask the staff or check the receipt.\nMobile phones and SIM cards.\nConvenience Stores.\nYeoncheon has major convenience store chains (CU, GS25, 7-Eleven), especially around Jeongok-eup and near bus terminals. Some branches sell prepaid SIM cards, but availability is less consistent than in large cities, so it is easier to purchase and activate a SIM in Seoul before coming. "}
{"id": "213107", "revid": "2183461", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213107", "title": "Yeongdong County", "text": "Yeongdong-gun (\uc601\ub3d9\uad70) is a rural county in southern North Chungcheong, South Korea. \nKnown for its vineyards, wine-themed attractions, and traditional music festivals, Yeongdong makes a relaxed stop between the mountains of central Korea and the Geum River basin.\nDistricts.\nYeongdong County is divided into one town (eup) and ten townships (myeon). These subdivisions are mostly rural, with vineyards, mountains, and traditional villages.\nUnderstand.\nYeongdong County, located in southern Chungcheongbuk-do, is best known for its vineyards and locally produced wines. The area brands itself as the \"home of grapes\" in Korea, hosting an annual Grape &amp; Wine Festival each September. The county seat, Yeongdong-eup, is a quiet market town surrounded by valleys and low mountains. Traditional music also plays a key role in local identity, with Yeongdong often referred to as the birthplace of Korean gugak (traditional music).\nHistory.\nYeongdong\u2019s history is defined by its role as a strategic borderland and a cultural crossroads. During the Three Kingdoms period, the area was a fiercely contested territory between Baekje and Silla due to its location in the heart of the peninsula. Originally known as Gitsu-hyeon under Silla, the region was officially named Yeongdong in 1018 during the Goryeo Dynasty, a name it has carried for over a millennium.\nThe county is most famously known as the birthplace of Park Yeon (Nangye), one of the three greatest musical geniuses of Korea and a key figure in the development of \"Gugak\" (traditional Korean music) during the early Joseon Dynasty. His legacy transformed Yeongdong into the spiritual center of Korean traditional music. In the modern era, the county faced a dark chapter during the Korean War with the No Gun Ri Massacre in 1950, a tragic event that is now commemorated by a memorial park dedicated to peace and human rights. Today, Yeongdong has successfully rebranded itself as the \"Vineyard of Korea,\" utilizing its unique climate to become the nation's leading producer of grapes and premium domestic wine.\nClimate.\nYeongdong features a typical inland continental climate, characterized by significant temperature fluctuations between day and night and across the four seasons. This large diurnal temperature range is the secret behind the exceptionally high sugar content and quality of the local fruits, particularly grapes and persimmons.\nTalk.\nStandard Korean is widely spoken and understood throughout Yeongdong County. \nLocals may also use the Chungcheong dialect, known for its slow and relaxed intonation. \nA few regional expressions, such as \"georyeo\" (\uadf8\ub824, meaning \"that's right\") or \"geosigi\" (\uac70\uc2dc\uae30, a vague word used in place of \"that thing\"), may be heard in daily conversation, but visitors will generally have no problem communicating in standard Korean.\nGet in.\nKTX/ITX and Mugunghwa services stop at Yeongdong Station; there are intercity buses from Cheongju, Daejeon and Seoul. National Route 4 and the Jungbu-naeryuk Expressway provide road access.\nBy train.\nThe train is the most convenient way to reach the county, as the Gyeongbu Line (the main railway artery connecting Seoul and Busan) runs directly through the heart of the town.\nBy bus.\nThe intercity bus terminal is located within walking distance of Yeongdong Station, providing easy transfers for regional travel.\nBy car.\nYeongdong is easily accessible via the Gyeongbu Expressway (Highway 1), which has two main exits within the county.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest airport is Cheongju International Airport (CJJ), located about 1 hour and 15 minutes away by car or train. However, for most international travelers, arriving at Incheon International Airport (ICN) and taking a KTX to Daejeon (then transferring to a local train) is the most efficient route.\nGet around.\nLocal buses connect the town with surrounding villages; taxis are easy to hail around the station. While the town center is easily walkable, reaching the far-flung vineyards, temples, and mountain valleys requires a bit of planning with local buses, taxis, or a rental car.\nBy bus.\nThe local bus system (often called the \"Nong-eo-chon\" bus) is the lifeline for budget travelers.\nBy car.\nA car is the most efficient way to explore the county, especially if you plan to visit multiple wineries or remote mountain passes.\nBy taxi.\nTaxis are plentiful near Yeongdong Station. \nBy bicycle.\nFor those who enjoy slow travel, the paths along the Yeongdong-cheon Stream and the Geumgang River offer peaceful scenery.\nSee.\nYeongdong offers a refined blend of cultural heritage and dramatic inland scenery, where traditional melodies harmonize with the aroma of aging wine.\nDo.\nFrom immersive cultural experiences to adrenaline-pumping drives through mountain passes, Yeongdong offers a variety of activities for every type of traveler.\nBuy.\nYeongdong is the agricultural heart of central Korea, famous for its high-sugar grapes, persimmons, and walnuts.\nEat.\nThe culinary highlight of Yeongdong is \"Olbaengi-guk\" (marsh snail soup), harvested from the clean waters of the Geumgang River.\nDrink.\nWhile wine is the primary draw, Yeongdong also offers scenic cafes that take advantage of its dramatic mountain and river views.\nSleep.\nYeongdong offers a mix of traditional cultural stays and scenic outdoor camping grounds.\nStay safe.\nYeongdong County is generally safe with very low crime rates. Visitors are unlikely to encounter any problems beyond the usual precautions needed anywhere in Korea. \nWeather, however, can present some hazards. Summers are hot and humid, with occasional heavy rains that can cause flash flooding in valleys. Winters can be icy, and rural roads may be slippery after snow. If hiking in the surrounding mountains such as Minjujisan, bring proper gear and be prepared for sudden weather changes.\nAs in most rural areas, medical facilities are limited outside the county seat, so consider bringing basic medication and allow extra travel time in case of emergencies."}
{"id": "213111", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213111", "title": "Okcheon County", "text": "Okcheon County (\uc625\ucc9c\uad70, \"Okcheon-gun\") is a rural county in North Chungcheong Province, South Korea, positioned on the vital corridor between Daejeon and Yeongdong. This peaceful inland destination offers stunning lakeside scenery along Daecheong Lake and the Geum River, while maintaining a strong cultural identity centered on renowned poet Jeong Ji-yong (\uc815\uc9c0\uc6a9) and the county's thriving nursery-tree industry.\nUnderstand.\nOkcheon County embodies the essence of slow travel in Korea's heartland. Here, visitors discover short scenic hikes to panoramic viewpoints, tranquil waterside walks, intimate museums showcasing local heritage, and authentic traditional markets. The county seat, \"Okcheon-eup\", serves as the practical base, while the most breathtaking natural attractions line the pristine shores of Daecheong Lake.\nThe county's character reflects a harmonious blend of literary heritage, agricultural tradition, and natural beauty. Unlike the bustling tourist destinations of Korea, Okcheon rewards those seeking genuine cultural immersion and unhurried exploration.\nHistory.\nOkcheon's modern administrative structure emerged during the early 20th century reorganization of Korea's local districts. Throughout history, the county has functioned as a crucial gateway between the Chungcheong interior and the Yeongnam region, positioned strategically along the Gyeongbu transport corridor that has connected Korea's heartland to its southeastern regions for centuries.\nThe area's cultural landscape was profoundly shaped by poet Jeong Ji-yong (1902-1950), one of Korea's most celebrated literary figures, who was born in Okcheon. His modernist poetry captured the essence of Korean nature and sensibility, and his legacy continues to define the county's cultural identity today.\nGeography and landscape.\nOkcheon County spans approximately 537 km\u00b2 of varied inland terrain. The dramatic transformation of the landscape came with the construction of Daecheong Dam in 1980, which created Daecheong Lake (\ub300\uccad\ud638), one of Korea's major reservoirs. This artificial lake now defines much of the county's character, with its serpentine shoreline creating numerous peninsulas, coves, and scenic viewpoints.\nThe Geum River (\uae08\uac15), Korea's third-longest river, flows through the county and forms spectacular meanders that have created the famous \"Korean Peninsula Topography\" viewpoint at Dunjubong. The county's interior features rolling hills and modest mountains, with Jangnyeongsan (\uc7a5\ub839\uc0b0) offering accessible hiking and forest recreation.\nThe landscape reflects typical Chungcheong agricultural patterns: terraced rice paddies in valley bottoms, orchards on hillsides, and extensive nursery operations that have made Okcheon one of Korea's primary seedling production centers.\nClimate.\nOkcheon County experiences a characteristic inland central-Korean climate with pronounced seasonal contrasts that create distinctly different visiting experiences throughout the year.\nWinter (December-February) brings cold, crisp conditions with temperatures frequently dropping below freezing. Snowfall is moderate but creates picturesque lakeside scenes. The dry air and clear skies make this an excellent time for photography, though outdoor activities require proper cold-weather preparation.\nSpring (March-May) may be the most rewarding season for visitors. Cherry blossoms and azaleas bloom throughout April, the Jeong Ji-yong Festival enlivens the county in late spring, and comfortable temperatures make hiking and lakeside walks particularly enjoyable. Rainfall increases gradually but remains manageable.\nSummer (June-August) is hot, humid, and wet under the East Asian monsoon influence (jangma). July and August see the heaviest rainfall, with monthly totals often exceeding 280 mm. Despite the heat and humidity, the lush green landscapes and full reservoir levels create their own appeal. Early morning or evening activities are most comfortable.\nAutumn (September-November) rivals spring as the ideal visiting season. September can still be warm, but October and November offer perfect hiking weather with spectacular fall foliage, especially along the lakeside roads and mountain trails. Stable weather patterns and clear skies provide excellent conditions for the peninsula-shaped river views at Dunjubong.\nGet in.\nBy train.\nOkcheon is served by conventional rail services on the historic Gyeongbu Line, which connects Seoul to Busan via the central corridor. While high-speed KTX trains bypass Okcheon, conventional Mugunghwa and ITX-Saemaeul services provide practical access.\nMost visitors from Seoul or international arrivals transfer at Daejeon Station, where connections to Okcheon are straightforward. The conventional trains offer comfortable seating and large windows ideal for enjoying the scenic Chungcheong countryside.\nBy bus.\nIntercity bus services provide flexible connections between Okcheon and major Chungcheong cities, often with more frequent departures than train services.\nMajor routes and frequencies:\nLocal bus network: From Okcheon-eup, county-operated local buses (\ub18d\uc5b4\ucd0c\ubc84\uc2a4) connect to villages and tourist sites including Janggye Tourist Site, Jangnyeongsan, and various lakeside areas. Frequencies are limited (often 4-8 services daily) with reduced weekend schedules. Timetables are posted at the bus terminal and major stops, but inquiring at the information desk is recommended for current schedules.\nBy car.\nDriving provides maximum flexibility for exploring Okcheon's dispersed attractions and is highly recommended for visitors planning to cover multiple lakeside and mountain sites in a day.\nMain access routes:\nParking: Most tourist sites offer free parking. Okcheon-eup has public parking near the traditional market and cultural sites (typically \u20a91,000-2,000 for 2-3 hours). Lakeside areas can become congested on sunny spring and autumn weekends; arriving before 10:00 ensures easier parking at popular spots like Janggye Tourist Site.\nRoad conditions: Main routes are well-maintained. Lakeside scenic roads are narrow in places with limited passing areas. Mountain access roads to Jangnyeongsan Recreation Forest are paved but winding. Winter driving may require snow tires or chains during January-February cold snaps.\nCar rental: No major rental agencies operate in Okcheon itself. Rent vehicles from Daejeon Station area or Cheongju before arrival. One-way rentals back to these cities are typically available.\nBy taxi.\nTaxis are readily available at Okcheon Station and the bus terminal, serving as practical fill-ins for routes not well-covered by local buses. Fares are metered and reasonable for short trips within the county.\nTypical fares:\nFor full-day or half-day touring, negotiate a charter rate (typically \u20a980,000-120,000 for 4-5 hours depending on distance). This works well for visitors without cars wanting to cover lakeside attractions efficiently. Drivers often provide local insights and photo-stop recommendations.\nFor remote lakeside and mountain locations, arrange return pickup times in advance, as finding taxis in rural areas can be difficult, especially on weekdays. Save your driver's phone number for the return call.\nGet around.\nMovement within Okcheon County requires planning, as public transportation is limited outside the main town. Understanding the options helps visitors make the most of their time.\nBy local bus.\nCounty-operated local buses (\ub18d\uc5b4\ucd0c\ubc84\uc2a4) radiate from Okcheon-eup to surrounding villages and some tourist sites. These services are designed primarily for local residents rather than tourists, resulting in limited frequencies and schedules that may not align with sightseeing plans.\nKey routes for visitors:\nPractical considerations: Buses display route numbers and destinations in Korean. Timetables change seasonally and are reduced on weekends and holidays. One-way fares are typically \u20a91,500-3,000 depending on distance. Payment by T-money or cash is accepted. Waiting times between services can be 2-4 hours, making this option best suited for visitors with flexible schedules or those staying overnight in specific areas.\nBy taxi.\nTaxis offer the most flexible transport option for visitors without cars, particularly when touring multiple sites or venturing to locations poorly served by buses.\nFinding taxis: Available at Okcheon Station, the bus terminal, and around the traditional market area during business hours. In the town center, taxis can usually be hailed on main streets. Outside Okcheon-eup, taxis are scarce\u2014always arrange return pickups when heading to remote locations.\nUsing taxis effectively: For half-day or full-day touring, negotiate a charter arrangement with the driver. Most are willing to wait at attractions while you explore, then continue to your next destination. This eliminates waiting for rare local buses and provides flexibility to adjust your itinerary. Drivers generally have good knowledge of local attractions and can suggest optimal routes.\nBy car.\nPersonal or rental vehicles provide freedom to explore Okcheon's scattered attractions at your own pace, making early-morning photography possible and allowing spontaneous stops at scenic viewpoints. They provide access to remote lakeside areas, and the ability to visit multiple sites efficiently. Parking is generally available and free or inexpensive.\nKorean-language GPS apps (Naver Map, Kakao Map) provide the most accurate directions and real-time traffic information. Download offline maps if mobile coverage concerns exist in remote valleys. Major attractions are well-signed in Korean; some have English transliterations.\nRural roads are generally relaxed. Lakeside scenic routes are narrow in places; yield to oncoming traffic at blind curves. Local drivers are courteous but may move slowly on mountain roads. Parking customs are informal in rural areas\u2014follow local practice at trailheads and viewpoints.\nOn foot.\nOkcheon-eup's compact downtown is easily explored on foot. The area around Okcheon Station, the traditional market, and the Jeong Ji-yong Museum complex can be covered in a pleasant 2-3 hour walking circuit. Sidewalks are available on main streets but may be limited in old-town lanes. Once beyond the town center, walking becomes impractical for reaching distant attractions due to distances and limited roadside walking infrastructure.\nBy bicycle.\nWhile not heavily promoted, cycling can be rewarding for fit visitors with appropriate equipment. The lakeside roads offer scenic riding, though traffic increases on weekends and holidays. No formal bike rental services exist in Okcheon, so cyclists must bring their own equipment or rent from Daejeon. Road shoulders vary in width; high-visibility clothing is advisable.\nSee.\nOkcheon County's attractions emphasize natural beauty, literary heritage, and authentic local culture over commercial tourism development. The experiences here reward visitors seeking genuine insight into rural Korean life and landscape.\nBuy.\nShopping in Okcheon reflects rural Korean county realities: practical agricultural products, seasonal produce, and local food specialties rather than crafts, souvenirs, or luxury goods. Visitors attuned to this reality can find authentic regional items and agricultural products unavailable in urban areas.\nWhat to buy.\nSeasonal fruit: Okcheon County's agricultural output includes high-quality grapes (\ud3ec\ub3c4), particularly prized varieties cultivated in the county's favorable microclimate. September through October is peak season. Persimmons (\uac10) follow in late autumn. Purchase at the traditional market or from roadside stands near orchards. Quality is excellent but transport considerations favor local consumption or gifting to Korean friends rather than international travel.\nNursery products: Okcheon's specialty seedling industry produces ornamental trees, fruit tree saplings, and landscape plants. The Seedling Festival provides best access, but nursery farms throughout Iwon-myeon conduct year-round business. Practically, international visitors cannot transport living plants, but Korean domestic buyers find excellent selection and prices. Visiting nursery operations offers insight into this specialized agricultural sector even without purchasing.\nTraditional market purchases: Okcheon's traditional market offers typical Chungcheong food products: dried fish and seafood, regional banchan (side dishes), seasonal kimchi preparations, traditional snacks and sweets, household items, and daily necessities. For visitors, small packages of regional specialties or traditional snacks make practical purchases. Market vendors sometimes vacuum-seal food items to facilitate transport.\nLimited souvenir options: The Jeong Ji-yong Museum shop sells poetry collections in Korean, postcards, bookmarks, and small themed items. Selection is modest but authentic to the literary focus. Traditional craft items or regional handwork are not prominent in Okcheon's commercial offerings\u2014this reflects the county's agricultural rather than artisan economy.\nPractical shopping considerations.\nHours vary. Traditional market operates daily but is most active mornings. Many small shops close one day weekly (often Sundays or Mondays). \nLanguage can be challenging. Outside convenience stores and chain businesses, English is minimal. Patience, gestures, and translation apps help. Vendors appreciate respectful efforts to communicate and are generally helpful despite language barriers.\nPayment methods: Cash remains preferred in the traditional market and small shops. Cards are widely accepted at convenience stores, supermarkets, and established businesses. ATMs are available in downtown Okcheon-eup; bring cash when venturing to rural areas.\nEat.\nOkcheon's dining scene reflects its rural character and Chungcheong regional identity: hearty, unpretentious local food emphasizing fresh ingredients, generous portions, and traditional preparations over modern fusion or international influences. English menus are rare; ordering may require gestures, translation apps, or adventurous pointing.\nLocal specialties.\nSaengseon-guksu (\uc0dd\uc120\uad6d\uc218): Freshwater fish noodle soup is Chungcheong's iconic comfort dish and Okcheon's culinary signature. The broth, simmered from whole freshwater fish (typically carp or catfish), achieves deep, clean flavor without fishiness. Thin wheat noodles, generous fish pieces, and subtle seasoning create satisfying, restorative meals. Every Okcheon local has their favored restaurant; quality is generally high. Expect whole fish requiring some bone navigation\u2014this is authentic traditional preparation.\nMinmul-maeuntang (\ubbfc\ubb3c\ub9e4\uc6b4\ud0d5): Spicy freshwater fish stew showcases Chungcheong's landlocked adaptation of Korea's seafood stew traditions. Fresh river fish in a robust, gochugaru-laced broth with vegetables and tofu creates warming, boldly flavored dishes perfect for cold weather or hearty appetites. Typically ordered for 2-3 people to share; the communal pot sitting on a burner at your table continues cooking throughout the meal.\nJeongsik (\uc815\uc2dd): Traditional Korean set meals feature multiple banchan (side dishes), rice, soup, and a main dish\u2014typically grilled fish, braised meat, or stew. Chungcheong jeongsik emphasizes vegetable variety and balanced flavors over extreme spiciness or rich sauces. This style suits visitors wanting to experience traditional Korean dining structure with diverse tastes in a single meal.\nPractical dining tips.\nLanguage challenges: English menus are extremely rare. Prepare by: 1) Learning basic dish names in Korean or having them on your phone, 2) Using translation apps to photograph and translate menus, 3) Asking your accommodation host for specific restaurant recommendations and having them write the dish names in Korean for you to show servers, 4) Being comfortable with \"pointing and hoping\"\u2014most servers will try to help and confirm your order.\nOrdering etiquette: Korean dining typically involves multiple people sharing dishes. Portion sizes assume this. Solo diners can still order full dishes but expect leftovers. Some restaurants offer smaller portions if you ask. Don't feel obligated to finish everything\u2014Koreans don't typically clean every plate, though wasting food is frowned upon.\nMeal timing: Lunch is 12:00-13:30, dinner 18:00-20:00. Many restaurants close between lunch and dinner service (approximately 14:30-17:00). Late dining (after 21:00) is limited in rural Okcheon. Breakfast dining-out culture is minimal; convenience stores or accommodation breakfasts are typical.\nBanchan: The small side dishes that accompany meals are usually refillable\u2014don't hesitate to ask for more of favorites. They're included in the meal price. The variety and quality of banchan often indicate overall restaurant standards.\nWater and tea: Most restaurants provide free water or barley tea. Korean dining doesn't emphasize elaborate beverages\u2014water or tea with meals is standard.\nBills and payment: Typically you pay at the register when leaving, not at the table. Bills usually aren't itemized in small restaurants\u2014the server knows what you ordered. Credit cards are widely accepted, but cash is prudent in very small establishments. Tipping is not practiced in Korea.\nDrink.\nOkcheon's drinking culture reflects rural Korean patterns: practical coffee shops for daily caffeine, simple bars frequented by locals, and limited nightlife compared to urban areas. Visitors seeking sophisticated cocktail scenes or craft beer selections won't find them here\u2014approach with appropriate expectations.\nSleep.\nAccommodation options in Okcheon County are limited, reflecting its rural character and modest tourist volumes. Most visitors either day-trip from Daejeon or stay one night to explore at a relaxed pace. Book ahead during the Jeong Ji-yong Festival (late spring) and peak autumn foliage season (mid-October) when the limited inventory fills quickly.\nConnect.\nMobile phones and internet.\nMobile coverage in Okcheon-eup and along main roads is reliable across all major Korean carriers (SK Telecom, KT, LG U+). Signal strength can weaken in remote mountain valleys and some lakeside areas away from main routes, but tourist sites generally maintain adequate connectivity. 4G/LTE is standard; 5G availability is limited to the town center.\nFor travelers: If you have a Korean SIM card or international roaming, connectivity supports navigation, translation apps, and communication throughout most of your visit. Download offline maps (Naver Map, Google Maps) before venturing to remote locations as a backup. GPS generally functions even when data is weak.\nWiFi availability: Caf\u00e9s and convenience stores in Okcheon-eup typically offer free WiFi\u2014passwords are usually posted or provided on request. Accommodations include WiFi though speeds in small establishments may be modest. Tourist sites generally lack WiFi beyond what your mobile data provides.\nPostal services.\nA post office operates in downtown Okcheon-eup, handling mail, packages, and some banking services. Hours are typically 09:00-17:00 weekdays, limited Saturday service, closed Sundays and holidays. Staff speak Korean only but postal transactions are usually manageable through gestures and written information.\nGo next.\nOkcheon's central location on the Chungcheong-Yeongnam corridor provides excellent access to diverse onward destinations, each offering distinct experiences that complement Okcheon's rural character.\nSuggested itineraries.\nChungcheong rural circuit (3-4 days): Daejeon (base) \u2192 Day trip to Okcheon \u2192 Day trip to Boeun/Songnisan \u2192 Day trip to Gongju or Buyeo \u2192 Return or continue to Seoul/Busan. This covers major central Korean heritage sites with manageable day trips from comfortable Daejeon accommodation.\nSeoul-Busan slow route (5-7 days): Seoul \u2192 Daejeon (overnight) \u2192 Okcheon (day or overnight) \u2192 Gyeongju (2 nights) \u2192 Busan (2+ nights). This corridor route emphasizes smaller destinations and rural Korea between the major urban endpoints, providing balanced urban-rural experiences.\nChungcheong mountains and lakes (2-3 days): Daejeon \u2192 Okcheon/Daecheong Lake (overnight) \u2192 Boeun/Songnisan (overnight) \u2192 Return to Daejeon or Cheongju. Focus on natural landscapes and quiet rural experiences away from major tourist routes."}
{"id": "213113", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213113", "title": "Gimpo", "text": "Gimpo (\uae40\ud3ec) is in the western part of Gyeonggi, South Korea and lower part of the Han River. As soon as the Ara Waterway in Gyeongin was completed, the area was surrounded by water. Although the area is treated as an island, the eastern part of the island and Gochon-eup are Incheon, and the northern part of the Yangchon-eup is a peninsula close to Seoul.\nUnderstand.\nGimpo sits between the Han River and the Yellow Sea (locally called West Sea). It boasts a rich history, cultural heritage, and vast plains. Today, Gimpo is characterized by a unique contrast, where the rapidly developing Hangang New Town (featuring modern landmarks like Laveniche) coexists with the traditional downtown areas and extensive agricultural plains, which famously produce the high-quality Gimpo Geumsal rice. Due to its strategic location on the Han River, the city has historically served as a vital gateway for water transportation and a key military defense point protecting the capital, Seoul. The name 'Gimpo' is said to originate from the legend of 'Tugeumpo,' where two brothers found a gold nugget in the river, and the younger brother, out of jealousy, threw it back into the water. Another theory suggests that the ancient word 'geom' means sacred land, indicating Gimpo's long-standing historical significance.\nOrientation.\nGimpo is divided into several districts and neighborhoods, each with its own unique atmosphere and attractions.\nHistory.\nGimpo\u2019s history can be traced back to ancient kingdoms, as its territory once belonged to Goguryeo and later the Silla dynasty. The region is renowned for the fertile Gimpo Plain, which has been used for rice cultivation for millennia, even providing rice to Korean royalty\u2014archaeological evidence indicates rice was grown here over 5,000 years ago. Local legends claim Gimpo\u2019s name comes from a gold nugget thrown into the river. Throughout Korea\u2019s dynastic past, the Gimpo area played an important defensive role with historic sites like Deokpojin and Munsusanseong Fortress, serving as strongholds along major rivers and strategic entry routes toward Seoul.\nIn the 20th century, Gimpo\u2019s transformation accelerated during the Japanese occupation with the construction of Gimpo Airfield, which later played a key role in the Korean War. After the war, Gimpo developed rapidly, highlighted by the growth around Gimpo International Airport and large-scale land development including residential districts and massive urbanization projects such as the Han River New Town. Officially promoted to city status in 1998, Gimpo has experienced continued population growth, advanced transportation infrastructure, and a vibrant coexistence of historical tradition and modern city life. Today, Gimpo offers visitors a window into Korea\u2019s past and present, with rich heritage sites standing alongside thriving urban areas.\nClimate.\nGimpo features a monsoon-influenced humid continental climate (\"K\u00f6ppen: Dwa\"), characterized by four distinct seasons. Its location near the Yellow Sea (West Sea) influences its weather, making it slightly milder and breezier than more inland cities like Seoul and Suwon, and comparable in overall pattern to nearby Incheon.\nAnnual precipitation is high, with the vast majority concentrated in the summer months. The region is noticeably breezy year-round, particularly in its open and coastal areas due to the maritime influence.\nOverall, Gimpo\u2019s climate combines marine and continental influences, making it slightly milder than inland cities like Suwon, with more humidity and wind because of its closeness to the West Sea.\nSummer (June to August).\nSummers are long, hot, muggy, and wet, with high humidity and concentrated rainfall. The monsoon season (\"Jangma\") typically peaks during July and August, bringing the majority of the annual precipitation. Average high temperatures in these months reach 29\u2218C\u201330\u2218C (84\u2218F\u201386\u2218F). The coastal proximity contributes to high humidity, often exceeding 75% and feeling particularly heavy.\nWinter (December to February).\nWinters are short, cold, and dry, with clear skies and occasional, but not heavy, snowfall. The coldest month is January, where average daily high temperatures are near 0 C and average lows drop to around -7 C.\nSpring and Autumn.\nThese transitional seasons offer the most pleasant weather and are generally considered the best times to visit. They are relatively mild, less rainy, and feature clear skies, with comfortable temperatures typically ranging from 10 C to 22 C.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nGimpo International Airport (GMP) is located on the border of Seoul and Gimpo. While many travelers use it to reach Seoul, it is also the primary gateway to Gimpo city. Travelers can easily reach the city center via the Gimpo Goldline, which connects the airport station directly to major residential and commercial areas like Sau, Unyang, and Gurae. Local buses and taxis are also available for destinations not covered by the rail line.\nBy train.\nGimpo is well-connected to Seoul and Incheon via subway and railway services, with Gimpo International Airport acting as the main transit hub. Gimpo is connected to the Seoul metropolitan subway network primarily via the Gimpo Goldline. This light rail line starts at Gimpo International Airport Station, where travelers can transfer from Seoul Subway Lines 5 and 9, as well as the AREX (Airport Railroad Express). The Goldline runs through the heart of Gimpo city, making it the most convenient way to get in by rail.\nBy bus.\nGimpo is served by an extensive network of intercity and regional buses connecting it to the Seoul Metropolitan Area.\nGet around.\nBy train.\nThe primary way to move between major residential and commercial districts within Gimpo is the Gimpo Goldline (\uae40\ud3ec\uace8\ub4dc\ub77c\uc778) light rail system.\nBy bus.\nGimpo's local bus system covers areas not serviced by the Goldline, providing access to historical sites, rural areas, and coastal attractions.\nBy taxi.\nTaxis are readily available near subway stations, bus terminals, and major commercial areas (especially in Gurae-dong and Janggi-dong).\nBy bicycle.\nGimpo has excellent bicycle paths, especially along the Ara Waterway and within the Hangang New Town.\nSleep.\nPensions.\nIn South Korea, the word for a boarding house where three meals a day are provided along with your room is \"pension\". There are 1,635 pensions around Incheon according to a 'Naver page', including the following:"}
{"id": "213114", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213114", "title": "Goheung", "text": "Goheung (\u9ad8\u8208\u90e1) is in South Jeolla. Goheung is an aerospace city that embraces the Dadohae.\nUnderstand.\nGoheung County is located in the southeastern part of Jeollanam-do and consists of the Goheung Peninsula and its surrounding islands. It is home to the Naro Space Center, the launch site for Korea\u2019s space rockets \u201cNaro\u201d and \u201cNuri,\u201d operated by the Korea Aerospace Research Institute (KARI).\nCockles are harvested mainly along the northern coast of Goheung. These cockles are often transported over the hills to Beolgyo, where they are sold under the name \u201cBeolgyo Cockles.\u201d The region is also famous for its yuzu (citron) production; however, due to the difficulty of cultivation, the cultivation area is gradually decreasing, while pomegranate farming has been increasing in popularity. In the northern areas, garlic is also a well-known local specialty.\nHistory.\nGoheung County is a scenic peninsula surrounded by the sea on three sides, with a history of human settlement dating back to prehistoric times. Archaeological sites reveal early farming and fishing communities. Historically, it was part of Mahan, later Baekje, and a key naval base during the Joseon era where Admiral Yi Sun-sin fought. Today, Goheung consists of 2 towns and 14 townships, celebrated for its beautiful nature and rich cultural heritage.\nClimate.\nGoheung-gun's climate shows the characteristics of high temperature and humidity in summer and warm drying in winter.\nWhile Goheung is relatively less affected by continents than other regions, it is located on the southern coast where turbulence flows throughout the year, so winter temperatures are relatively mild and rain more than snow, and in summer, the topographical convergence of humid air currents creates a rainy climate.\nTherefore, it strongly represents the characteristics of oceanic climates with relatively few annual crossings and mild and high precipitation.\nClimate differences vary slightly depending on the mountainous, plain, and coastal regions, but they are generally in a mild climate zone, with an average temperature of 1.0\u2103 in January and an average annual temperature of 13.5\u2103, which is home to ovarian plants, such as birch and camellia trees.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest airport to Goheung is Yeosu Airport (RSU), located about 40 km away. From Yeosu Airport, you can take a local bus or taxi to Goheung, which takes approximately 1 hour. There are also flights connecting Yeosu to Seoul (Gimpo), Busan, and Jeju.\nBy bus.\nGoheung Bus Terminal operates regular intercity bus services to major cities such as Seoul (Central City Terminal), Gwangju, Suncheon, and Yeosu.\nBy train.\nThere is no direct train service to Goheung. The nearest train stations are Suncheon Station and Yeosu Expo Station on the Jeolla Line. From either station, you can transfer to a bus bound for Goheung (about 1\u20131.5 hours)\nGet around.\nGoheung's attractions are widely dispersed across the peninsula and islands, making a private car/rental car the most convenient and efficient mode of transport.\nBuy.\nGoheung is famous for its abundance of agricultural and fishery specialties, collectively known as the 'Goheung Eight Products' (\u516b\u54c1). These specialties can also be purchased online. https://go-hmall.com/ http://www.aaw.kr/"}
{"id": "213115", "revid": "2442140", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213115", "title": "Anyang (Gyeonggi)", "text": "\"For the city in China, see Anyang\"\nAnyang (\uc548\uc591, \u5b89\u990a) is a city in the southern part of Gyeonggi Province, and one of the satellite cities of Seoul. It serves as the central hub of the Anyang region, which also includes Gunpo and Uiwang. Once known as a major industrial city, it has since shifted toward a knowledge- and service-based economy. \nUnderstand.\nAnyang is bordered by Uiwang to the east, Gwangmyeong, Siheung, and Ansan to the west, Gunpo to the south, and Seoul\u2019s Geumcheon and Gwanak districts as well as Gwacheon to the north.\nThe Pyeongchon New Town area is famous for its strong emphasis on education. It has the highest concentration of private academies in Gyeonggi Province, and local schools consistently achieve high admission rates to prestigious special-purpose high schools nationwide.\nHistory.\nAnyang is a city just southwest of Seoul. Its name comes from Anyangsa Temple, which was built during the Goryeo Dynasty and means \u201ca place of peace and rest.\u201d Archaeological finds show people have lived in the area since ancient times. Anyang became a city in 1973 and has grown rapidly due to its location near Seoul. Today, it is a modern city known for its industry and culture, where tradition and modern life exist side by side. \nClimate.\nAnyang's climate is similar to that of nearby Seoul and Suwon. It has a distinct humid continental climate, characterized by four clear seasons. The city is relatively hotter and colder than coastal areas, showing a significant temperature difference between summer and winter. The city experiences a notable monsoon season from June to September, when most of the annual rainfall is concentrated. According to data from the Korea Meteorological Administration, Anyang receives an average of about 1,198 mm of precipitation annually. The city has well-equipped drainage facilities, so there is typically no major flood damage. Although it's not a coastal city, the summer humidity can be high, often exceeding 90% even on days without rain. During the night, it can even reach 100%.\nGet in.\nApproximate travel times from other locations are as follows:\nBy subway.\nAnsan is served by seven subway stations. Four stations are in Line 1, listed from the closest to Seoul: Seoksu, Gwanak, Anyang, Myeonghak. Three stations are in Line 4, listed from the closest to Seoul: Indeokwon, Pyeongchon, Beomgye.\nBy bus.\nExpress buses from Seoul (Gangnam, Seoul Express Bus Terminal) take 1\u20132 hours.\nLocal buses connect between districts.\nGet around.\nBy bicycle.\nHiring a bicycle is a good way to get around this city. There are bicycle sharing services such as 'Everybike (\uc5d0\ube0c\ub9ac \ubc14\uc774\ud06c)', 'KakaoBike (\uce74\uce74\uc624 \ubc14\uc774\ud06c)'.\nBy taxi.\nRegular taxi: You can take a taxi at taxi stand. The taxi stand is near the bus station. As of 2023, the basic fare for regular taxis is \u20a94,800 (\u20a95,800 at night), with a surcharge of \u20a9100 applied according to time and distance. (The basic fare is up to 1.6 km (1 mi), plus \u20a9100 per 135 m.)\nCall taxi: When you move to another place where is hard to access, you can call a taxi. Basic fare of call taxi is around \u20a93,800. You can call kakao taxi or T-map taxi. Kakao taxi is easier to call than T-map taxi.\nDo.\nFestivals.\nThere are several regular festivals in Anyang. Most of the festivals are held in Pyeongchon Central Park.\nMovie theaters.\nThere are several movie theaters in Anyang. Big multiplex movie theater chains, such as CGV, Lotte Cinema show movies of various genres.\nStay safe.\nAnyang is as safe as most urban areas in South Korea. Standard urban common-sense applies: keep valuables secure, be aware of late-night transport options.\nAs always with travel, monitor local weather (especially summer heat/humidity and monsoon rain) and transit updates."}
{"id": "213121", "revid": "2441212", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213121", "title": "Siheung", "text": "Siheung (\uc2dc\ud765, \u59cb\u8208) is a city to the southwest of Seoul. It is a satellite city because it is close to Seoul and Incheon. Because of the Sihwa National Industrial Complex, it forms an important pillar of Gyeonggi Province's industry on its own, and is also an attractive city with nature, such as the Siheung Gaetgol Ecology Park and sea route.\nDistricts.\nSiheung's urban structure is largely divided into five districts: Northern, Eastern, Central, Wolgot, and Jeongwang Living Zone.\nUnderstand.\nA city located in southwestern Gyeonggi-do. The Siheung-si area used to be the land of Incheon Dohobu (formerly Sorae-eup) and Ansan-gun (formerly Suam-myeon, Gunmyeon), and has no connection to the original Siheung. \nThe city area is about 140 \u33a2. However, as the city is widely distributed like Yongin and Namyangju, and the ratio of mountains and flat lands is similar, it feels much wider than the actual area.\nAt the time of the city's promotion in 1989, the population was only around 100,000, but from the 1990s to the early 2000s, the number of people surged to 310,000 in just over a decade as various land readjustment projects, housing site development projects, and residential areas behind the Sihwa Industrial Complex were moved in. Since then, development has been carried out in various places such as Neunggok, Baegot, Mokgam, Eungye, and Janghyeon, adding fuel to the population growth, and even now, various projects such as Ha-joong District, Geomo District, and Gwangmyeong Siheung New Town are planned and underway, so they are free from the problem of population decline, which is a nationwide headache.\nHistory.\nAnother meaning of Siheung is as follows.\nClimate.\nSiheung\u2019s climate is very similar to that of nearby Ansan, Incheon, and Seoul. Compared to inland cities such as Suwon, Siheung shows more moderate temperature changes throughout the year. It generally features a temperate climate influenced by both continental and marine characteristics. As Siheung is located along the West Sea, sea breezes frequently affect the area.\nAccording to the Korea Meteorological Administration, Siheung\u2019s climate is closer to that of Incheon than to inland regions like Suwon. Summers tend to be slightly cooler and winters slightly warmer than Seoul or Suwon, with an average difference of about 1\u20132 degrees Celsius due to maritime influence. While most areas of Siheung do not show strong coastal-city characteristics, the nearby tidal flats and coastline contribute to a relatively mild and comfortable climate. Annual precipitation is around 1,300mm, similar to other west coast regions. Although summer is very humid due to oceanic influences, the city has well-developed drainage systems, so flood damage is rare.\nGet in.\nBy train/subway.\nThere are eight subway stations in Siheung. Five stations are on the Seohae Line, three on the Suin-Bundang Line, and two of the three stations are also shared with Line 4.\nBy bus.\nExpress buses from Seoul (Gangnam, Seoul Express Bus Terminal) take 1\u20132 hours. Local buses connect districts and coastal areas.\nGet around.\nBy bicycle.\nSiheung has well-equipped bicycle paths and is conveniently located on the streets with various bicycles that offer rental services, such as 'Everybike(\uc5d0\ube0c\ub9ac\ubc14\uc774\ud06c),' 'KakaoBike(\uce74\uce74\uc624\ubc14\uc774\ud06c),' and 'SOCAR Elekle(\uc3d8\uce74 \uc77c\ub808\ud074).' It also operates a public bicycle rental shop.\nBy bus.\nSiheung City Bus operates a variety of routes that connect major areas in the city as well as routes that lead to the outskirts. In Siheung City, there are regular city buses that connect village buses for local residents and major bases, and wide-area buses and shuttle buses that connect to other areas of the Seoul metropolitan area, such as Seoul and Incheon, are also available. This diversification of routes is greatly helpful in enhancing the convenience of residents and enhancing the efficiency of public transportation on their way to and from work. This composition of routes makes it easier to travel to various destinations, including commuting to and from Siheung City.\nBy taxi.\nYou can take a taxi at the taxi stand. The basic fare for a regular taxi is about \u20a9 4,800. The taxi stand is near the bus stop.\nThe basic fare for a regular taxi is \u20a9 4,800 per 1.6 km, after which the additional distance charge is \u20a9 100 per 131 m and the hourly rate is \u20a9100 per 30 seconds.\nLate at night, the surcharge is applied and the hours are from 23:00 to 4:00 a.m. the next day. The surcharge rate is 30%.\nSleep.\nThere are various hotels and motels at low prices."}
{"id": "213122", "revid": "2379452", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213122", "title": "Beiwudang Mountain Scenic Area", "text": "The Beiwudang Mountain Scenic Area (\u5317\u6b66\u5f53\u5c71\u98ce\u666f\u533a) is located in Fangshan County, L\u00fcliang City, Shanxi Province. It is one of the birthplace of Taoism in China and a sacred place of Taoism in the north. It is known as the first mountain in Shanxi. The scenic area has 72 peaks, 32 cliffs and 24 streams.\nNot to be confused with Wudang Mountain National Park (Hubei).\nSee.\nAlso known as \u201cZhenwu Mountain\u201d."}
{"id": "213124", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213124", "title": "Junlian", "text": "Junlian (\u7b60\u8fde\u53bf) is a county in East Sichuan, China. It is located in the southern part of Sichuan province, known for its rich history, beautiful natural scenery, and local cultural heritage.\nUnderstand.\nJunlian County, located in Yibin City, is a county with a long history, rich culture and abundant natural resources.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nJ\u00fanli\u00e1n County is accessible by road from the major cities in Sichuan and Chongqing."}
{"id": "213125", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213125", "title": "Neijiang", "text": "Neijiang (\u5185\u6c5f) also known as the \u201cSweet City\u201d, is a prefecture-level city in East Sichuan, between Chengdu and Chongqing. Neijiang is known for its long history and cultural heritage, with attractions such as the Longchang Ancient Archways, Xilin Temple, Chonglong Mountain cliff carvings, and the Weiyuan dome landform. It is also the hometown of painter Zhang Daqian, earning the title \u201cHometown of Calligraphy and Painting\u201d.\nUnderstand.\nNeijiang is a prefecture-level city in Sichuan Province, with a strategically important location as a major transportation hub in Southwest China and a key intersection of the Belt and Road Initiative. The city covers an area of about 5,385 square kilometers, governing 2 districts, 2 counties, and 1 county-level city, with a registered population of approximately 3.97 million (as of 2023).\nNeijiang is both a national old industrial base and a rapidly developing emerging city in southern Sichuan. With high-speed rail lines running throughout the area, it takes just over an hour to reach either Chengdu or Chongqing. The city moves at a relaxed pace and offers a strong sense of everyday life\u2014an ideal place to experience the slow rhythm and charm of Bashu culture.\nHistory.\nDuring the Western Han dynasty, Zizhong County was established, and in the Eastern Han period, Hanan County was later created to its south. Because the area was encircled by rivers for over 90 li (about 45 kilometers), it was renamed Neijiang during the reign of Emperor Wen of the Sui dynasty.\nNeijiang is also the hometown of Chang Hong, the teacher of Confucius, and the renowned Chinese painter Zhang Daqian, earning it the titles \u201cHometown of Zhang Daqian\u201d and \u201cCity of Calligraphy and Painting.\u201d\nClimate.\nNeijiang has a subtropical humid monsoon climate, influenced by the terrain of the Sichuan Basin and local environmental conditions. The city experiences mild temperatures, abundant rainfall, ample sunlight, and a long frost-free period. The year is generally cloudy and humid, with relatively warm winters and hot summers. Most of the rainfall occurs in summer, accounting for the majority of annual precipitation.\nNeijiang has abundant heat resources and moderate sunlight, making it suitable for crop growth. The annual relative humidity is relatively high, and the overall climate is stable, though droughts, heavy rains, and other extreme weather events can occur. Traditionally, the region is known for the saying \u201ca major drought every ten years, a minor drought every five years,\u201d reflecting the alternating patterns of dry and wet weather.\nGet in.\nNeijiang is well connected by road and rail to major cities in China.\nBy bus.\nNeijiang has several bus stations that connect the city with Chengdu, Chongqing, Leshan, and other nearby cities and towns.\nGet around.\nBy bus.\nThe fare is usually \u00a51\u20132, with routes covering major attractions and transport hubs in the city.\nBy taxi.\nThe starting fare is about \u00a56\u20138, and short trips within the city typically cost no more than \u00a520."}
{"id": "213126", "revid": "2182907", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213126", "title": "Xilai Ancient Town", "text": "Xilai Ancient Town (\u897f\u6765\u53e4\u9547, X\u012bl\u00e1i G\u01d4zh\u00e8n) is a historic riverside town in Pujiang County, about 80 km southwest of Chengdu, Sichuan. It is known for its well-preserved Ming and Qing dynasty architecture, old stone streets, ancient banyan trees, and local tea culture. Xilai is a good option for a half-day or full-day trip from Chengdu.\nUnderstand.\nXilai dates back more than 1,700 years and flourished during the Tang and Song dynasties as a trading hub along the ancient tea routes. Today, it retains a number of traditional residences, guild halls, and pagodas, while also serving as a weekend leisure spot for visitors from Chengdu.\nGet in.\nBy bus.\nDirect long-distance buses run from Chengdu (Xinnanmen Bus Station) to Pujiang County. From Pujiang, take a local minibus or taxi (~15 minutes).\nBy car.\nThe journey is 1.5\u20132 hours from Chengdu via the Chengdu\u2013Ya\u2019an Expressway (\u6210\u96c5\u9ad8\u901f).\nBy train.\nThere is one railway station in Xilai:\nAlternatively, take the high-speed rail to Qionglai or Pujiang stations, then continue by taxi or local bus.\nGet around.\nThe old town is compact and best explored on foot. Wear comfortable shoes for the cobbled streets.\nSleep.\nThere are a few guesthouses and boutique inns inside the old town.\nStay safe.\nThe old stone streets can be slippery in rain. Pickpocketing is rare but keep valuables safe during festivals."}
{"id": "213128", "revid": "2183461", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213128", "title": "Dongtai", "text": "Dongtai is a city located in the central coastal area of Jiangsu. \nIt faces the Yellow Sea to the east, borders Taizhou to the west, Nantong to the south, and Yancheng to the north.\nUnderstand.\nDongtai has a total area of 3,240 square kilometers. The city is well known for its salt production."}
{"id": "213130", "revid": "235382", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213130", "title": "Pujiang", "text": "Pujiang (\u84b2\u6c5f, P\u00faji\u0101ng) is a county in Sichuan, about 80 km southwest of Chengdu. It is known for its tea culture, ancient towns, scenic lakes, and Taoist temples. Pujiang makes a pleasant short trip from Chengdu for travelers interested in quieter, less commercialized destinations.\nUnderstand.\nPujiang County covers an area of around 600 km\u00b2, and a population of 460,000 in 2020. It lies in a fertile basin with a mild climate, ideal for growing tea. The county seat, Pujiang Town, is the main transport hub and base for exploring the surrounding sights.\nXilai Ancient Town, which is also in Pujiang County, is covered in a separate article.\nGet in.\nPujiang is located southwest of Chengdu and is most easily reached via the provincial capital, which serves as the main transportation hub for the region.\nBy bus.\nLong-distance buses operate frequently between Chengdu and Pujiang, with services departing from major terminals such as Xinnanmen and Shiyangchang. The trip usually takes 1.5 to 2 hours, depending on traffic conditions. Buses arrive at Pujiang Bus Station, from where taxis and local buses provide easy access to the town center and nearby villages.\nBy train.\nPujiang is on the Chengdu\u2013Ya\u2019an high-speed railway and is normally served by Pujiang Station, offering a fast and comfortable connection from Chengdu East Railway Station. As of October 2025, Pujiang Station is temporarily closed for a major upgrade and is expected to reopen in May 2026. During the closure, passengers should get off at Chaoyang Lake Station and continue to Pujiang by bus or taxi, which usually takes about 30 to 40 minutes.\nBy car.\nDriving from Chengdu to Pujiang takes around 1.5 hours under normal traffic conditions. The most direct route follows the Chengdu\u2013Ya\u2019an Expressway (\u6210\u96c5\u9ad8\u901f), which provides convenient access to the county. Traveling by car is a good option for visitors who wish to explore the surrounding countryside, tea plantations, and nearby scenic areas at their own pace.\nGet around.\nPujiang is a small and relaxed county town with a compact urban area, and getting around is generally easy and convenient. Most daily travel within the town can be done by public bus, taxi, shared bike, or on foot, and distances between the main commercial streets, residential areas, and riverside parks are short. For trips to nearby tea plantations, rural villages, or scenic countryside areas, taxis or chartered cars are usually the most practical options.\nBy bus.\nPujiang\u2019s public bus system connects the main town area with surrounding neighborhoods and nearby townships. Bus fares are usually inexpensive, typically around \u00a51\u20132 per ride. Most routes operate from early morning until around 20:00\u201321:00. Buses are commonly used by local residents and are the most economical way for visitors to move around the town, although schedules can be less frequent outside peak hours.\nBy shared bike.\nShared bicycles are available in parts of the urban area and near riverside parks and residential neighborhoods. They are well suited for short trips, casual riding, and exploring flat areas of the town. Since Pujiang is surrounded by rolling hills and rural countryside, cycling is most practical within the central urban zone and less suitable for longer trips into the surrounding villages.\nBy taxi and ride-hailing.\nTaxis and ride-hailing services such as Didi are available in Pujiang, though they are less numerous than in large cities. The starting fare is usually around \u00a57\u20139, and most trips within the town cost between \u00a510 and \u00a520. Taxis are especially useful for traveling in the evening, carrying luggage, or visiting tea gardens and rural attractions outside the town center.\nOn foot.\nPujiang\u2019s town center is easy and pleasant to explore on foot. The main streets, riverside greenways, small parks, and local markets are all within comfortable walking distance. Walking is a good way to experience daily local life, especially in the early evening when residents gather for strolling, chatting, and light exercise.\nConnect.\nMobile network coverage in Pujiang is generally reliable within the county town and most surrounding towns. All three major Chinese carriers\u2014China Mobile, China Unicom, and China Telecom\u2014provide 4G service across the urban area, and 5G is available in parts of the town center. Voice calls and mobile data work smoothly in hotels, restaurants, shopping streets, and residential areas. In rural tea plantation areas and low mountain villages, the signal may become weaker or unstable.\nVisitors who need mobile data can purchase a local SIM card at convenience stores, mobile service halls, or directly at Chengdu Shuangliu International Airport before arriving in Pujiang. eSIM services also work for most unlocked international phones. A passport is required for SIM registration.\nWi-Fi is commonly available in hotels, caf\u00e9s, larger restaurants, and shopping centers, and most mid-range chain hotels in Pujiang provide free in-room Wi-Fi. Connection speeds are generally sufficient for messaging, navigation, and video streaming, although occasional slowdowns can occur during peak evening hours.\nInternational websites and apps may be partially restricted in mainland China. Travelers who rely on services such as Google, Instagram, WhatsApp, or Gmail should set up a VPN before arrival, as VPN apps cannot always be downloaded easily once inside China.\nPostal services are available at the Pujiang County Post Office and several smaller neighborhood outlets. Sending domestic parcels is convenient and inexpensive. International mail is possible but slower than in large cities, and services are more limited.\nGo next.\nChengdu (\u6210\u90fd) is the most common next stop from Pujiang and lies about one hour away by car or intercity bus. As the capital of Sichuan Province, Chengdu offers a much wider range of museums, shopping districts, nightlife, and dining options, as well as major transport hubs including high-speed rail services and an international airport.\nMingshan (\u540d\u5c71) and the surrounding tea-growing areas near Ya\u2019an form a natural extension of a trip focused on Pujiang\u2019s tea culture. Known for misty mountains, traditional tea villages, and a slower rural lifestyle, this area can usually be reached by bus or taxi within one to two hours.\nQionglai (\u909b\u5d03) lies to the west of Pujiang and serves as a gateway to forest parks, hiking routes, and hot spring resorts in the western Sichuan mountain corridor. It is better suited for travelers with flexible schedules and is typically reached by a combination of bus travel and private transport.\nMeishan (\u7709\u5c71) is a convenient southbound stop known for cultural sites related to the famous poet Su Shi (Su Dongpo) and its traditional Sichuan city atmosphere. From Meishan, travelers can easily continue onward to Leshan and the Giant Buddha."}
{"id": "213133", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213133", "title": "Tongxiang", "text": "Tongxiang is a county-level city in northern Zhejiang Province, China, renowned for its rich cultural heritage, thriving economy, and picturesque water towns like Wuzhen, serving as the hometown of famous writer Mao Dun and a key hub for textiles and tourism.\nUnderstand.\nTongxiang is situated in the northern part of Zhejiang Province, within the heart of the Hangjiahu Plain. It covers an area of 727 square kilometers and has a population of approximately 1.227 million. The city is strategically located in the \"Golden Triangle\" between Shanghai, Hangzhou, and Suzhou, with convenient access to major cities within a one-hour drive.\nSee.\nWuzhen.\nWuzhen used to be two towns administered by different prefectures, divided by a canal. The two main preserved quarters of Wuzhen today (the \"Eastern Quarter\" and \"Western Quarter\") are located on opposite sides of the same canal, and access to them are quite separate. They are based around two old main streets called the \"Eastern Palisade\" (Dongzha) and \"Western Palisade\" (Xizha) respectively. Both of these quarters have been comprehensively redeveloped for tourists, are fenced off and charge admission. The areas around the \"Northern Pallisade\" and \"Southern Palisade\" (two other old main streets) are also preserved scenic areas, but have not been redeveloped for tourists as much (and still have local residents) and so have less of a \"theme park\" feel. They are not as scenic, but the Southern Quarter in particular is interesting for a walk that is further off the beaten track, although there are few attractions open to the public. The Northern Quarter has a couple of preserved buildings open to the public, but it has mostly been replaced by modern buildings, so is much less atmospheric. Between the old streets is the modern town centre, which is less atmospheric but has cheaper accommodation than (especially) the Western Quarter."}
{"id": "213136", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213136", "title": "Zhongmu County", "text": "Zhongmu County is in Henan, located northeast of Zhengzhou City, lies in the heart of the Central Plains and is known as the \"Back Garden of Zhengzhou.\" With a long history and rich cultural heritage, the county is a vital component of Zhengzhou City. Zhongmu County is committed to developing ecotourism and rural tourism, attracting numerous visitors.\nUnderstand.\nZhongmu County, with a history of over 2,700 years, served as the capital of the State of Zhao during the Spring and Autumn and Warring States periods. After the death of Marquis Xiang of Zhao, his successor, Marquis Xian of Zhao, ascended the throne at a young age and moved the capital from Jinyang to Zhongmu in 423 BC. The city remained the capital until 386 BC, when Marquis Jing of Zhao relocated the capital to Handan, making Zhongmu the capital for a total of 38 years.\nThe county is the birthplace of many well-known historical tales, among which the most renowned include the Battle of Guandu, the Three Wonders of Lu Gong, the Hometown of Zhong Yao, the Anecdotes of Pan An, and Confucius Turning His Carriage Around. These stories have not only shaped Zhongmu's cultural heritage but have also profoundly influenced the course of Chinese history.\nHistory.\nZhongmu has a long history and has been an important region of agricultural civilization in Central China since ancient times. Multiple cultural relics have been discovered within the country, showcasing its rich historical and cultural heritage.\nZhongmou County has become a region with convenient transportation and thriving industries, thanks to the development of the Zhengzhou metropolitan area. This is not only an important agricultural production area but also attracts tourists for its modern tourism and leisure facilities such as Henan Drama Cinema, Zhengzhou International Cultural and Creative Park, Zhengzhou Fangte Joy World, and Green Expo Park.\nZhongmou is also renowned for its local specialty snacks and folk culture, such as braised noodles and Sichuan spicy soups, which embody typical Central Plains flavors.\nZhongmu's population is primarily Han. Rural traditions and lifestyle habits reflect characteristics common in the Central Plains region, including local festivals, food culture, and dialect features of Henan Province. (Specific data on the proportion of ethnic minorities is unavailable.)\nAgriculture is well-developed in the county, with many villages relying on crops such as wheat, corn, vegetables, and fruit. Agriculture and the rural economy play an essential role in local livelihoods.\nClimate.\nZhongmu has a warm temperate continental monsoon climate, with distinct seasons, hot and humid summers, and cold winters. \nThe average annual temperature ranges from about 14\u201315 \u00b0C. Although winter is cold, extreme low temperatures are uncommon.\nAnnual precipitation is approximately 600\u2013700 mm, with most rainfall occurring between June and August. The county experiences a clear summer rainy season.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nZhongmou County is 25 kilometers south of Zhengzhou Xinzheng International Airport and you can reach two-thirds of the country's major cities in 1.5 hours.\nBy train.\nThere is a high-speed train from Zhengzhou East Station to Zhongmu County, which takes approximately 20 minutes. The Jingguang High-Speed Rail (Jinggang High-Speed Rail) and S223 run north-south; they connect west to the Jingguang Railway and G107 Line, and east to the Jingjiu Railway and G106 Line. Stations: Zhengzhou Airport Station, Green Expo Garden Station, Jialuhe Station, Zhanyang Station.\nBy car.\nFrom central Zhengzhou, take the Beijing-Hong Kong-Macao Expressway (G4) and reach Zhongmu County in approximately 30 minutes. \nBy bus.\nZhongmou \u2194 Kaifeng: There are direct intercity buses departing from Zhongmou Bus Station to Kaifeng Central Station, operating approximately 3 times a day. Bus Shuttle / City Commute: Bus Route 888 connects Zhongmou Bus Station with Zhengzhou High-Speed Rail Long-distance Bus Station, operating from approximately 05:10 to 19:40 (subject to adjustment during special times), with intervals of about 12 to 20 minutes. Local bus routes such as Route 1, Route 6, Route 8, and Route 798 can be taken from Zhongmou Bus Station to Green Expo Park Station or transfer points at metro stations (or intercity stations).\nGet around.\nBy bus.\nZhongmu operates 22 bus routes, all designed to integrate seamlessly with Metro Line 8, ensuring smooth transfers between major transit hubs, tourist attractions, and residential areas.\nBus 201.\nIt connects hospitals, shopping outlets, and entertainment districts.\nMetro Line 8 feeder buses.\nMetro Line 8\u2019s Lumiao Station serves as Zhongmu\u2019s central transportation hub, providing direct feeder buses to popular tourist sites:\nBy taxi.\nStarting fare: \u00a55 for the first 3 km. After 3 km: \u00a51.5 per km. Fares may slightly increase during holidays or after midnight. Most taxis accept cash or mobile payment. Alipay is strongly recommended because it allows simple onboarding for foreigners, and is widely accepted on buses, metro, and taxis.\nBy bicycle.\nCycling is a practical option due to Zhongmu\u2019s flat terrain and growing network of bike lanes. Shared bicycles such as Meituan and Hellobike can be found near bus stops, residential areas, and commercial streets.Bicycle rentals usually cost \u00a51\u20132 per 30 minutes. Popular cycling routes include:Old Town \u2192 Chenghuang Temple \u2192 Wenming Road.Wetland Belt \u2192 Yellow River ecological boardwalks. Agricultural Expo Park circuits.\nOn foot.\nWalking is convenient within the main urban core. Attractions such as the Confucius Temple, Guangwu Ancient City Ruins, and nearby markets are within comfortable walking distance.\nSleep.\nMid-range.\n | name=Aimei Hotel\n | alt=\u7231\u7f8e\u9152\u5e97\n | url=\n | email=\n | address=South Zhongmu Urban Area, near Xinyuan Road\n | lat=34.720950\n | long=113.931220\n | directions=\n | phone=+86 371 6083 9555\n | tollfree=\n | checkin=14:00\n | checkout=12:00\n | price=\u00a5280\n | lastedit=2025-11-27\n | content=A comfortable mid-range hotel with modern design and quiet surroundings, offering good value for business or leisure trips.\n | name=Jinjiang Metropolo Hotel Zhongmu\n | alt=\u9526\u6c5f\u90fd\u57ce\u4e2d\u725f\u5e97\n | url=\n | email=\n | address=268 Renmin Road, Zhongmu County\n | lat=34.731880\n | long=113.927340\n | directions=\n | phone=+86 371 6216 8899\n | tollfree=\n | checkin=14:00\n | checkout=12:00\n | price=\u00a5260\n | lastedit=2025-11-27\n | content=A clean and reliable mid-range hotel with convenient access to the town center, suitable for business and short-term stays.\nSplurge.\n | name=Zijin Jianye International Hotel \n | alt=\u7d2b\u8346\u5efa\u4e1a\u56fd\u9645\u9152\u5e97 \n | url=\n | email=\n | address=Wenchuang Road, Jianye Cultural Tourism Zone, Zhongmu County\n | lat=34.750820\n | long=113.969450\n | directions=\n | phone=+86 371 6218 8888\n | tollfree=\n | checkin=14:00\n | checkout=12:00\n | price=\u00a5480\n | lastedit=2025-11-27\n | content=A modern high-end business and leisure hotel near the Jianye cultural tourism area, with spacious rooms and easy access to Movie Town and the performing arts complex.\n | name=InterContinental Zhengzhou Green Expo\n | alt=\u90d1\u5dde\u7eff\u535a\u56ed\u6d32\u9645\u9152\u5e97\n | url=\n | email=\n | address=Inside Zhengzhou Green Expo Park, Zhongmu County\n | lat=34.771060\n | long=113.945210\n | directions=\n | phone=+86 371 6819 8888\n | tollfree=\n | checkin=14:00\n | checkout=12:00\n | price=\u00a5700\n | lastedit=2025-11-27\n | content=A premium international-standard hotel with excellent business facilities and wide views of the Green Expo landscape.\n | name=Movie Town Themed Hotel \n | alt=\u5efa\u4e1a\u534e\u8c0a\u5144\u5f1f\u7535\u5f71\u5c0f\u9547\u4e3b\u9898\u9152\u5e97\n | url=\n | email=\n | address=Inside Jianye Movie Town, East Section of Xiangyun Road, Zhongmu County\n | lat=34.744960\n | long=113.982300\n | directions=\n | phone=+86 371 6890 5566\n | tollfree=\n | checkin=14:00\n | checkout=12:00\n | price=\u00a5520\n | lastedit=2025-11-27\n | content=A boutique themed hotel inspired by cinematic and theatrical aesthetics, ideal for visitors exploring Movie Town."}
{"id": "213140", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213140", "title": "Yesan", "text": "Yesan-gun is a county in South Chungcheong Province, South Korea. It is a richly agricultural inland region, particularly famous for its high-quality produce, such as Yesan apples and scallions. The county is also known for its well-preserved historical sites and beautiful natural scenery, and offers a quiet and peaceful atmosphere.\nWhat sets Yesan-gun apart from other cities is the harmonious coexistence of tradition and nature. The area is represented by Buddhist heritage sites like Sudeoksa Temple, which boasts a long and proud history.\nUnderstand.\nYesan-gun is a mid-sized region in the northwestern part of South Chungcheong Province where you can enjoy the beauty of all four seasons. In spring, a beautiful natural landscape unfolds, centered around the Yedangho Suspension Bridge. In autumn, during the rich harvest season, festivals are held where you can taste the famous Yesan apples. It is a great place for those seeking a quiet trip and delicious local food.\nHistory.\nYesan\u2019s history traces back to the Baekje Kingdom as Osan-hyeon. It was renamed Gosan-hyeon during the Unified Silla period. In the early Goryeo Dynasty, the name was changed to Yesan-hyeon (\u79ae\u5c71\u7e23), a name it has retained for over 1,100 years. The county was reorganized into its modern form in 1914 by merging the former Yesan, Daeheung, and Deoksan counties. Today, Yesan is recognized for its historical heritage, including Sudeoksa Temple, and for hosting Naepo New City, the relocated capital of Chungcheongnam-do since 2012.\nClimate.\nSummers are characterized by extreme heat and high humidity due to the East Asian monsoon (Jangma), which concentrates heavy rainfall. The humidity level is particularly high in summer, even on non-rainy days. The most agreeable weather occurs during Spring and Autumn, which feature mild temperatures, low humidity, and clear skies, offering the best conditions for visitors. Winter is predominantly cold and dry.\nGet in.\nNearby cities include Asan and Gongju to the east, Seosan and Hongseong to the west, and Cheongyang to the south. Approximate travel times from other locations are as follows:\nBy train.\nYesan is on the Janghang Line.\nTravel times to Yesan Station:\nYesan County is served by six stations on the Janghang Line: Sinchang (shared with Seoul Subway Line 1), Hongseong, Sapgyo, Hanam, Yesan, and Ungcheon.\nYesan County is served by the Janghang Line, a major KORAIL train line connecting cities in the Chungcheong and Jeolla provinces. The main train station is in Yesan-eup, and it is a key transportation hub for the region.\nBy taxi.\nTaxi stands are usually near the bus stop.\nThe basic fare for a regular taxi is \u20a94,800 during the day and \u20a95,800 at night. A \u20a9100 surcharge is added based on time and distance. The basic fare covers up to 1.6 km (about 1 mile), with an additional \u20a9100 charged for every 135 meters after that.\nGet around.\nBy bus.\nThe bus system in Yesan-gun operates with local buses that connect various areas, primarily centered around the Yesan General Bus Terminal. Most bus routes start or end at the terminal, while some routes also depart from the Ssangsongbaegi area near the north gate of Yesan Market.\nMost major bus stops have well-maintained bus timetables, and some are equipped with amenities like heating/cooling systems or heated benches. However, there are not many English announcements or signs for tourists, so it's a good idea to confirm your route number and destination in advance.\nYesan bus routes by number\nAs of 2023, the adult fare with a transportation card is \u20a91,400."}
{"id": "213161", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213161", "title": "Batavia (Illinois)", "text": "Batavia is in the Fox River Valley.\nUnderstand.\nno population data for this page\nBatavia is part of \"the Tri-City area\", along with St. Charles and Geneva, all western suburbs of Chicago.\nFermi National Accelerator Laboratory, a federal government-sponsored high-energy physics laboratory, where both the bottom quark and the top quark were first detected, is located just east of the city limits.\nGet in.\nSome bus transportation is serviced by Pace.\nThe Geneva and Aurora Metra train stations are nearby. \nDo.\nPaths for biking and walking exist along the Fox River. "}
{"id": "213162", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213162", "title": "St. Charles (Illinois)", "text": "St. Charles is a city in the Fox River Valley.\nUnderstand.\nSt. Charles is part of a tri-city area along with Geneva and Batavia, all western Chicago suburbs. \nno population data for this page\nGet in.\nIt lies roughly 40 miles (64 km) west of Chicago on Illinois Route 64. "}
{"id": "213174", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213174", "title": "Voskopoja", "text": "Voskopoja (or Moscopole) is a village in Southeastern Albania, located in Kor\u00e7\u00eb County. Once a prominent urban center in the 18th century, it was a hub of Aromanian, Greek, and Albanian culture, featuring a printing press and numerous churches. Today, it stands as a quiet rural community with significant historical heritage.\nUnderstand.\nVoskopoja is a place for history enthusiasts and nature lovers alike. Once a major urban center in the 18th century, it was a hub of commerce and culture for the Aromanian, Greek, and Albanian communities. The village's golden age earned it the nickname \"New Athens\" for its vibrant intellectual life, which included a printing press and an academy. This history is etched into the stunning frescoes and architecture of its surviving churches.\nToday, Voskopoja offers a peaceful, mountainous escape from modern life. The atmosphere is serene and the pace is slow. The village is situated at an altitude of 1,160 meters, giving it a cool, comfortable summer and a long, snowy winter. Its location makes it ideal for hiking in the warmer months and a potential destination for winter sports.\nThe village's decline began with a series of raids in the late 18th century. Many inhabitants fled, with some settling in nearby Kor\u00e7\u00eb, about 21 km away, creating a strong historical link between the two areas.\nThe administrative unit of Voskopoja includes the main village and smaller hamlets like Shipska, Krushova, and Lavdari. While Voskopoja village itself is the main point of interest with its historic churches and guesthouses, the other hamlets offer additional sites and trails for those looking to explore the wider rural landscape.\nGet in.\nVoskopoja is most easily reached from the city of Kor\u00e7\u00eb.\nSee.\nThe main attractions in Voskopoja are its historical churches, many of which are adorned with beautiful post-Byzantine frescoes.\nItineraries.\nA walk through history: Start your day at the main square (Old Town Hall Square). From there, take a short walk to the Church of Saint Nicholas to admire its famous frescoes. Then, follow the signposts or a local's directions for an uphill walk to the Church of Saint Athanasius, which offers panoramic views of the entire area. Afterwards, head to the nearby Church of the Dormition of the Theotokos to complete your tour of the central churches. For the afternoon, consider a short drive or a longer walk to the Monastery of Saint John the Baptist located just outside the village to explore its tranquil grounds. The entire route can be completed at a leisurely pace in one day.\nNature and culture trip: Combine the historical tour with some light hiking. After visiting the central churches, take one of the walking trails that lead to the surrounding hills and nearby hamlets like Shipska. These trails offer a chance to see the rural landscape and discover other smaller, less-frequented churches and historical sites in a peaceful, natural setting.\nBuy.\nVoskopoja is a small village with a limited number of shops. There are no major commercial districts, but you can find small local stores that sell basic goods. The best things to buy are locally-produced products and artisanal goods.\nIt is advisable to carry cash (Albanian Lek) as credit cards are not widely accepted. There are no ATMs in Voskopoja, so be sure to withdraw cash in Kor\u00e7\u00eb before you arrive.\nEat.\nVoskopoja is known for its traditional Albanian mountain cuisine, with an emphasis on fresh, locally-sourced ingredients. Many of the restaurants and guesthouses serve classic dishes, and the atmosphere is typically cozy and rustic.\nMost restaurants are found within the guesthouses or as standalone family-run establishments. You can usually find a place to eat by walking along the main street.\nDrink.\nVoskopoja is not a nightlife destination. The village is quiet after dark, and the \"drink\" scene is centered around relaxing in a guesthouse or restaurant with a good meal and a glass of local wine or \"raki\".\nSleep.\nAccommodation in Voskopoja consists primarily of family-run guesthouses and a few small hotels. Booking in advance is recommended, especially during the summer and winter holidays, as the number of available rooms is limited. The guesthouses often offer a full package, including meals.\nStay safe.\nVoskopoja is a very safe place with a low crime rate. The main hazards are related to the natural environment."}
{"id": "213175", "revid": "898440", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213175", "title": "Nan'ao Island", "text": "Nan'ao Island (\u5357\u6fb3\u5c9b) is in Guangdong Province, China. An inhabited island off the coast of Shantou in eastern Guangdong, Nan'ao is a popular side trip for beaches, sunrise viewpoints (especially around Qing'ao Bay), coastal drives, and seafood.\nUnderstand.\nMost visitor services (banks, supermarkets, many hotels and restaurants) are concentrated in Houzhai Town, with smaller villages and coves scattered around the ring road.\nIt is administered as part of Nan'ao County in Shantou City. It is the only island in Nan'ao County with residents.\nClimate.\nNan'ao Island lies on the Tropic of Cancer and has a subtropical monsoon marine climate. Summers are long, hot, humid and very sunny, with most of the island\u2019s heavy rainfall and occasional typhoons arriving from April to September. Winters are short and mild, and daytime temperatures usually stay well above 10\u00b0C, though damp northeasterly winds can make it feel cooler. Thanks to the surrounding sea, temperatures are a little cooler than inland Shantou in summer and slightly warmer in winter. The most pleasant seasons for outdoor activities are spring (March\u2013May) and autumn (September\u2013November), when the weather is generally warm, dry and clear.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nMost visitors arrive via the Nan'ao Bridge. The bridge is tolled: for small passenger cars (Class 1) the fee is \u00a548 per crossing, collected as a round-trip charge when entering the island. Have cash or ETC available, and expect slower traffic on weekends and holidays.\nBy boat.\nPassenger ferries between the mainland and the island still operate on some routes, but schedules can be limited and may change with weather. Check locally or through official notices before planning around the ferry.\nA passenger ferry operates between Laiwu (mainland) and Changshanwei (on the island). Service can be limited on ordinary days, but extra sailings are sometimes added during major holidays (weather permitting). For the latest schedule and ticketing rules, call the ferry information line (+86 754 8550 0214) before you go.\nBy bus.\nDirect buses connect Shantou with Nan'ao. Route 161 runs between Shantou (Qishan Coach Station) and Nan'ao County Town (Houzhai), with the first buses typically around 06:30\u201307:00 and later last buses in summer than winter. The full fare is about \u00a514 (cash), with cheaper short-hop segments.\nGet around.\nOn foot.\nDue to the considerable distances between the main attractions, it is not recommended to explore the entire island entirely on foot. Some hiking trails and mountaintop viewpoints still require other means of transportation to reach.\nBy car.\nDriving is the easiest way to cover the island, especially for remote coves and viewpoints. Car rentals are available in Houzhai Town, but options can be limited in peak season, so booking ahead is recommended.\nBy bus.\nLocal buses run in loops around the island:\nAsk at the stop for the current timetable, as service frequency varies by season.\nDrink.\nMost places focus on food rather than nightlife. For a wider range of bars and caf\u00e9s, consider spending an evening in Shantou.\nSleep.\nCamping.\nCamping is permitted in designated areas of beaches like Qing'ao Bay, where you can pitch your own or a rented tent. Always check weather and tide forecasts beforehand, set up camp well above the high-tide line, and follow strict leave-no-trace camping principles. \nFor wilderness hiking in areas like Huanghuashan Forest Park or remote coves, it is strongly advised to travel with a partner, carry sufficient water and communication devices, and inform others of your planned route.\nConnect.\nMobile coverage is generally good in towns and around major beaches, but it can be patchy in remote coves and on mountain roads. Many hotels and restaurants offer Wi-Fi."}
{"id": "213186", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213186", "title": "Wheeling (Illinois)", "text": "Wheeling is a village in the Northwest Suburbs of Chicago. no population data for this page.\nUnderstand.\nWheeling is named after Wheeling, West Virginia.\nGet in.\nIt is approximately 23 mi (37 km) northwest of downtown Chicago."}
{"id": "213199", "revid": "2409164", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213199", "title": "British Florida", "text": "Although Florida was under Spanish control for most of its history prior to U.S. control, there was a brief period of 20 years during which it was a British colony. During British rule in the region, it was divided into two colonies: East Florida governed from St. Augustine, and West Florida governed from Pensacola. This arrangement continued after 1783, when Florida was returned to the Spanish Empire, and concluded in 1821, when Florida became part of the United States.\nUnderstand.\nFollowing Britain's successful siege of Havana in 1762, a deal was struck between Britain and Spain. Cuba would be returned to the Spanish in exchange for British control over Florida, which would last for 20 years. Britain's first move was to divide the colony into West and East Florida, with a new capital established at the Spanish settlement of Pensacola for the West.\nBritish colonists established slave plantation and indentured worker colonies in East Florida. The largest and most infamous of these, Smyrnea, was established by Robert Turnbull and used indentured labor for about ten years before the workers revolted and fled north to St. Augustine. Other colonies were established in Ormond Beach and across Inland Florida, but these were generally unsuccessful, as slaves and other workers could escape to virtually anywhere.\nThe outbreak of rebellion and then the War of Independence in the Thirteen Colonies undermined British attempts to develop Florida. Florida remained a frontier colony outside its two capitals, and it was likely viewed by Britain as a trade-able asset after the War of Independence and the war against the French were over. The Spanish went to war against Britain in the late stages of the War of Independence and successfully laid siege to Pensacola. The last major action by loyalist forces in the war was taken when Colonel Andrew Deveaux led a force from St. Augustine to successfully capture the Bahamas from Spain.\nAt the Treaty of Paris, Britain \u2014 having lost control of the Thirteen Colonies to the north \u2014 agreed to return Florida to Spain. The Spanish maintained the division between the two capitals, and after a brief period of development and relative prosperity in the colonies, Spain handed the two Floridas to the United States, in exchange to the latter renouncing all its claims to Texas and agreed to pay $5 million of Spanish debts to American citizens.\nFurther reading.\nThe Florida Department of State wrote a guidebook, \"British Heritage Trail\", which details colonial British sites across Florida.\nHistoric sites.\nAn explorer and writer, William Bartram, traveled across Florida during the British colonial era and wrote his famous book \"Travels\" based on what he saw across the South. His route has been named the Bartram Trail and informative plaques have been placed along his route by the Bartram Trail Society of Florida.\nIn St. Augustine, several Spanish houses built during the first Spanish period were occupied or altered during the British period. The Governor's House and the Pe\u00f1a-Peck House were both homes of British colonial governors. Other structures are of uncertain construction date or were demolished and reconstructed in the mid-20th century. The only known structure built during the British period in St. Augustine is the King's Bakery."}
{"id": "213210", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213210", "title": "Sineu", "text": "Sineu is a town in Pla de Mallorca, the central region of Mallorca. In 2024 it had a population of 4500.\nGet in.\nSineu is 33 km east of Palma, follow Ma-3011.\nTIB buses from Palma take 35 min to Algaida, where they connect with minibus 432 to Pina, Lloret and Sineu (another 25 min).\nBus 441 runs every couple of hours between Ariany, Maria de Salut and Sineu.\nBus 442 runs every couple of hours from Vilafranca via Sant Joan.\nTrain T3 runs hourly from Palma, taking 50 min via Inca. It continues to Petra then Manacor, another 15 min. \n for buses and trains is east side of town.\nGet around.\nTown is compact, but you need wheels for outlying sights.\nEat.\nA dozen restaurants cluster around the main square.\nDrink.\nBars as above. Central Mallorca is a wine-growing region.\nConnect.\nAs of Sep 2025, Sineu and its approach roads have 4G from Vodafone, and 5G from MasMovil, Movistar and Orange."}
{"id": "213213", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213213", "title": "Hallein", "text": "Hallein is in Salzburg, Austria with 19,000 inhabitants. It is the main town of the Tennengau region. There is a spa in Bad D\u00fcrrnberg.\nUnderstand.\nThe area around the town (D\u00fcrrnberg) has been settled since around 2500 BC, and underground salt mining began around 600 BC. The salt mine was in operation until 1989. \nGet in.\nBy car.\nHallein is about 20 \u202fkm south of Salzburg on the Tauern Autobahn. \nBy rail.\nHallein has 2 stations on the Salzburg S-Bahn.\nBy plane.\nHallein is a short distance from Salzburg Airport.\nGet around.\nThe regional bus lines operate within the Salzburg Transport Association. \nDo.\nOn the D\u00fcrrnberg hill you'll find a summer toboggan run.\nDrink.\nAlmost all of the nightlife takes place in Hallein's city centre (Griestorgasse, Bayrhamerplatz, Kuffergasse), only later in the evening does the nightlife shift sporadically towards Neualm, to the discotheques located there.\nEspecially in summer, the outdoor dining areas attract numerous visitors to the various restaurants located in the heart of the historic town center. These outdoor dining areas close between 23:00 and 23:30 (according to the outdoor dining regulations), and indoor dining also closes around 02:00, with only a few exceptions. For night owls, there are two alternatives: either head towards Neualm, where the restaurants stay open until the early hours, or travel to Salzburg. However, the latter is not recommended at such a late hour; it's better to take a train before 23:00 to reach Salzburg.\nIn general, the range of venues in the old town lies between cozy chill-out bars and packed venues."}
{"id": "213223", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213223", "title": "Jiyuan", "text": "Jiyuan (\u6d4e\u6e90, J\u00ecyu\u00e1n) is in North Henan.\nUnderstand.\nJiyuan is a county-level city under the jurisdiction of Henan Province, China, located at the southern foot of the Taihang Mountains and the middle reaches of the Yellow River. Named after the \"source of the Ji River,\" Jiyuan is the site of the ancient Chinese myth \"Foolish Old Man Who Moved the Mountains.\"\nDo.\nMountain climbing and nature exploration:\nCultural experiences:"}
{"id": "213224", "revid": "2388049", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213224", "title": "Jiaozuo", "text": "Jiaozuo (\u7126\u4f5c Ji\u0101ozu\u00f2) is a city in northwestern Henan, covering over 4,000 km\u00b2and home to around 3.5 million people.\nIts role as a regional transport hub means it's easily accessible, with frequent high-speed rail connections from Zhengzhou, making it a convenient base for travelers.\nBeyond its natural wonders, Jiaozuo is steeped in history. The area has been inhabited since ancient times, and it was the hometown of notable figures like Sima Yi, the master strategist of the Three Kingdoms, and Han Yu, a leading Tang dynasty literary figure. The city also holds a place in Chinese lore as the gathering place of the free-spirited \"Seven Sages of the Bamboo Grove\" and the final retreat of Emperor Xian of Han.\nUnderstand.\nRegions.\nJiaozuo administers 4 districts, 2 county-level cities, and 4 counties:\nClimate.\nJiaozuo enjoys a climate of distinct seasons, characterized by its annual average temperature of around 14\u00b0C and precipitation of 600-700 mm. This translates to hot, rainy summers and cold, relatively dry winters\u2014best classified as a warm temperate continental monsoon climate.\nHistory.\nJiaozuo has a long and storied history, known in ancient times as \"Shanyang(\u5c71\u9633)\" As one of the core areas of early Chinese civilization, it is the proud hometown of illustrious figures such as the master strategist Sima Yi and the literary giants Han Yu and Li Shangyin.\nThe region's coal resources were first tapped during the Ming and Qing dynasties. Its modern identity, however, was forged in the late 19th and early 20th centuries with the establishment of the British-owned Yingfu Company, which industrialized mining operations. This period was so defining that it gave the city its present name, \"Jiaozuo,\" which literally reflects its role in coal mining. It became one of the birthplaces of China's modern coal industry.\nFacing the gradual depletion of coal reserves since the 1990s, Jiaozuo has successfully undertaken a remarkable economic transition. This shift has transformed its reputation from an industrial center into a celebrated region of landscape and culture.\nGet in.\nBy train.\nJiaozuo is well-connected by rail and has multiple train stations.\nGet around.\nBy bus.\nJiaozuo's city bus network offers extensive coverage, reaching most urban areas and some nearby attractions. \nBy taxi.\nTaxis and ride-hailing services are a very convenient way to get around within the city.\nBy car.\nRenting a car is the most flexible option for travelers planning to explore the various sites of the Taihang Mountains(\u592a\u884c\u5c71\u8109) in depth. Jiaozuo has a well-developed road network with good conditions on the routes leading to major scenic spots.\nSee.\nJiaozuo is located in northern Henan Province, at the confluence of the Taihang Mountains and the Yellow River alluvial plain., and renowned for magnificent landscapes of the southern Taihang Mountains and its profound historical and cultural heritage. The following are the main attractions located in Jiaozuo:\nLearn.\nJiaozuo is home to several higher education institutions spanning fields like engineering, transportation, and teacher training. If you're interested in the local academic atmosphere or campus culture, consider visiting these key schools.\nWork.\nJiaozuo is not a major destination for foreign employment. Job opportunities are primarily found in local enterprises, the tourism-related service industry, and some manufacturing sectors. For travelers, long-term work options here are relatively limited.\nEat.\nJiaozuo's food culture blends elements of Henan cuisine with local traditions, featuring hearty noodles and robust flavors. Popular local dishes include:\nSome restaurants feature specific local dishes.\nDrink.\nJiaozuo has a strong local drinking culture, where baijiu (a clear spirit) is commonly consumed. For visitors, local beer brands and soft drinks are widely available in restaurants. Teahouses are also relatively common in the city, offering a quieter alternative.\nSleep.\nJiaozuo offers diverse accommodation options."}
{"id": "213225", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213225", "title": "Huainan", "text": "Huainan is a prefecture-level city, located in the north-central part of Anhui. It is a traditional heavy industry city, with main industries in coal, chemicals, electricity, and pharmaceuticals.\nUnderstand.\nThe city administers 5 urban districts, 2 counties, and 1 county-level administrative area:\nEat.\nHuainan Beef Soup (\u6dee\u5357\u725b\u8089\u6c64), Found in many local breakfast shops throughout Huainan. 06:30\u201310:30. A signature dish of Huainan made from slow-cooked beef bones, giving the broth a milky white color and deep flavor. Commonly served with fried dough sticks or pancakes. Around \u00a515 per bowl. \nHuainan Steamed Dishes (\u6dee\u5357\u84b8\u83dc), Available in local family restaurants. 11:00\u201321:00. Various meats, tofu, and vegetables are steamed together in bamboo baskets, preserving their natural taste and nutrition. A light and healthy local favorite. \u00a525\u201340 per person. \nHuainan Roast Chicken (\u6dee\u5357\u70e7\u9e21), Wenfeng Street, Tianjia\u2019an District. 10:00\u201322:00. A famous traditional delicacy with crispy skin and tender meat, marinated in secret spices. Often sold as a whole chicken for take-away. Around \u00a550 each. \nHuainan Tofu Skin (\u6dee\u5357\u8c46\u8150\u76ae), Available in night markets and snack streets such as People\u2019s Road. 17:00\u201323:00. Soft and chewy tofu skin made from soybeans, usually mixed with chili oil, vinegar, and garlic for a refreshing street snack. \u00a58\u201312 per portion. \nHuainan Fried Rice Noodles (\u6dee\u5357\u7092\u7c73\u7c89), Local eateries near Shungeng Road and Xinjian Avenue. 11:00\u201321:00. Rice noodles stir-fried with vegetables, pork, and spicy sauce, giving a rich aroma and chewy texture. Around \u00a520. "}
{"id": "213226", "revid": "2441460", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213226", "title": "Pingshan", "text": "Pingshan (\u5c4f\u5c71\uff09is a city in Sichuan Province, China. It is known for its beautiful mountains and hot springs.\nUnderstand.\nPingshan has a long history and rich culture. Visitors can experience traditional festivals, local cuisine, and historic sites. The city combines urban areas with surrounding rural landscapes, offering a glimpse of local life.\nGet in.\nBy Air: The nearest airport is Yibin Wuliangye Airport, with bus or taxi connections to Pingshan.\nBy Train: Pingshan can be reached via Yibin West Railway Station, which has regular services to major cities.\nBy Bus: Long-distance buses connect Pingshan with Chengdu, Chongqing, and neighboring towns."}
{"id": "213227", "revid": "2248002", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213227", "title": "Yuseong-gu", "text": " (\uc720\uc131\uad6c, \u5112\u57ce\u5340) is the largest district in Daejeon. Yuseong-gu is best known as the place of Korea Advanced Institute of Science and Technology (KAIST), one of the country's top research universities, and the Yuseong Hot Springs Resort, a historic spa area popular with both locals and tourists.\nUnderstand.\nYuseong-gu is one of the five districts of Daejeon and serves as the city\u2019s main hub for science and research. The district is the largest in Daejeon, and is the 2nd most populated district. It is home to KAIST (Korea Advanced Institute of Science and Technology) and several other research institutes clustered in the Daedeok Innopolis area, which has been called the \u201cKorean Silicon Valley.\u201d\nApart from its modern reputation, Yuseong has long been known for its natural hot springs. The Yuseong Hot Springs has selected as national special tourist zone on 1993, and the area still attracts visitors seeking relaxation in public spas and resorts.\nThe district combines a youthful university-town atmosphere with traditional Korean spa culture, offering travelers both a modern and historic side of Daejeon in one place.\nGet in.\nYuseong-gu is easily accessible from central Daejeon and other parts of Korea. Most visitors arrive through Daejeon station and then transfer to the local subway or bus network.\nBy train\nThe nearest KTX stop is Daejeon Station, located in the city center. From there, take Daejeon Subway Line 1 westbound to reach Yuseong-gu (about 25 minutes to Yuseong Spa Station).\nBy bus\nLong-distance express buses arrive at the Daejeon Government Complex Bus Terminal or Daejeon Express Bus Terminal. From either terminal, the subway Line 1 connects directly to Yuseong-gu.\nBy air\nThe nearest airport is Cheongju International Airport (CJJ), about 50 km north of Daejeon. Limousine buses and trains link the airport to the city. Incheon International Airport is also reachable within 2 hours by KTX or intercity bus(the ride takes 3 hr 15 min)."}
{"id": "213229", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213229", "title": "Gongyi", "text": "Gongyi is a county-level city under the jurisdiction of Zhengzhou, Central Henan.\nUnderstand.\nGongyi\uff08\u5de9\u4e49\uff09 is a county-level city under the jurisdiction of Zhengzhou, Henan Province. It is located in central China, historically known for its long history and proximity to the famous Songshan Mountains. The city has a mix of cultural heritage and modern development, making it a convenient stop for travelers interested in history and natural scenery.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest major airport is Zhengzhou Xinzheng International Airport (about 60 km away).\nBy train.\nGongyi has its own railway station with high-speed and regular trains connecting to Zhengzhou, Luoyang, and other major cities.\nBy bus.\nLong-distance buses connect Gongyi with Zhengzhou and surrounding cities."}
{"id": "213297", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213297", "title": "Sedan (France)", "text": "Sedan is a sub-prefecture and the second most populous city in the Champagne-Ardenne region in France: no population data for this page. Sedan is known for its castle, which is claimed to be the largest fortified medieval castle in Europe.\nUnderstand.\nOrigins.\nSedan has been occupied since the Palaeolithic period. The first mentions of the city date back to around 997, as a document attests to the existence of a \"villa sedensis\". Two hamlets were at the modern centre of the town: (1) Villers (which can be located near the castle) and (2) M\u00e9nil (or Mesnil, certainly located at the level of the current Place Nassau). These two hamlets were separated by swamps, which were filled in at the request of Henri de la Tour d'Auvergne to unify the two villages. Early on, Sedan was under the influence of the abbey of Mouzon. Around 1306, a priory was built on the rocky spur overlooking the town. In 1424, Louis de Braquemont, then the owner of the lands of Sedan and Florenville, sold them to Evrard II de la Marck-Aremberg, originally from Westphalia, to settle his personal debts. Ervard then expelled the monks from Mouzon and began to build a castle, which still makes the town famous today.\nThrough the expulsion of the monks of Mouzon and the building of the Ch\u00e2teau de Sedan in 1424, the lords of Sedan gradually became masters of the city. The following century saw the creation and development of the independent Principality of Sedan, which at its height had nearly twenty towns and villages in addition to Sedan. This apogee came to an end in the 17th century when King Louis XIII learned that Fr\u00e9d\u00e9ric-Maurice de La Tour d'Auvergne had participated in a conspiracy against him. Fr\u00e9d\u00e9ric-Maurice asked for and obtained the help of the Holy Roman Empire. Both sides came to a clash at the Battle of the Marf\u00e9e. which took place on 6 July 1641 on the heights of Sedan. Fr\u00e9d\u00e9ric-Maurice de La Tour d'Auvergne was forced to submit to the King of France following a siege of the city by the royal army. Sedan was then subsequently annexed into the kingdom of France in September 1642.\nThe Battles of Sedan.\nSedan was the scene of resounding defeats by the French army on several occasions:\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThere is no airport in the Ardennes; the closest one is in Luxembourg, 135 kilometres away. In France, the closest is Nancy-Metz, 210 km away.\nBy car.\nSedan is connected to the motorway network by the eastern section of the French motorway A34. This allows you to go to Charleville-M\u00e9zi\u00e8res, then Reims and Paris, but also to Metz, if you continue east of the city to Bazeilles, then on the French national road 43, and to Bouillon (Belgium) on the French national road 58."}
{"id": "213299", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213299", "title": "Shopping in Singapore", "text": "Shopping is second only to eating as a national pastime, which means that Singapore has an \"abundance\" of shopping malls, and low taxes and tariffs on imports coupled with huge volume mean that prices are usually very competitive. \nWhile you won't find any bazaars with dirt-cheap local handicrafts (in fact, virtually everything sold in Singapore is made elsewhere), goods are generally of reasonably good quality and shopkeepers are generally quite honest due to strong consumer protection laws. \nWhere to shop.\nMost residential neighbourhoods in Singapore have at least one shopping mall, often next to a bus interchange or MRT station, but these are primarily designed to cater to the basic needs of local residents, and are primarily home to F&amp;B outlets, supermarkets, banks and some mid-range clothing stores. \nThe malls at Orchard Road tend to have a wider array of options, including luxury brands that you will not find at the neighbourhood shopping malls. Orchard Road is Singaporean consumerism at its most manic, a two-kilometre stretch of interconnected shopping malls, hotels, and hotels with shopping malls catering to all budgets and tastes.\nShopping hours.\nMost stores are open daily 10AM-10PM, although smaller operations (particularly those outside shopping malls) close earlier \u2014 7PM is common \u2014 and perhaps on Sundays as well. Mustafa in Little India is open until 2 AM every day, 365 days a year.\nGST refund.\nFor purchases of over $100 per day per participating shop, you may be able to get a GST refund if you depart by air within two months of making the purchase, but the process is a bit of a bureaucratic hassle. At the shop you need to ask for a tax refund cheque. Before checking in at the airport, present this cheque together with the items purchased and your passport at the GST customs counter. Get the receipt stamped there. Then proceed with check-in and go through security. After clearing departure immigration, bring the stamped cheque to the refund counter to cash it in or get the GST back on your credit card. You must be visiting Singapore as a tourist, so foreigners working or studying in Singapore, as well as people departing Singapore as airline crew, are ineligible. See Singapore Customs for the full scoop. \"No\" GST refund is available if you depart by land or sea.\nWhat to buy.\nAntiques.\nThe second floor of the Tanglin Shopping Centre on Orchard and the shops on South Bridge Rd in Chinatown are good options if looking for the real thing (or high-quality reproductions).\nBooks.\nKinokuniya's main branch at Ngee Ann City in Orchard is Singapore's largest bookstore, with a smaller branch at Bugis Junction (Bugis MRT station). Many second-hand book stores are in Far East Plaza and Bras Basah Complex, where you may attempt to bargain if you are buying a lot. For university textbooks, the bookshops at the National University of Singapore have the best prices on the island, up to 80% off compared to prices in the West. \nCameras.\nPeninsula Plaza near City Hall has Singapore's widest selection of camera shops. However, there are no great bargains to be had, and many camera stores in Singapore (particularly those in Lucky Plaza and Sim Lim Square) have a reputation for fleecing even the most careful tourists. The best way is to know \"exactly\" what you are looking for and then when you arrive, drop by the shops at the airport's transit area and take a look at the price and check with them whether they have any promotions. Then go to the downtown shops and compare prices/packages to see which shop will give you value for money. To be safe, \"always\" check prices and packages for everything you're interested in at large retailers like Courts, Harvey Norman and Best Denki first. Be very careful when shop staff attempt to promote brands or models other than the one you have in mind; a few stores at Sim Lim Square, Lucky Plaza, and elsewhere are known to use this tactic and sell products at two to four times their actual list prices. Also watch for the bait-and-switches. Inspect the model number and condition of the item, then do not let it out of your sight when you pay. (In Lucky Plaza, the most common scam is doubling the charge without your agreement.)\nClothes.\nHigh-street clothing: The Shoppes at Marina Bay Sands, Resorts World Sentosa as well as Ion Orchard, Ngee Ann City (Takashimaya) and Paragon on Orchard and have the heaviest concentration of branded boutiques.\nTailored clothing: Virtually all hotels have a tailor shop attached, and touting tailors are a bit of a nuisance in Chinatown. As elsewhere, you'll get what you pay for and will get poor quality if you don't have the time for multiple fittings or the skill to check what you're getting. Prices vary widely: a local shop using cheap fabrics can do a shirt for $40, while Lee Kuan Yew's favourite tailors CYC the Custom Shop will charge at least $120.\nYouth clothing: Most of Bugis is dedicated to the young, hip and cost-conscious. Bugis St (opposite Bugis MRT) is the most popular in the Bugis area, consisting of 3 levels of shops. Some spots of Orchard, notably Far East Plaza (not to be confused with Far East Shopping Centre) and the top floor of the Heeren, also target the same market but prices are generally higher.\nComputers.\nSim Lim Square (near Little India) is great for the hardcore geek who really knows what he's after - parts price lists are available on HardwareZone.com and are given out in Sim Lim itself, making price comparison easy. Lesser mortals (namely, those who have failed to do their price-checking homework) stand a risk of getting ripped off when purchasing, but this is generally not a problem with the price lists offered by most shops. Some Singaporeans purchase their electronic gadgets during the quarterly \"IT shows\" usually held at Suntec City Convention Centre or at the Expo, at which prices on gadgets are sometimes slashed (but often only to Sim Lim levels). Do not be attracted by side gifts/sweeteners of thumb drives, mice and so on; these only tend to hide inflated prices. If you just need a cable or new mouse in a hurry, Challenger stores can be found in most shopping malls.\nConsumer electronics.\nSingapore used to be known for good prices, but nowadays electronics here are generally more expensive than from US and international online vendors. Funan (Riverside) and Mustafa (Little India) are good choices. \"Avoid\" the tourist-oriented shops on Orchard Road, particularly the notorious Lucky Plaza, or risk getting ripped off. Also take great care to be wary of shops on the 1st and 2nd levels of Sim Lim Square, some of which tend to rip off tourists and locals alike by overcharging by 100% or more, adding on ludicrous charges beyond what was agreed on, swapping items for used ones, leaving out cases and batteries, and a host of other practices that should be (or are) criminal. \nDo your research before buying electronics from \"any\" store in Singapore; online research and multi-shop price comparison (and bargaining, occasionally) are essential. Mustafa has fixed, fair prices and is a good option, and so are Challenger and other large fixed-price retailers. For any purchases, remember that Singapore uses 230 V voltage at 50 Hz with a British-style, three-pin plug.\nElectronic components.\nFor do-it-yourself people and engineers, a wide variety of electronic components and associated tools can be found at Sim Lim Tower (opposite Sim Lim Square), near Little India. You can find most common electronic components (such as breadboards, transistors, various ICs, etc.) and bargain prices for larger quantities as well.\nEthnic knick-knacks.\nChinatown has Singapore's heaviest concentration of glow-in-the-dark Merlion soap dispensers and ethnic souvenirs, mostly but not entirely Chinese and nearly all imported from somewhere else. For Malay and Indian stuff, the best places to shop are Geylang Serai and Little India respectively.\nFabrics.\nArab Street and Little India have a good selection of imported and local fabrics like \"batik\". Chinatown does sell rather reasonable and cheap fabrics, bargaining is allowed so do know your stuff on what fabric to buy. Fabrics in Singapore may not be as cheap as overseas, for most fabrics are imported to Singapore.\nFakes.\nUnlike most Southeast Asian countries, pirated goods are not openly on sale and importing them to the city-state carries heavy fines due to its strong stance on intellectual property (IP) protection. Fake goods are nevertheless not difficult to find in Little India or Bugis.\nFood.\nLocal supermarkets Cold Storage, Prime Mart, Shop 'n' Save and NTUC Fairprice are ubiquitous, but for specialities, Jason's Marketplace in the basement of Raffles City and Tanglin Market Place at Tanglin Mall (both on Orchard) are some of Singapore's best-stocked gourmet supermarkets, with a vast array of imported products. Takashimaya's basement (Orchard) has lots of small quirky shops and makes for a more interesting browse, while the Little Farms chain is all-organic and priced to match. For a more Singaporean (and much cheaper) shopping experience, seek out any neighbourhood wet market, like Little India's Tekka Market.\nMarine sports.\nMany of the shophouses opposite The Concourse on Beach Rd in Bugis sell fishing and scuba diving gear.\nPeranakan goods.\nThe \"Peranakan\", or Malay-Chinese, may be fading but their colourful clothing and artwork, especially the distinctive pastel-coloured ceramics, are still widely available. Antiques are expensive, but modern replicas are quite affordable. The largest selection and best prices can be found in Katong on the East Coast.\nSouvenirs.\nFashion label Charles &amp; Keith (started out as Shoe Heaven), has got you covered if you're looking for a pair of perfect shoes &amp; has evolved into handbags &amp; accessories. Grab the mini Singapore sling cocktail set at Raffles Hotel or Changi Airport for the true heritage flavour. With their luxurious gold plating technology, RISIS provides beautiful gifts like gold-plated Orchids and brooches.\nOne of the popular snack souvenirs - Bak Kwa from Bee Cheng Hiang (Smoked Barbecue Pork) is a well-loved snack by Chinese tourists, though most locals prefer the version from Lim Chee Guan, which has extremely long queues lasting several hours over the Chinese New Year period. Kaya is a savoury coconut milk, eggs, and sugar, usually spread on toast where locals consume for their breakfast. Depending on the brand, it can taste rich &amp; sweet to having a light pandan flavour. Ya Kun Kaya is readily available in their nationwide outlets and Changi Airport.\nA must-get, Chilli crab &amp; Laksa sauce kits from Prima Taste are also saliva-inducing souvenirs available to purchase at supermarkets. These are Halal.\nBak Kut Teh (literally translated as Meat Bone Tea) Spices are also a fine choice to bring back a taste of Singapore, such as A1 Bak Kut Teh. \nLocal Designers like Supermama have also came up with Singaporean omiyage (contemporary giftware) ranging from porcelain tableware to quirky socks. Most of these souvenirs can be found in their own store outlets, or Changi Airport.\nCat Socrates is a quirky bookstore that sells Singaporean literature as well as postcards, stationery and trinkets with Singaporean motifs made by local artists.\nSporting goods.\nQueensway Shopping Centre, off Alexandra Rd and rather off the beaten track (take a taxi), seems to consist of nothing but sporting goods shops. You can also find foreigner-sized sporty clothing and shoes here. Do bargain! Expect to get 40-50% off the price from the shops in Orchard for the same items, as the items here are parallel-imported. Velocity in Novena is also devoted to sports goods, but is rather more upmarket. Martial arts equipment is surprisingly hard to find, although most of the clothing shops around Pagoda Street in Chinatown sell basic silk taiji/wushu uniforms. If you plan to buy weapons such as swords, you have to apply for a permit from the police (around $10) to get your weaponry out of the country.\nTea.\nChinatown's Yue Hwa (2nd floor) is unbeatable for both price and variety, but Time for Tea in Lucky Plaza (Orchard) is also a good option. Local brand TWG has glittery gold-bedecked branches throughout the island aimed squarely at the luxury gift and souvenir market.\nVideo Games.\nVideo games for PC and game consoles are widely available, but prices may not be cheaper than in the West. Games sold for the local market are generally in English, though some games imported from Hong Kong or Taiwan will be in Chinese. Singapore's region code is NTSC-J (together with Japan, South Korea, Taiwan, Hong Kong, etc): games sold may not be compatible with consoles in mainland China, North America, Europe or Australia. During the IT Shows, which happen four times in a year, Xbox, PlayStation, Nintendo and PC games prices may drop; if not, the games will be bundled with others (example: buy 2 at $49.90). Search for reputable shops online and avoid Sim Lim Square's first two floors, as always.\nWatches.\nHigh-end watches are very competitively priced. Ngee Ann City (Orchard) has dedicated stores from the likes of Piaget and Cartier, while Millenia Walk (Marina Bay) features the Cortina Watch Espace retailing 30 brands from Audemars Piguet to Patek Philippe, as well as several other standalone shops."}
