{"id": "213511", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213511", "title": "Woodburn (New South Wales)", "text": "Woodburn is a small riverside town that sits along the Richmond River in the Northern Rivers region of New South Wales. It used to sit along the Pacific Highway between Sydney and Brisbane, but its importance significantly dwindled when a motorway was built to bypass the town in 2020. Today, considerably fewer people pass through the town, primarily those headed to Evans Head, some of whom stop for a riverside stroll.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nWoodburn is a few kilometres off the M1 Pacific Motorway: 42 km south of Ballina and 53 km north of Maclean.\nFrom Lismore, use Tourist Drive 24 (Wyrallah Road) south.\nBy coach and bus.\nThere is one daily coach between Grafton and Byron Bay in each direction, connecting to the Casino/Brisbane XPT. The coach headed north to Byron Bay leaves Grafton in the afternoon and returns back the next morning (leaving Woodburn at 6:55AM).\nLocal bus services exist from Evans Head, Ballina, Lismore and Grafton."}
{"id": "213521", "revid": "1087011", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213521", "title": "Bodinga", "text": "Bodinga is a Local Government Area in Sokoto State, Northwestern Nigeria. It is known for its traditional culture, markets and closeness to Sokoto city.\nUnderstand.\nBodinga is mostly home to Hausa and Fulani people. The town shows Hausa\u2013Fulani way of life, with Islam, farming and trading. Most people are farmers, growing millet, sorghum and rice.\nGet in.\nBy road.\nBodinga is about 20 to 30 km from Sokoto city. You can go there by car, taxi, or motorcycle. The main road from Sokoto to Gusau passes through Bodinga.\nBy plane.\nYou can get to Bodinga from Sokoto International Airport by road. The town is about 30 to 40 minutes away. Taxis and cars are the main way to travel from the airport to Bodinga.\nSafe.\nBodinga is a calm town, but it is good to be careful. Move around in the day and use main roads. If you are new, go with a local person you trust. Dress modestly and respect the culture. Hold your bag, phone, and money well, especially in crowded places. If there is a problem, you can go to the police station or health center in the town."}
{"id": "213525", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213525", "title": "Charleville-M\u00e9zi\u00e8res", "text": "Charleville-M\u00e9zi\u00e8res is a town in France in Champagne-Ardenne, the capital and largest city in the Ardennes department in terms of population.\nUnderstand.\nCharleville-M\u00e9zi\u00e8res was created in 1966 through the merger of several towns: M\u00e9zi\u00e8res, long a military stronghold contained within its ramparts; Charleville, a bourgeois city founded in the seventeenth century by Charles de Gonzague around the Place Ducale; and the industrial municipality of Mohon, together with \u00c9tion and Montcy-Saint-Pierre. The city is best known as the birthplace of Arthur Rimbaud and for hosting the World Festival of Puppet Theatres and the Cabaret Vert eco-rock festival each August. Yet its roots reach back much further: the area lay on the Roman road between Reims and Cologne, traces of which remain near Warcq. Archaeological excavations at the Manchester exit have uncovered Gallo-Roman dwellings and burials. Castrice, a Roman site at Montcy-Saint-Pierre, once boasted temples on Mount Olympus and the Berthaucourt plateau before invasions brought its destruction.\nM\u00e9zi\u00e8res emerged as a fortified city from at least the ninth century, its name linked to the old French word for \u201cwall\u201d. Repeatedly besieged yet never conquered, its strategic position controlling river traffic made it vital. Its most famous episode came during the 1521 siege, when Pierre Terrail de Bayard\u2019s deception forced the vastly superior Imperial forces to withdraw. Although fortified further during the Wars of Religion, M\u00e9zi\u00e8res declined in the seventeenth century as Charles de Gonzague established Charleville nearby, exempt from French trade restrictions and granted tax privileges. Charleville flourished while M\u00e9zi\u00e8res withered. Rivalry persisted through the French Revolution and successive wars, with both cities enduring devastation in 1815, 1870, and especially the First World War, when Charleville served as William II\u2019s wartime capital and M\u00e9zi\u00e8res was bombed to ruins. After rebuilding with help from Manchester, M\u00e9zi\u00e8res initiated a merger with Le Theux in 1965, and soon after joined with Charleville, Mohon, Montcy-Saint-Pierre, and \u00c9tion. Today, the former rivals thrive together, united since 2014 within the Charleville-M\u00e9zi\u00e8res-Sedan agglomeration of over 150,000 inhabitants.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThere is no airport in the Ardennes, the closest in France being Paris-Vatry at 150 km. Charleroi-Bruxelles-Sud, in Belgium, is at 100 km and that of Luxembourg-Findel, to Grand Duchy of Luxembourg, is 154 km.\n* the Lille \u2194 Metz line: 3 daily round trips from Lille with connections at Hirson or Aulnoyes-Aymeries; 2 daily round trips from Metz. It also allows you to arrive from Sedan, the second largest city in the department, in less than half an hour: there are 18 daily round trips.\n* the Charleville-M\u00e9zi\u00e8res \u2194 Givet line: 15 daily round trips.\n* the Charleville-M\u00e9zi\u00e8res \u2194 Reims line then Paris by TGV: 2 daily round trips.\nGet around.\nTraffic in Charleville-M\u00e9zi\u00e8res is not the most problematic: of course, there will be the usual rush hour slowdowns, but not much more. However, it should be noted that traffic can become complicated during the puppet festival, or during le Cabaret Vert, because the population of the city can triple! It is better to fall back on the bus network or the bicycle during this period.\nSee.\nCharleville, birthplace of Arthur Rimbaud.\nJean Nicolas Arthur Rimbaud was born in Charleville on October 20, 1854 rue Bonaparte, above a bookstore, now at where is... a bookstore! A commemorative plaque marks the place. Far from holding a grudge against the poet who did not carry the city in his heart, Charleville took every opportunity to celebrate the most famous of its children.\nBuy.\nArdennes specialities.\nAny self-respecting bakery in the Ardennes will offer the sugar tart, a typically regional pastry: the, near the Place Ducale offers it for example. Sweets lovers will also find what they are looking for: slates (nougatine coated with a coloured chocolate... slate!) and chocolate wild boars are sold in Charleroi confectionery, including. Anyone who has tasted the Ardennes charcuterie (its country ham, its white pudding with onions, etc.) will want to extend their stay in the Ardennes by taking home some \"souvenirs\": the charcuterie allows you to do your shopping before leaving, or even to place your order on the site!"}
{"id": "213530", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213530", "title": "Triesenberg", "text": "Triesenberg is a mountain town and municipality of 2,600 (2022) in Liechtenstein. It also happens to be the country's largest municipality. The municipality is part of \"The Most Beautiful Swiss Villages\" association, which represents 48 villages in Switzerland and Liechtenstein. \nGet in.\nBy bus.\nTriesenberg is on bus route 21, 10 minutes from Vaduz. Timetables can be found at liemobil.li."}
{"id": "213536", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213536", "title": "Egbe", "text": "\u1eb8gb\u1eb9 M\u1eb9kun, popularly called \u1eb8gb\u1eb9, is a historic town in the Yagba West local government area of Kogi State, Nigeria.\nUnderstand.\nThis town is an ancient town bordering Kogi and Kwara States. It is surrounded by some undulating and luxuriant hills of savannah vegetation which are visible as you move around the town; this explained why it is sometimes referred to as \"Jerusalem in Nigeria\". The dialect spoken by these people is called Yagba, they have ancestry root linked to the Yorubas in Nigeria as they speak Yoruba. \nClimate.\nThe wet season lasts from April to October with rainfall peaking in September. The dry season from November to March is characterized by the northeast tradewind, known as the Harmattan.\nGet in.\nBy bus You can travel to Egbe, Kogi State by bus from major cities like Abuja, Benin, Lagos. There are numerous transport services to help get into Egbe such as ABC Transport, God is Good motors, Big Joe Motors, Young shall grow and other suitable transport companies are available.\nGet around.\nBy tricycle Tricycle can be seen all around the roads most times."}
{"id": "213550", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213550", "title": "Ahualulco", "text": "Ahualulco is a small town in San Luis Potosi, just north of the state capital. \nUnderstand.\nBefore the Spanish conquest, the area around Ahualulco was inhabited by the Chichimeca indigenous culture. The Chichimeca did not build a permanent settlement here, but relics, including arrowheads and flint pieces have been found in caves nearby. \nThe first Spanish settlers came to the area in 1542. They built ranches and a small town that grew slowly. Construction on the town's first church began in 1574. The Hacienda of San Francisco de la Parada also dates from the 16th century.\nThe town was not always part of San Luis Potosi. It was originally part of the state of Zacatecas, but in 1858, the two states agreed to exchange territory and Ahualulco went to San Luis Potosi while the town of Ojos Calientes became part of Zacatecas. \nThe town adopted the formal name \"Ahualulco de Sonida 13\" which acknowledges the work of local son, Julian Carillo who is known for theorizing that instead of there being 12 musical tones, as generations of musicians have learned, maybe there was another tone that nobody could hear. Carillo dubbed this the \"13th sound\". It was best heard on speakers from the band \"Spinal Tap\" that could be turned up to 11.\nGet in.\nThe nearest airport is San Luis Potosi International Airport (). From San Luis Potosi, a taxi will take as little as 30 minutes to reach Ahualulco. The cheapest way to get there is by bus.\nBy bus.\nBuses operated by Del Norte or by Transportes Frontera depart from the Central de Autobuses in San Luis Potosi for the 30-45 minute ride to Ahualulco. Tickets cost as little as M$50. Buses are very frequent with many departures throughout the day.\nGet around.\nIt's a small town that is easily navigated by walking or by riding a bike, though there is a taxi sitio near the town plaza and there are usually taxis waiting near the Grupo Senda bus stop.\nSleep.\nThere's really only one hotel is Ahualulco, so your best bet for a wide selection of lodging options is in San Luis Potosi."}
{"id": "213555", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213555", "title": "Baras", "text": "Baras is a town in Rizal, Philippines. no population data for this page\nUnderstand.\nThe town was established by the Franciscans at the end of the 16th century as Visita de Santiago. no population data for this page\nGet in.\nBaras is 49 km away from Manila and 32 km away from Antipolo. It is accessible via Manila East Road and Marcos Highway.\nJeepneys, buses and UVs pass by the town through J.P. Rizal Street, which runs through the center of town, or the parallel Manila East Road, which serves as a bypass route at the outskirts of town.\nJeepneys from the terminal in Paenaan, near the town's border with Antipolo, are bound for Marikina.\nThe minibus service from SM Masinag in Antipolo bound for Pililla passes through Baras.\nGet around.\nPlaces within the town are accessible by tricycle and cars. The northernmost part of the town, including Pinugay, is accessible from the town proper via Lagundi-Baliksaka Road.\nDo.\nBaras is the birthplace of native martial arts Sikaran, which was popular among the farmers in the village of Bara.\nSafety.\nThe Baras stretch of the Marilaque Highway is a known accident-prone area with its winding curves and the occasional horde of motorcyclists and drag racers turning the road into a playground for stunts they cannot do in Metro Manila."}
{"id": "213563", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213563", "title": "South Cotabato", "text": "South Cotabato is a province in Soccsksargen. It is known for its tuna and pineapple industries. It is home of Mount Matutum and Lake Sebu.\nUnderstand.\nSouth Cotabato is a province in the Philippines located in the Soccsksargen region in Mindanao and borders Sultan Kudarat to the north and west, Sarangani to the south and northeast, and Davao del Sur to the far northeast. To the southeast lies Sarangani Bay. South Cotabato was formed from Cotabato province in 1966.\nTalk.\nHiligaynon is the most widely spoken language in the province, with Cebuano being widely spoken in the eastern part of the province, including General Santos.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nGeneral Santos International Airport () is served by the province with daily commercial flights.\nBy bus.\nBusses from Davao City have a terminal in General Santos and Koronadal. Busses with terminals in General Santos are bound for Cotabato City.\nBy car.\nSouth Cotabato is accessible from Sarangani and Sultan Kudarat via Pan-Philippine Highway.\nGet around.\nSouth Cotabato's respective towns are served by tricycle. There are jeepneys and UVs from General Santos and Polomolok that are bound for Koronadal via Pan-Philippine Highway."}
{"id": "213608", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213608", "title": "Jamboni", "text": "Jamboni (\u099c\u09be\u09ae\u09ac\u09a8\u09bf \"J\u0101mbani\") is a town in the Jhargram district of West Bengal."}
{"id": "213623", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213623", "title": "Skelmersdale", "text": "Skelmersdale is a market town in West Lancashire with 35,000 people as of 2021.\nUnderstand.\nSkelmersdale means \"Skjaldmarr's valley\", and in the Domesday Book was recorded as \"Skalmeresedel. Skem\" is the shortened/slang name for Skelmersdale, and locals will use that commonly.\nIn 1961 Skelmersdale was designated a new town, designed to house overspill population from the north Liverpool conurbation. The River Tawd runs through Skelmersdale. It is \"unsafe to drink\" from the river. \nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest airports are Liverpool John Lennon ('), Leeds/Bradford Airport (') and Manchester (). Overall, Manchester has better flight connections.\nBy rail.\nA nearby train station is Ormskirk, where you can then transfer.\nBy road.\nThe M58 and A577 lead into Skelmersdale.\nBy bus.\nThere are bus stops around. Buses in the North West typically make stops in Skelmersdale.\nSkelmersdale Bus Station is in Concourse Shopping Centre, but there are other minor stations littered around.\nGet around.\nBy bus.\nTransport via bus to The Concourse is the most common in Skem.\nBy taxi.\nSkem Express Cars is the official taxi service. It has an office in the Conny, an app to request taxis and its phone number is: \u260f.\nOn foot.\nSkelmersdale has many hidden wooded paths, and pedestrian walkways to get around the town on foot. Most places are walking distance from the Concourse Shopping Centre.\nEat.\nThe local specialties are Lancashire Hot Pot and Black Pudding. Sit-down restaurants are not common here.\nDrink.\nThe most popular alcohol is lager beer. Pubs often have darts, pub gardens and live sports including darts, cricket and football. \nCope.\nThere are various churches in Skelmersdale. Catholicism is the most practised religion in Skem, although other branches of Christianity as well as Islam are practised. \nLaundry in Skem is available in 2 launderettes.\nStay healthy.\nTap water is safe to drink in Skelmersdale, but pipes can stop working. If a pipe stops working, let your taps run for a while before using them again. There are various doctors offices in Skelmersdale, including a walk-in centre. The nearest hospital is Ormskirk.\nSkelmersdale can be blistering cold in the winter, Be sure to take a jacket and stay warm.\nStay safe.\nSkelmersdale is prone to knife and drug crime. Gang activity is not uncommon, especially in Digmoor. Exercise high caution at night. Its crime rate is 71 out of every 1000 people, which is low but still unsafe.\nConnect.\nSkelmersdale has 5G and all UK Mobile Carriers. Internet Services is average in Skelmersdale, The Post Office is inside ASDA Superstore."}
{"id": "213635", "revid": "2185646", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213635", "title": "Imphal Valley", "text": "The Imphal Valley, also known as Inner Manipur, is the heart of the Indian state of Manipur."}
{"id": "213636", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213636", "title": "Manipur Hills", "text": "The Manipur Hills are the hilly area of the Indian state of Manipur, also called Outer Manipur."}
{"id": "213672", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213672", "title": "Jiribam", "text": "Jiribam (also known as Jiri in Manipuri) is a tiny strip of plain at the westernmost edge of the Indian state of Manipur. The meeting point of Meitei and Indo Aryan cultures, Jiribam is also the sole railhead to Manipur. Jiribam plain is geographically an eastern extension of the Barak river plains in particular and the Ganga-Brahmaputra river plains in general. Though separated from the Imphal Valley, by the Manipur hills, its demographic feature resembles that of the Imphal Valley, with Meitei population dominating the region, and tribals and immigrants as minority groups.\nUnderstand.\nAccording to the official Jiribam district website, the region has several essential public utilities. There are four banks serving the area\u2014Bandhan Bank, Punjab National Bank, State Bank of India, and Manipur State Co\u2011operative Bank. Jiribam has one college called Jiri College, and electricity is provided by the Manipur State Power Distribution Company Limited (MSPDCL). Healthcare needs are met by the District Hospital Jiribam. The Jiribam Municipal Council manages local governance, and postal services are handled by the Jiribam Sub Post Office.\nGet in.\nBy other terrestrial means of transport.\nPassenger services for cars, buses, or taxis are available, from Imphal to Jiribam inside Manipur, or from Silchar (of Assam) to Jiribam. For bus service, one can visit the Interstate Bus Terminus of Imphal. For others, one can contact private or government run taxi services.\nBy airways.\nBy helicopter.\nPassenger service in helicopter to and from Imphal is available. Pre-booking is recommended because it is limited in flight and demands are high.\nBy aeroplane.\nThe nearest airports are the Silchar Airport in Assam and the Imphal International Airport (alias Bir Tikendrajit International Airport) in Imphal.\nBy waterways.\nBoats are available in the Jiri river to go to Jiribam from neighborhood towns and villages in the bordering state of Assam."}
{"id": "213679", "revid": "2265795", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213679", "title": "Gombi", "text": "Gombi is a local government area in northern Adamawa State, Nigeria, located on the main road between Yola and Biu. It is an important commercial and cultural hub in the region.\nUnderstand.\nGombi is a historical town in northern Adamawa State, Nigeria, inhabited mainly by the Bura, Fulani, and Higgi people, with Hausa widely spoken. Once part of the old Borno Empire, it became part of the Adamawa Emirate in the 19th century under Modibbo Adama. The town grew into a major trading center linking Yola to Borno and remains an agricultural hub producing maize, millet, groundnuts, and beans. Gombi is also known for its rich cultural heritage, traditional festivals, and leadership under the District Head within the Adamawa Emirate Council.\nGet in.\nBy road.\nGombi is well connected by road to several major towns in the region.\nConnect.\nGombi has MTN and Airtel networks, but Glo is not strong there. To connect with people, you need to go through the community leaders."}
{"id": "213696", "revid": "36420", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213696", "title": "Touts", "text": "Touts are a common problem in tourist areas. As paid operatives, they work hard to convince tourists to seek services from certain businesses. Typically, a straightforward \"no\" will suffice, but in some cases touts can be concentrated in such large numbers (such as near parks or tourist areas) or so persistent that they become a genuine obstacle to tourist activities.\nUnderstand.\nTouts often have one face for tourists and run separate errands entirely for where they're working, such as free food and drinks, and don't pay attention to customers once they've taken the bait. Don't expect their friendliness to last beyond the transaction.\nTouts exist everywhere, but some cities face far more of an issue with touts than others. In Europe, you're far more likely to encounter touts in more popular cities such as Paris, London, Rome or Barcelona than in cities like Hamburg, Vienna or Zurich. In North Africa, touts/hagglers are a serious problem in Egypt and also Marrakesh to a lesser extent; less so in Casablanca. Typically, the lower a country's standard of living or the more touristy a place is, the worse you'll find the touting \u2013 this isn't universal, but it's a good guideline.\nAvoiding touts.\nDepending on their level of desperation, touts may focus only on large groups. This is especially true if they are recruiting customers for restaurants, and if you travel separately or on your own, they might not bother you.\nWhile touts most often target pedestrians, touting strategies affect vehicle traffic as well. One tout and borderline scam to avoid is providing parking near (but \"not\" in!) parks or stadiums at a higher price than official parking. Signs often imply that parking is official but aren't outright illegal. Know exactly where you're going, such as by checking the location on Street View before visiting, to know where to park in advance.\nWhat not to do.\nTouts often create a sense of urgency that prevents you from thinking clearly, so you should avoid making hasty decisions. Although they may appear friendly, sharing personal information such as your accommodation, itinerary, or phone number with strangers carries significant risks. Do not follow them to unclear locations or places outside public areas, as this could compromise your safety. Additionally, be cautious with tours or services that lack transparent pricing and details \u2014 always request a price list or clear documentation before making decisions. Showing too much interest or being easily persuaded may make you a prime target for other touts."}
{"id": "213714", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213714", "title": "Polomolok", "text": "Polomolok is a town in South Cotabato, Soccsksargen. It is home of the country's largest pineapple plantation.\nUnderstand.\nThe town's name was derived from \"Flomlok\", a B\u2019laan term for \"hunting grounds\". It was historically a hunting ground for the B'laan people.\nThe town was first established on November 2, 1940 as Polomolok Settlement District, but it was on August 21, 1957 when it was formally established as a town.\nGet in.\nBaras is 17 km away from General Santos and 41 km away from Koronadal. It is accessible via Manila East Road and Marcos Highway.\nJeepneys, buses and UVs pass by the town via Pan-Philippine Highway.\nUVs from the terminal near the park are bound for General Santos and Koronadal.\nGet around.\nAll of the main places to visit within the town are accessible by tricycle and car."}
{"id": "213748", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213748", "title": "Jainagar", "text": "\"For other places with the same name, see Jaynagar (disambiguation).\"\nJainagar (Maithili, Hindi and Nepali: \u091c\u092f\u0928\u0917\u0930 \"Jaynagar\") is a town in the Mithila region of Bihar, India, at the border to Nepal. The border town is one of the overland gateways to the Himalayan nation from India.\nSee and do.\nThe Himalayas of Nepal, including the Mount Everest and Kangchenjunga, are visible from Jainagar after rainfall, as the air quality of the area improves."}
{"id": "213749", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213749", "title": "Kangchenjunga", "text": "Kangchenjunga is the world's third-highest mountain at 8586 m. It straddles the border of India (Sikkim) and Nepal and can be visited from either side:\nOther than these, the mountain is famously visible from North Bengal in India, particularly Darjeeling. It can also be seen from northern Bihar, as well as Panchagarh in northern Bangladesh."}
{"id": "213764", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213764", "title": "Bolton Valley", "text": "Bolton Valley is a town and ski resort in Chittenden County, Vermont.\nUnderstand.\nThe town, a couple miles to the south, is known as Bolton.\nGet in.\nBolton Valley Access Rd connects Bolton with the ski resort. It connects with US-2, but not I-89.\nDo.\nThere are several hiking areas on Duxbury Rd, on the south side of the Winooski River in Bolton. Turn south on Cochran Rd from US-2, and then onto Duxbury Rd after you cross the river."}
{"id": "213795", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213795", "title": "Madagali", "text": "Madagali is a Local Government Area in Adamawa State, North East Nigeria. It lies close to the border with Borno State and Cameroon, and is known for its rich culture, farmlands, and scenic hilly landscapes.\nUnderstand.\nMadagali is one of the oldest towns in northern Adamawa. Long ago, people from the Marghi and Higi (Kamwe) tribes settled in the area because of its rich soil and water from nearby streams. They built small farming villages that later grew into the town known today as Madagali.\nThe town became an important trade point between Nigeria and Cameroon, where farmers and traders exchanged goods such as grains, livestock, and handmade items.\nDuring the colonial period, Madagali was under the Old Adamawa Province and later became part of Adamawa State when Nigeria created new states.\nIn the early 2020, the town faced insecurity that affected daily life, but peace is returning. Many people have gone back to their farms and reopened local markets. Today, Madagali continues to grow as a peaceful farming and trading community.\nGet in.\nBy road.\nYou can travel to Madagali by road:"}
{"id": "213825", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213825", "title": "Canaries", "text": "\"This article is about the town in Saint Lucia. If you are looking for the Canary Islands, click here.\"\nCanaries is in Saint Lucia. It has a population of 2,200 (2022) and is south of Castries and on the Saint Lucian coast. It is 16 km\u00b2.\nUnderstand.\nCanaries was founded by the French in the 18th century. Its governing body is the Canaries Village Council and it is named after Kannawi, the Creole word for cooking pots.\nSee.\nThe beaches are stunning in Canaries, and you can relax on them all day. although, some areas of the beaches are part of the bay, and could be busy. \nConnect.\nYou can get a Saint Lucian SIM card for 15 EC$ from Digicel in their stores or from resellers. Flow hands out SIM cards for free, but be prepared to buy a plan on the spot. 4G is the best data available in Saint Lucia.\nWi-Fi is average in Canaries, and is commonly available in major areas, but do not count on Wi-Fi being available in small areas and small restaurants.\nStay safe.\nDeadly wildlife, including snakes and bugs, have caused deaths on the island, and you should keep on high alert. Take bug spray and anti-venom if necessary.\nCommon foods in Saint Lucia can contain allergens, including eggs and nuts. Allergy information isn't as good in Saint Lucia as most countries, so be careful about what you eat. You also shouldn't go into the foragery at night.\nStay healthy.\nThe nearest hospital is in Castries, which is 15 miles away. Ambulances and air ambulances are available in Canaries in emergencies. Tap water is generally safe in Saint Lucia, but bottled water is safer to drink.\nCope.\nReligion.\nThere is one church in Canaries.\nLaundry.\nThere are no launderettes in Canaries, although hotels may have laundry services.\nGo next.\nCastries - Capital of St Lucia\nGros Islet - Northern, seaside town with remarkable beaches\nMarigot Bay - Bumpy road adventure, amazing for the daring!\nVieux Fort - Small town on the coast\nSoufriere - Small town with a large stadium on the south coast"}
{"id": "213848", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213848", "title": "Spencer (Massachusetts)", "text": "Spencer is a small town in the Worcester Hills of Central Massachusetts near Worcester. As a rural community, there is not much in the way of nightlife but there are many opportunities for outdoor exploration and recreation.\nGet in.\nThere are no major highways through Spencer, so you will need to take either the state routes or backroads. MA Route 9 runs east-west through the center of Spencer, and MA Route 31 runs through the center of Spencer north-south. If you take MA Route 49 northbound from Sturbridge, you will intersect with Route 9 in the western part of Spencer.\nGet around.\nTraveling by car is the most practical way to navigate Spencer. A bus line does go through the town center, but outside the town center places can be far-flung.\nSleep.\nThere are a handful of other AirBnB/Vrbo listings for Spencer, in addition to the two listed above."}
{"id": "213852", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213852", "title": "Rinc\u00f3n de Guayabitos", "text": "Rinc\u00f3n de Guayabitos is a small beach town on the Pacific coast of Nayarit. It is popular with Mexican families and surfers looking for low-cost accommodations and restaurants. Rincon de Guayabitos is part of a cluster of other popular beach areas including Lo de Marcos and La Pe\u00f1ita de Jaltemba.\nGet in.\nThe closest airport is in Puerto Vallarta (), which is served by most major airlines in North America, as well as domestic and international charter flights. You can rent a car and drive to Rincon de Guayabitos, or use local buses.\nBy car.\nFrom Puerto Vallarta, drive north on federal highway MEX-200. From the Puerto Vallarta Airport, it is 75 km to Rincon de Guayabitos and will take about 90 minutes (longer if you stop at other beach towns to check out the scenery).\nBy bus.\nThe Compostela bus company has direct bus service departing Puerto Vallarta hourly for the 3-1/2 hour trip to Rincon de Guayabitos. Fares cost about M$100. This is a decidedly second-class bus that stops everywhere along the route. Compostela and Pacifico buses stop along highway MEX-200 to let passengers off and on. Grupo Estrella Blanca and Primera Plus buses also stop at their company-owned bus stations. These first-class buses are faster, more comfortable and more expensive than local buses (but can save you an hour of travel time). Estrella Blanca is about a few meters walk from the Compostela stop, but on Avenida Sol Nuevo, Primera Plus is about a kilometer from the Compostela stop at the corner of Av. Jacarandas and Av. Guayabitos.\nGet around.\nWalking is the best way to get around town. Renting a bike can be fun and will expand your range.\nSleep.\nSeveral traditional and beachfront resort hotels are available in the town of Rincon de Guayabitos. Small bungalows and hostel-style lodging can be found around Playa Los Ayala."}
{"id": "213866", "revid": "2320419", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213866", "title": "Methoni", "text": "Methoni (Greek: \u039c\u03b5\u03b8\u03ce\u03bd\u03b7), formerly Methone or Modon, is in Messenia.\nUnderstand.\nMethoni is 11 km south of Pylos and 11 km west of Foinikounta, with an area of 97.202 km2. Its name may be derived from Mothona, a mythical rock. The municipal unit of Methoni includes the nearby villages of Grizokampos, Foinikounta, Foiniki, Lachanada, Varakes, Kainourgio Chorio, Kamaria, Evangelismos, and the Oinnoussai Islands. The islands are Sapientza, Schiza, and Santa Marina; they form a natural protection for Methoni harbour.\nMethoni was of relatively minor importance in antiquity and the early Byzantine period, though it was used as a naval base as early as the 6th century when the general Belisarius is said to have operated from here during campaigns against the Vandals in North Africa. In the medieval period, its position made it strategically significant, but it also suffered from instability and piracy, at times even being used (or tolerated) as a base for attacks on Venetian shipping, leading the Venetians to destroy the settlement in 1125.\nAfter the Fourth Crusade in 1204, Methoni came firmly under Venetian control from 1209 onwards. The Venetians heavily fortified the site and developed it into a key maritime station on the trade and pilgrimage routes between Venice and the Latin East, particularly the lucrative traffic to the Holy Land. Together with nearby Koroni, it became known as the \u201ceyes of the Serene Republic,\u201d reflecting its critical role in monitoring and securing sea routes in the eastern Mediterranean. From 1210, Methoni\u2019s bishop was made a suffragan of the archbishopric of Corinth, further integrating it into the Latin ecclesiastical system of Frankish Greece.\nGet in.\nBy bus.\nThere are KTEL services that stop in the centre of the old town.\nBy car.\nTo see the countryside around Methoni, you'll need a car."}
{"id": "213882", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213882", "title": "Cala d'Or", "text": "Cala d'Or is the main coastal resort in Migjorn de Mallorca, the southern region of that island, with a population in 2024 of 4100. It was built from the 1930s around three sea inlets: Cala Gran central, Cala Egos to the west and Cala Ferrera to the east. \nGet in.\nBy road from Palma city or airport head east on MA-19, all the way to its terminus in Cala d'Or. \nTIB buses link the towns:\nGet around.\nBus 521 runs every 70 min from Cala d'Or to Portopetro and Cala Mondrag\u00f3.\nTaxi operators include Mallorca Transfers (+34 604 805454), Taxis Cala d\u2019Or (+34 971 657058) and Taxi Barcel\u00f3 (+34 618 082189).\nConnect.\nAs of Oct 2025, Cala d'Or and its approach roads have 4G from Vodafone, and 5G from MasMovil, Movistar and Orange."}
{"id": "213887", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213887", "title": "Huaibei", "text": "Huaibei (\u6dee\u5317, Hu\u00e1ib\u011bi) is a prefecture-level city in northern Anhui Province, China. What draws visitors to Huaibei is not just its history, but also its simple and honest way of life. It's not as bustling as big cities, yet it's full of cultural depth. Here, you can experience human history and natural beauty at the same time.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nHuaibei's history dates back to ancient times, making it one of the important regions for the southward expansion of Central Plains civilization in China. Humans inhabited the area thousands of years ago, and its strategic location along transportation routes gradually led to the formation of villages and markets. During the Sui and Tang dynasties, the Grand Canal passed through the surrounding area, bringing trade and cultural exchange. Although modern Huaibei is a relatively young city, its rapid development stemmed from coal mining that began in the mid-20th century, transforming it from a rural area into a vital energy base in northern Anhui in a short period. As coal resources gradually dwindled, Huaibei, once a \"resource city,\" began its transformation, increasingly focusing on ecology, urban renewal, and cultural tourism. Therefore, the Huaibei you see today is a city constantly evolving between history and modernity. \nGeography.\nHuaibei was a crucial node in the Sui and Tang dynasties' Grand Canal, and also a convergence point for Chu and Han cultures, canal cultures, and wine cultures. Huaibei City is also known as Xiangcheng. It sits along the Sui River, at the junction of Shandong, Jiangsu, Henan, and Anhui provinces, and has a population of 2.1 million. It administers three urban districts and one county: Duji District, Xiangshan District, Lieshan District, and Suixi County. \nClimate.\nHuaibei City is located in the mid-latitude region and belongs to the warm temperate semi-humid monsoon climate zone. Its main climatic characteristics are distinct monsoons, four distinct seasons, mild climate, moderate rainfall, variable spring temperatures, clear and crisp autumn, distinct winter, and concentrated summer rain.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nHuaibei City is about 90 km away from Xuzhou Guanyin International Airport.\nGet around.\nBy bus.\nIf you're new to Huaibei, taking the bus is a more convenient way to get around. Most buses cost only 2 yuan, and if you get a transportation card, it will be cheaper and more convenient than cash. Students and seniors also get additional discounts. If you transfer to another bus within 40 minutes after swiping your card, you don't need to pay again.Huaibei is not a large city, so public buses can basically reach the city center, train station, new urban area, hospitals, universities, and there are also some \"health special lines\" for convenient access to hospitals.\nPayment can be made by scanning a QR code using WeChat Pay or Alipay, or by cash. Exact exact change is usually required, as drivers typically don't give exact change. Bus displays or announcements are usually not in English, and route information is primarily in Chinese, so translation apps or assistance from locals may be necessary. Hotel staff and younger passengers are generally willing to help.\nBy taxi.\nIn Huaibei, like many Chinese cities, taxis are a very convenient option for residents and tourists traveling short distances. Taxis can usually be hailed on the street or booked through mobile ride-hailing apps such as Didi or Gaode.The starting fare is generally around 6 RMB (approximately US$0.80), covering a distance of about 2.5 kilometers (approximately 1.5 miles). Beyond this initial distance, the fare is calculated per kilometer (km \u2248 0.62 mph). Waiting fees may also apply if the vehicle is moving slowly or in congested traffic. Nighttime taxi rides may be slightly more expensive than daytime rides due to a nighttime surcharge.\nBy bicycle.\nHuaibei City offers a public bicycle rental service, similar to the bike-sharing systems in many large cities. Simply scan a QR code to rent a bike; it's very convenient. You can park anywhere you like, and use your mobile phone to check nearby stations and available bikes.\nStay safe.\nHuaibei is a very safe city, day and night."}
{"id": "213891", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213891", "title": "Springsure", "text": "Springsure is a small town of 950 (2021) on the A7 highway in Central Queensland. Between Emerald and Roma, it is the largest town in the 400-km section of highway.\nGet in.\nSpringsure is only accessible by car. No coach routes operate along this section of the A7.\nFrom Rolleston, Springsure is a 50-minute drive (~71 km) northwest on the Dawson Highway. The highway ends here and continues north of Springsure as the Gregory Highway.\nFrom Emerald, drive for about 50 minutes south (~68 km) south on the Gregory Highway."}
{"id": "213893", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=213893", "title": "Cad\u00ed-Moixer\u00f3 Natural Park", "text": "Cad\u00ed-Moixer\u00f3 Natural Park is spread across three countys (Comarques) of Catalonia: Alt Urgell, Bergued\u00e0 and Cerdanya in three provinces. It includes the special protection area of Paratge Natural d'Inter\u00e8s Nacional del Mass\u00eds del Pedraforca in the south east side of the park.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nThe park, established in 1983, spans over 41,000 hectares across the regions of Alt Urgell, Bergued\u00e0, and Cerdanya in Catalonia. The park encompasses the dramatic ridges of the Cad\u00ed and Moixer\u00f3 ranges, forming an imposing natural barrier in the Pre-Pyrenees and dividing the Segre and Llobregat river basins. Efforts to protect the area date back to regional planning initiatives during the Spanish Republic in 1932, with further conservation steps in the 1960s when it became a national hunting reserve. Medieval villages such as Bag\u00e0 and Bellver de Cerdanya, along with castles and remnants from Cathar, smuggler, and resistance histories, add significant cultural value.\nClimate.\nWeather varies by season: winters can be cold with snow, while summers are warm and ideal for hiking. Always check local forecasts, dress in layers, and bring suitable footwear. Some services may be limited in the off-season, so confirm availability before visiting.\nLandscape.\nWith peaks exceeding 2,500 m, including the prominent Vultur\u00f3, the park features sharp northern cliffs, gentle southern slopes, and deep valleys that create a rugged mosaic visible throughout the region. Diverse habitats range from dense forests of oak, black pine, and beech to meadows and rocky escarpments, supporting a rich variety of flora and fauna from both Central European and Mediterranean zones. Hiking, climbing, and panoramic gondola rides offer extraordinary views of iconic summits like Pedraforca, the Montserrat mountains, and far-reaching valleys. Throughout the seasons, visitors encounter breathtaking scenery shaped by glacial and tectonic forces,\nFlora and fauna.\nThe park is home to more than 1,500 plant species, with habitats ranging from Mediterranean woods at low altitudes to alpine meadows at higher elevations. Unique flora includes regional endemics such as Xatardia scabra and Delphinium montanum, alongside notable alpine plants like the gentian and saffron crocus. Forests of mountain pine, beech, and oak provide a rich undergrowth for diverse plant life. The park\u2019s fauna is equally varied, hosting 249 vertebrate species, including emblematic mammals such as chamois, deer, marmot, and the wolf, which has been recolonising the area since 2000. Birdlife features majestic raptors like bearded vulture and golden eagle, woodland specialists such as capercaillie and black woodpecker, and numerous butterfly varieties. Reptiles like European green lizard and amphibians including the Pyrenean brook newt reflect the park\u2019s ecological richness, making it a biodiversity hotspot.\nFees and permits.\nThe park is free to enter, however there are some rules to follow and some permits are required for specific activities.\nPark rules.\nThe park enforces specific rules and regulations to balance nature conservation with public enjoyment and safety. Visiting the park is encouraged, but respecting the environment and following regulations is fundamental for ensuring long-term preservation and compliance with local laws.\nGet around.\nThe park is mainly a mountain range with few roads inside the park area.\nEat.\nThere are many leisure areas in the \"Do\" section where you can picnic.\nLook for restaurants at villages around the park. \nDrink.\nLook for bar/cafeteries at villages around the park."}
