{"id": "214835", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=214835", "title": "Hinsdale (Massachusetts)", "text": "Hindsdale is a small rural community deep in the Berkshire Hills of Massachusetts. It mostly offers outdoor recreation and camping opportunities.\nUnderstand.\nThe area that is now Hinsdale used to be Mohican land, bordering land that was Pocumtuc and Mohican. It was first colonized by Europeans in 1763. It was incorporated as \"Partridgefield\" in 1771, named for Oliver Partridge, one of the three purchasers of the town. The Western Parish of Partridgefield broke away from its eastern half and incorporated in 1804, renaming itself for the family of the reverend Theodore Hinsdale. Hinsdale owned an important woolen mill, which remained important to the economy of the town until it closed during the Great Depression.\nHinsdale is bordered by Dalton to the west, Washington to the south, Peru to the east, and Windsor to the north. The Appalachian Trail runs through forest in the southeast of the town but has no designated trailheads or overnight sleeping areas in the Hinsdale section.\nGet in.\nThe best way into Hinsdale is MA Route 8, which runs northwest from Dalton, through Hinsdale, and then south into Washington. MA Route 143, whose eastern terminus originates off MA Route 9 in Williamsburg, terminates in Hinsdale and is the best route into the town from the east. While it is possible to travel to Hinsdale on foot from neighboring towns, the best means of travel is by car.\nGet around.\nThe main roads of Hinsdale are traversable on foot, but going further afield on foot is more difficult and more time consuming. Car is the best means of transportation around town. Cycling is also possible. \nSleep.\nIf you want an indoor hotel experience, you will have to go nearby; Hinsdale only offers outdoor campsites as listed below. For hotels or other similar lodging, look in Peru, Windsor, Cummington, Dalton, and Pittsfield.\nGo next.\nVisit Pittsfield and Great Barrington to the west or go further even west to Albany and the New York Capital District. East is Northampton and Amherst, and southeast is Springfield. South on MA Route 8 goes to Torrington and Waterbury, Connecticut."}
{"id": "214854", "revid": "107092", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=214854", "title": "Washington (Massachusetts)", "text": "\"Not to be confused with Mount Washington (Massachusetts).\"\nWashington is small rural town in the east central Berkshire Hills of Massachusetts. Washington mostly offers outdoor wilderness activities, including at October Mountain State Forest. It also has several historic buildings. no population data for this page\nUnderstand.\nThe area that is now Washington is historically Mohican land, near the Pocumtuc. Europeans colonized the area in 1760 and the town was incorporated in 1777. The town went by various names, including Greenoch, Watsontown and Hartville, before being renamed for George Washington in 1784. The town has always been very rural and very small in population. Most of the town area is forest wilderness. Washington is bordered by Dalton and Hinsdale to the north, Peru to the northeast, Middlefield to the east, Becket to the south, Lee to the southwest, Lenox to the west, and Pittsfield to the northwest. The majority of the 16,500 acres of October Mountain State Forest is in Washington. The Appalachian Trail also passes through the town.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nThere are two main routes into Washington, both of which run north-south: MA Rt 8, from Hindsale to Becket, and Washington Mt Rd, from Dalton/Pittsfield to Becket. Washington Mt Rd begins on the Dalton/Pittsfield end at the corner junction of Williams St, and Dalton Division Rd, runs diagonally along the border of October Mountain State Forest, and turns into McNerny Rd in Becket. Washington Mt Rd can be difficult, even impassable, in bad winter conditions.\nOn foot.\nWashington lies on the Appalachian Trail and can be entered by it via the trailheads at street intersections. Route 8 and Washington Mt Rd can be traversed by foot, but this is long and difficult.\nGet around.\nCar is the feasible option to get around Washington. If you are a good hiker, you can traverse the town on foot. MA Rt 8 is fairly passable on bicycle.\nGo next.\nGo south to Becket or Torrington, Connecticut, north to Dalton and Pittsfield, or west to Lenox and Lee."}
{"id": "214861", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=214861", "title": "Akishima", "text": "Akishima (\u662d\u5cf6\u5e02 Akishima Shi) is a small city in western Tokyo, with a population of about 113,000 people (2020). The city is known for its pure water and lush greenery.\nBuy.\nYou can buy local souvenirs at Akishima Tourist Information, see \"Understand\"."}
{"id": "214868", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=214868", "title": "Asaka", "text": "Asaka (\u671d\u971e\u5e02 Asaka Shi) is a small city in Saitama, right across the river from Saitama City. Asaka is primarily a bedroom community with over 37% of its population commuting to the Tokyo metropolis for work. \nUnderstand.\nno population data for this page\nThe area is also a distribution hub for northern Tokyo.\nSleep.\nPretty much all of the hotels in the city are business hotels."}
{"id": "214871", "revid": "2182907", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=214871", "title": "Zhongshan (disambiguation)", "text": "There is more than one place called Zhongshan:"}
{"id": "214887", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=214887", "title": "Yashio", "text": "Yashio is in Saitama prefecture.\nUnderstand.\nYashio is close to the Saitama-Tokyo border and is part of the Greater Tokyo Metropolitan Area.\nYashio is primarily a distribution center, with a number of industrial parks. It is also bedroom community with part of its population commuting to the Tokyo metropolis for work. no population data for this page"}
{"id": "214888", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=214888", "title": "Rejinagar", "text": "Rejinagar (\u09b0\u09c7\u099c\u09bf\u09a8\u0997\u09b0 \"Rejinagar\") is a town in Murshidabad, West Bengal.\nUnderstand.\nno population data for this page\nGet in.\nNational Highway 12 (formerly NH 34) passes through Rejinagar."}
{"id": "214889", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=214889", "title": "Panchthupi", "text": "Panchthupi (\u09aa\u09be\u0981\u099a\u09a5\u09c1\u09aa\u09bf \"P\u0101ncthupi\") is a village in Murshidabad, West Bengal.\nUnderstand.\nno population data for this page\nGet in.\nDak Bangla Road links Panchthupi to State Highway 7 running from Rajgram (in Birbhum district) to Midnapore (in Paschim Medinipur district)."}
{"id": "214898", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=214898", "title": "Spanish-American War", "text": "The Spanish-American War was fought between the United States and the Spanish Empire in 1898. A decisive conflict, it saw the end of Spain's interests in the Americas and the Pacific and asserted the U.S.'s role as a global power.\nUnderstand.\nThe United States won the Spanish-American War in 1898, seizing the Spanish colonies of Cuba, Puerto Rico and Guam. They also bought the Spanish colony of the Philippines and defeated independence fighters there with great brutality; see the Philippines article.\nCuba was granted independence in 1902, and the Philippines in 1946, while Puerto Rico and Guam remain U.S. territories.\nForts.\nFollowing the outbreak of the war in 1898, the U.S. built several fortifications on its southern coast to protect against a potential Spanish invasion, which did not occur. Hence, these forts were in most cases operated for just a few years before being decommissioned."}
{"id": "214902", "revid": "2248002", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=214902", "title": "Otuocha", "text": "Otuocha is a vibrant town in Nigeria, serving as the headquarters of Anambra East Local Government Area in Anambra State. With its scenic waterfront and deep historical roots, Otuocha is a hidden gem of the Southeast.\nUnderstand.\nOtuocha was a divisional headquarters of the Anambra Divisional Council during the First Republic of Nigeria (1963\u20131966). Otuocha was situated on the northern flank of Biafra, a short-lived secessionist state, and was considered one of its food baskets due to its large market during the Nigerian Civil War. \nOtuocha has a sizeable population and attracts people from different ethnic groups in Nigeria. These include not only the Igbo natives of Aguleri and the Umueri community, regarded as the landowners, but also people from other ethnic groups such as the Igala and Ijaw. According to oral tradition from Umueri, the area now known as Otuocha used to be called \"Otu-Oche\" and served as a farmstead for the people of Aguleri and the ugwu na-Adegbe Village of the ancient Aguleri Kingdom.\nClimate.\nOtuocha experiences a tropical climate characterised by a warm, overcast wet season and a hot, humid dry season. Throughout the year, temperatures typically range from 19 \u00b0C (66 \u00b0F) at night in December to about 32 \u00b0C (90 \u00b0F) during the day in February. Temperatures rarely fall below 15 \u00b0C (59 \u00b0F) or rise above 33 \u00b0C (91 \u00b0F). The wet season spans from April to October, with September being the wettest month of the year.\nReligion and places of worship.\nTraditional African religious practices in Otuocha have largely been displaced by Christianity and the activities of early missionaries. Various churches have since been established in the town, representing both Protestant and Catholic denominations. In addition, there is a mosque located in Umuoba-Anam, serving the Muslim population in the area.\nGet in.\nBy road.\nYou can take a bus or drive to Otuocha from major cities like Onitsha, Nnewi, or Awka. The town is connected to the main road network, making it accessible by car or public transportation.\nBy water.\nOtuocha is located near the Niger River, and you can take a boat or ferry from nearby towns like Onitsha or Asaba.\nBy bus.\nSeveral bus companies operate from major cities to Otuocha.\nBy taxi.\nYou can hire a taxi or use ride-hailing services like Uber or Bolt from major cities.\nGet around.\nBy taxi.\nTaxis are readily available in Otuocha.\nBy motorcycle.\nMotorcycles (okadas) are a popular mode of transportation in Otuocha.\nOn foot.\nOtuocha is a relatively small town, and you can walk around to explore the local area.\nSee.\nOtuocha hosts a vibrant market known as Eke Market, situated at the centre of the town along the banks of the Omambala River. The market serves as a major commercial hub and is particularly recognised as a key yam transit point on the eastern bank of the River Niger.\nDo.\nLocated in Anambra East LGA, Otuocha is a vibrant riverine town on the banks of the Anambra River. Known for its rich Igbo culture, fishing trade, and bustling markets, it serves as a major hub for agricultural and commercial activities.\nHome to the famous Otuocha Market, the town thrives on trade, connecting farmers and merchants across Anambra and beyond. \nSleep.\nOtuocha has several accommodation options, including hotels, guesthouses, and lodges. "}
{"id": "214905", "revid": "898440", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=214905", "title": "Laid-Back Camp tour", "text": "Laid-Back Camp (\u3086\u308b\u30ad\u30e3\u30f3\u25b3, \u3086\u308b\u304d\u3083\u3093) is a Japanese manga with anime adaptation written by Afro, who was born in Hamamatsu and lives in Kofu. It focuses on camping, set in the area around Japan Alps, including Yamanashi, Nagano, Shizuoka, etc. There are many stores and transportation facilities based on real-life in the manga, making it useful when travelling.\nUnderstand.\n\"Laid-Back Camp\" was released in 2015 and adapted into anime in 2018. The protagonist is Rin Shima, who lives in Yamanashi and enjoys camping. The story starts when she meets Nadeshiko Kagamihara, who has just moved to Yamanashi on her first day and is interested in Shima's camping activity. It quickly gained attention when its anime adaptation was released in 2018 for its comfy pace and calming environment. Minobu, the manga's main setting, has therefore become a popular spot, and many people in Japan are interested in camping.\nSee Camping and Japan for details. If you consider driving a car or motorcycle like what characters in the manga do, see driving in Japan. In short, you can enjoy a drive in Yamanashi, Nagano, and Shizuoka.\nVisitor information.\nMuch of the government's travel information is available in English, but information about \"Laid-Back Camp\" is mostly in Japanese.\nPrepare.\nThe first thing a tourist should prepare besides watching \"Laid-Back Camp\" is camping facilities. Some facilities can only be bought or rented in Japan due to airline regulations (such as gas and vegetables). Don't worry, you can buy them in Japan.\nSee.\nTowns.\nTowns shown in the work are:\nEat.\nWhile you can cook by using the recipes shown in the work, some scenes feature restaurants based on real life.\nStay safe.\nMountains in Japan are very cold at night. In Episode 6 of Season 2, Yamanakako was shown as \u22122 \u00b0C in the afternoon, but it can be \"colder\" in real life: records there have dropped below \u22128 \u00b0C. Be especially careful when camping in winter. In mountainous areas, temperatures can change significantly with even slight differences in elevation, and local climate conditions can vary. Furthermore, the boiling point is lower at higher altitudes, and morning dew might also be a concern when camping."}
{"id": "214906", "revid": "1301297", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=214906", "title": "Dhamrai Upazila", "text": "Dhamrai Upazila (Bengali: \u09a7\u09be\u09ae\u09b0\u09be\u0987 \u0989\u09aa\u099c\u09c7\u09b2\u09be) is an administrative region of the Dhaka Division in central Bangladesh. Located about 40 km northwest of Dhaka, Dhamrai is known for its historic zamindari heritage, traditional metal craftsmanship, religious diversity, and rural Bengali landscapes. It is a culturally significant area on the outskirts of the capital and offers a quieter contrast to the bustle of Dhaka city.\nUnderstand.\nDhamrai has a long history shaped by ancient settlements, the zamindari system, and colonial-era administration. The region has traditionally been home to Hindu and Muslim communities living side by side, contributing to its syncretic culture and shared festivals.\nUpazila is particularly famous for its traditional metal crafts, especially brass and bell-metal work, which has been practiced for generations by local artisan families. Dhamrai also played a role in regional education and social development during the zamindari period.\nGet in.\nDhamrai is located approximately 40 km northwest of Dhaka.\nGet around.\nRickshaws and auto-rickshaws are commonly used within town areas. Walking is suitable for short distances, while local transport is used for nearby villages.\nEat.\nSmall local eateries serve traditional Bengali meals, including rice, lentils, fish, and seasonal vegetables. Snacks and sweets are available in local markets."}
{"id": "214907", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=214907", "title": "Umuleri", "text": "Umuleri is an ancient, significant Igbo town in Anambra State, South East Nigeria.\nUnderstand.\nUmuleri is in Anambra East Local Government Area, known as a major settlement in the Omambala valley and part of the Greater Onitsha Metropolis, famous for its historical Eri clan lineage and the Anambra International Airport, despite past boundary conflicts with Aguleri. \nHistory.\nUmuleri holds a significant place in Igbo history as a key part of the Eri clan, claiming descent from the legendary founder Eri, with traditions linking them to ancient migrations from Egypt and the Middle East. Its history involves its founder, Adamgbo (daughter of Eri), and later settlers, leading to its name \"children of Eri\" (Umu-Eri). Umueri's story is intertwined with its sister town, Aguleri, sharing deep ancestral roots and a history marked by both cultural unity and periods of conflict, particularly land disputes.\nGeography.\nUmuleri is located within the Anambra Valley, and bordered by the Anambra River and the Anam communities in the north, Nteje to the south, Aguleri and Nando in the east and Nsugbe to the west.\nClimate.\nUmuleri,has a tropical climate with distinct wet (April-Oct) and dry (Nov-Mar) seasons, featuring hot temperatures year-round, heavy rain in summer, and sunny, drier conditions in winter, typical of the Anambra Basin's rainforest zone with high humidity and rainfall, moderated by its location near the Niger River.\nReligion.\nPrior to the coming of Europeans (the British), Umueri people practised traditional religions, worshipping various deities. However, Christianity became the dominant religion about a century ago. 50% of the town's population are Christian, primarily Roman Catholic, Pentecostal and Anglican.\nGet in.\nBy plane: Through Chinua Achebe Airport International Airport situated at Agulueri. \nBy land: through different means of land transportation such as Bus, Tricycle, motorcycle or bicycle. \nGet around.\nThe Umuleri indigenes and visitors can move around the town by using Buses, Tricycles and Motorcycles as well trekking to their various locations (this doesn't require paying transport fare). \nSee.\nThere are some exciting places such as fields for football tournaments among the townspeople, worship centres (for praying service, crusade, night vigil) and hospital/health centre for doctors consultation if you fall sick while in the town.\nDo.\nUmuleri has prominent festivals such as Yam Festival, Masquerade parade and so on.\nBuy.\nOne of the major occupations of Umuleri indigenes is farming and as such so many farm products are harvested at the end of every planting season. Consequently, food stuffs such as tubers, grains, vegetables are bought at a very cheap price.\nEat.\nRice, stew or ofe akwu, Garri or fufu/soup, are available in different eateries such as Mama puts, guest house, and market places.\nDrink.\nAssorted drinks both local drinks (gin, palm wine, up-wine, water) and foreign drinks (wine, beers soft drinks) are available in the town of Umuleri.\nSleep.\nHostels and guests rooms are conductively available in case one visits and was not able to finish up the tasks in a day.\nGo next.\nNearby boundary villages where you can as well visit include: Anam, Nteje, Nando and Nsugbe."}
{"id": "214920", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=214920", "title": "Ufuma", "text": "Ufuma is in Anambra State, South East Nigeria.\nUnderstand.\nUfuma is a historic Igbo town in Orumba North Local Government Area, known for its rich traditions, strong agricultural roots, and nine constituent villages.\nHistory.\nUfuma is founded by Uvume in the 13th century. It's a community that preserves its cultural heritage, celebrates festivals like the new yam harvest, and focuses on unity, with resources available online to connect indigenes globally and promote development. \nClimate.\nUfuma town has a tropical wet and dry or savanna climate, characterized by two main seasons: a lengthy rainy season and a distinct dry season. \nGet in.\nVisitor and people other cities or town can get into Ufuma town by road and air.\nGet around.\nMembers of the town move freely by foot, private owned vehicles and tricycles as well.\nSee.\nIn the Ufuma town you can see the Ufuma Cave (Nei), and the bustling markets (Afo Ufuma). Ozi River and Mamu Waterfall, educational sites including the Federal Polytechnic Oko, Ufuma Campus,\nDo.\nThe Ufuma people engage in cultural spots like the EkpeleNwaanyi (All Christian Practical Praying Band).\nNew Yam Festival, alongside markets like Afo Ufuma for local commerce."}
{"id": "214924", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=214924", "title": "October Mountain State Forest", "text": "October Mountain State Forest is a state park in the Berkshires of Massachusetts.\nUnderstand.\nThe park is a 16,460-acre (6,660 ha) forest and recreational preserve. It is the largest state forest in Massachusetts and is managed by the Massachusetts Department of Conservation and Recreation. Most of the park is in the town of Washington, and the street address, main entrance, and main camping area are in Lee. Additional portions of the preserve stretch into Becket, Lenox, and Pittsfield.\nHistory.\nWhat became October Mountain State Forest originated as the personal estate of William C. Whitney, the Secretary of the Navy for President Grover Cleveland. That initial 11,000 acres (4,500 ha) was sold to the public in 1915 for a total of $60,000. Part of the money for the sale came from a pledge of a group of Berkshire residents. The park opened to the public that same year. The name \"October Mountain\" is credited to the author Herman Melville, who had a view of the mountain from his home in Pittsfield.\nLandscape.\nThe forest lies within part of the Hoosac Mountain Range in the west-central Berkshires region. The namesake mountain is the forest's highest point. It is a steeply rising ridge above the Housatonic River to the west. The center area of the forest is upland that includes several reservoirs and the Washington Mountain Marsh. The eastern section of the forest has a second, lower ridge, known as Washington Mountain.\nFlora and fauna.\nCommon animal species encountered at the park are beavers, deer, bears, songbirds, herons, hawks, and, more rarely, moose. Largemouth bass predominate in October Mountain Reservoir. Tree species include red and sugar maple, beech, oak, hickory, birch, black cherry, ash (although these are affected by the emerald ash borer), eastern hemlock, white pine, Norway spruce, and fir.\nClimate.\nWeather is variable, and during the winter many of the parking lots and some trails are shut down. Check the winter trail map if you plan on visiting during the winter (page 2 of the DCR map).\nGet in.\nOn foot.\nThe Appalachian Trail runs through the Washington portion of the reserve and is the most convenient trail by which to access the forest. The reserve can also be accessed on foot from the main lot in Lee.\nAppalachian Trail trailheads.\nThe following trailheads are those connected to an access road. There are more trailheads connected to other hiking trails throughout the forest.\nFees and permits.\nParking is $8 for Massachusetts residents and $30 for non-residents. Parking passes are available on the Mass.gov website.\nGet around.\nThe main campground is accessible by car and RV. Several car-accessible roads also go through the park; some of these are unpaved dirt roads. Off-road vehicles are allowed, with permits, but the terrain in the park is difficult and the ORV trails are recommended only for intermediate to expert riders. All of the park is traversable on foot trails, with varying degrees of difficulty. Walking off trail is possible, but more difficult. See the DCR map of the property for details on the many trails and roads through the property. Stay on trails and, if available, follow painted blazes and signs if available. Usually, double blazes mark an intersection or change in direction.\nDo.\nThe park is open to the public for hiking, camping, canoeing, kayaking, and non-motorized boating, cross-country skiing, fishing, hunting, mountain biking, off-road vehicles, and snowmobiling.\nHiking and off-roading trails.\nThere are numerous trails throughout the park, below are highlighted some of the most prominent:\nStay safe.\nAll of the park is available for hunting, so all visitors traversing the park should be vigilant. If on foot, wear a high-visibility jacket or vest during hunting season. See the hunting seasons for the state; October Mountain is in Zone 2. On the Appalachian Trail, hunting is not permitted and all firearms must be unloaded while on the trail. Dress appropriate to the weather. Layer for warmth in cooler and cold weather."}
{"id": "214925", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=214925", "title": "Abacha", "text": "Abacha is a prominent town within the Idemili North Local Government Area of Anambra State in South East Nigeria.\nUnderstand.\nLike many towns in the Igbo-speaking part of the country, Abacha is rich in culture, history, and tradition. It is a community that reflects the values, industriousness, and social cohesion of the Igbo people. Known for its vibrant festivals, skilled traders, and devoted residents, Abacha continues to maintain a unique identity while adapting to modern changes in Nigeria.\nHistory.\nThe history of Abacha Town is deeply rooted in Igbo tradition and culture. Like many Igbo communities, Abacha developed a governance system centered around chieftaincy, with leadership symbolized by the title of Igwe, or king. This traditional structure is supported by village councils, age-grade groups, and community elders, who collectively ensure social harmony, resolve disputes, and preserve cultural norms.\nIn modern times, Abacha\u2019s leadership has experienced significant events and changes. On January 9, 2021, Chief Nwabunwanne Godwin Odiegwu was crowned Ezedioramma Ikendim Abachaleku III, Igwe of Abacha, by community leaders and elders. His coronation followed the deposition of his predecessor, Igwe Godwin Chuba Mbakwe, by the then Willie Obiano Administration. The circumstances surrounding this transition became a subject of legal contest, as the matter was referred to a competent court to determine the legality of the government\u2019s actions.\nAbacha comprises five recognised villages: Umudisi, Umuazu, Umuokpolonwu, Umuekpeli,and Ugwuma. The village of Umuokpolonwu is further subdivided into two smaller communities: Umunneora and Umuaribo.\nClimate.\nAbacha has a typical Tropical Wet and Dry (Savanna) climate, characterized by hot, humid, and rainy wet seasons (April-October) with abundant rainfall and warm to hot, drier periods during the dry season (November-March). Expect high temperatures year-round (around 29 \u00b0C average), significant rainfall (over 200 mm annually), and a climate heavily impacted by changing weather patterns.\nGet around.\nThe people of this town can move around with their personal vehicles, tricycles which has become a more rampant means of transportation in the town.\nBuy.\nThe town is known for making African Salad which can be bought by their town members and outsiders."}
{"id": "214934", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=214934", "title": "Bruce Mines", "text": "Bruce Mines is a town in the Near North region of Northern Ontario.\nUnderstand.\nThe copper deposits of the region were first discovered by non-native settlers in 1846.\nAs of 2021, it had a population of 582 people.\nBuy.\nThe town has a grocery store, pharmacy, mechanic, an LCBO (liquor) outlet, and a single gas station. A hardware store is located 3km north of town, in the hamlet of Bruce Station."}
{"id": "214936", "revid": "2252661", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=214936", "title": "Thessalon", "text": "Thessalon is a town on the north shore of Lake Huron in the Near North region of Northern Ontario.\nUnderstand.\nThe town is a popular retirement community. As of 2021, it had a population of 1,260 people.\nThe area's timber resources drew Europeans to the area as early as 1869, with the first lumber camp being set up in 1870. By 1877 a more permanent townsite was established. The town was incorporated in 1892.\nThe origin of the town's name is unclear. \"Thessalon\" may be a corruption of a name given by local First Nations (Indigenous people), \"Neyashewun\", meaning \"a point of land\". It is also theorized that the town may have been named by Jesuit missionaries who compared their travels through the region to the plight of the Thessalonians described in the Pauline epistles. \nGet in.\nBy car.\nThessalon is on Highway 17 (Trans-Canada Highway), east of Sault Ste. Marie and west of Sudbury. It can also be reached from Chapleau via Highway 129.\nBuy.\nThe town has a grocery store, a pharmacy, a hardware store, and an LCBO (liquor) outlet."}
{"id": "214944", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=214944", "title": "Abagana", "text": "Abagana is in Anambra State in South East Nigeria.\nUnderstand.\nAbagana is the headquarters of Njikoka Local Government Area. It lies approximately 20 km from Onitsha along the old Enugu-Onitsha Trunk A Road that divides the town into two halves.\nHistory.\nAbagana is historically significant for its pivotal role in the Nigerian Civil War (Biafran War), especially the famous Abagana Ambush on March 31, 1968, where Biafran forces inflicted a devastating defeat on the Nigerian Army, boosting morale and halting their advance. Its origins trace back to ancestral tales of shared ancestry with neighboring towns like Nimo and Eziowelle, founded by figures like Owelle or Agana-Diese, though precise documentation is scarce. Today, it remains a vibrant cultural center, known for Igbo traditions and its tragic wartime history.\nClimate.\nAbagana has a warm, tropical climate with distinct wet and dry seasons, characterized by hot, humid conditions, high temperatures (65-87 F annually), oppressive heat and overcast skies in the wet season, and hot, muggy, partly cloudy weather in the dry season, typical for Anambra State's tropical savanna. \nGet in.\nPeople get into Abagana,primarily by road (driving, buses, taxis), as it's a significant location in the Njikoka Local Government Area, accessible via major routes connecting it to cities like Enugu and Onitsha, often through areas like the famous Abagana Ambush route (though that was a war event, it shows the strategic road access). You can drive, take public transport, or hire a private car to reach it from nearby towns or major hubs in Southeast Nigeria.\nGet around.\nIn Abagana, like many Nigerian towns, people get around using a mix of commercial motorbikes (okadas), taxis (keke napep), private cars, and by walking, especially for shorter distances, relying heavily on local transport systems that connect to major roads like the Enugu-Onitsha Expressway."}
{"id": "214945", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=214945", "title": "Abatete", "text": "Abatete is one of the historic and culturally significant towns in Idemili North Local Government Area of Anambra State, South East Nigeria. The town is known for its industrious and creative people, its rich cultural heritage, and its long history of resilience despite social and environmental challenges. \nUnderstand.\nIt shares borders with several important Igbo towns, including Ogidi, Uke, Umuoji, Abacha, Oraukwu, Alor, Ideani, Nimo Owelle, and Eziowelle. This strategic location places Abatete within the heart of the Idemili area, a region noted for its vibrancy in commerce, education, and cultural life.\nHistory.\nThe story of Abatete\u2019s origin begins with its ancestral founder, Okolie Otie, also known simply as Otie. Oral history says that Otie had three sons namely Ezeogu, Ora, and Ideke. From these sons came different lineages that spread across the Idemili region, linking Abatete with other neighboring towns like Umuoji and Nkpor. These family connections illustrate the deep kinship ties that form the foundation of Igbo identity, where communities often share bloodlines and historical ancestry.\nAbatete\u2019s early structure consisted of nine villages, which were believed to be descendants of Edeogu, the eldest son of Okolie Otie. These nine included Nsukwu, Agbaja, Isiuzo, Akwa, Azuogwa, Mputu, Odida, and Ogbu, among others. Over time, due to factors such as migration, wars, and population changes, only four major quarters survived and are recognized today. They are Nsukwu, Agbaja, Ogbu, and Odida. These quarters are now the backbone of Abatete\u2019s internal structure and social life.\nClimate.\nAbatete town experiences a tropical climate with distinct wet (April-October) and dry (November-March) seasons, characterized by high heat, humidity, and significant rainfall during the wet months, with temperatures generally ranging from the 70s to low 90s Fahrenheit (around 23\u00b0C to 30\u00b0C+), typical of Nigeria's equatorial/tropical zone, with forecasts often showing daily thunderstorms and high chances of rain in season.\nGet in.\nTo get to Abatete town, you'll travel to Anambra State, Nigeria, specifically the Idemili North Local Government Area, and since it's surrounded by towns like Ogidi, Uke, Umuoji, and Oraukwu, you'd typically drive or take a bus/taxi from major hubs like Onitsha or Nnewi, following signs to Idemili North and then asking for directions to Abatete.\nGet around.\nPeople primarily get around using motorcycles (Okadas) for quick trips, taxis (or private cars) for more comfort, and walking for short distances within villages, often using local buses or shared transport for longer routes between its villages (Agbaja, Nsukwu, Ogbu, Odida) or to nearby towns like Ogidi or Nkpor in Idemili North LGA, Anambra State.\nSee.\nIn Abatete, you'll find a mix of local life, food spots like Mighty Bar &amp; Restaurant (Bush Meat Centre), lively nightlife at places like Fusion Nightclub, cultural heritage rooted in its four villages (Agbaja, Nsukwu, Ogbu, Odida), and nearby attractions such as Ogbunike Cave, Odinani Museum in Nri, and spiritual sites like Holy Ghost Adoration Ministry Uke, showcasing rich Igbo traditions and modern hangouts.\nBuy.\nIn Abatete, you can buy agricultural products like yams, cassava, maize, and fresh vegetables, as it's a farming community. Also, expect to find typical Nigerian goods such as vibrant Ankara fabrics, beaded jewelry, leather items, and local crafts at markets, alongside daily essentials."}
{"id": "214947", "revid": "2432955", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=214947", "title": "Achina", "text": "Achina is in Anambra State, South East Nigeria. Achina is a living monument of Igbo heritage, ancient migration, and enduring values, with roots that stretch back over a thousand years. \nUnderstand.\nAchina town is made up of three villages, Ebele; Umueleke and Umueziyi. Achina has common boundaries with Akpo, Umuchu, Onneh, Ogboji, and Enugwu-Umuonyia.\nHistory.\nAchina\u2019s origin can be traced back over a thousand years, placing it firmly within the historical area influenced by the Nri civilization, one of the oldest and most refined Igbo cultural systems. The exquisite bronzes found in nearby Igbo-Ukwu stand as proof of this ancient heritage. These artifacts reflect advanced metalwork, sophisticated spirituality, and a structured social system that existed long before colonial contact.\nAccording to oral tradition, the early settlers came from the Okigwe escarpment, just like many of their neighbors. These were groups of hunters, farmers, and families in search of arable land, safety, and spiritual harmony. In time, it welcomed other migrants from different parts of Igboland, creating a blend of lineages and traditions.\nIn fact, some of its own people later ventured outward and helped establish other towns, most notably Urualla in what is now Imo State. This connection is not just historical, it is also cultural, visible in shared customs and language patterns.\nClimate.\nAchina has a tropical savanna climate. The district's average annual temperature is 30\u00b0C (87\u00b0F).\nGet in.\nPeople get to Achina by road, usually driving from major hubs like Onitsha, Awka, or Enugu, connecting through other towns like Umuchu or Akpo, using local buses (molues/danfo), private cars, or taxis, as it's a typical Igbo community located within the Orumba South LGA, accessed via local road networks. \nGet around.\nIn Achina town, people get around using a mix of personal vehicles (cars, motorcycles/Okadas), public transport like shared taxis (Keke napep/tricycles &amp; buses), and walking, especially for short distances within its villages (Ebele, Umueleke, Umueziyi), leveraging local roads connecting to neighbors like Umuchu and Onneh, typical of many Nigerian towns.\nBuy.\nIt is a busy local market where you can purchase a wide range of items, particularly food items and various goods for daily needs. It serves as a combined market for both Achina and the neighboring town of Akpo.The market is open on most weekdays, typically from 8 AM to 5 PM, but it is notably closed on Saturdays and Sundays. The day of \"Orie\" is a particularly busy market day."}
{"id": "214953", "revid": "2437150", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=214953", "title": "Enugwu-Ukwu", "text": "Enugwu-Ukwu is a rural community in Njikoka Local Government Area in Anambra State.\nUnderstand.\nEnugwu-Ukwu is one of the historic and culturally significant towns in Njikoka Local Government Area of Anambra State, Nigeria. The town is known for its historic and cultural background as well as it's industrious and creative people. It shares borders with several important Igbo towns; Agukwu nri, Abagana, Nawfia, Nise and Nimo. Enugwu-Ukwu is noted for its vibrancy in commerce, education, and cultural life. Enugwu-Ukwu have 18 villages which includes Osili, Awuvu, Avo-omimi, Orji, Umuakwu, Umuatulu, Akiyi, Enuagu, Urualor, Umuokpalanri, Uruokwe, Uruokwe (Nkw\u1ecd), Urukpeleke and others. \nClimate.\nEnugwu-Ukwu has a typical Tropical Wet and Dry (Savanna) climate, characterized by hot, humid, and rainy wet seasons (April-October) with abundant rainfall and warm to hot, drier periods during the dry season (November-March). Expect high temperatures year-round (around 29\u00b0C average), significant rainfall (over 200mm annually), and a climate heavily impacted by changing weather patterns.\nGet in.\nThe town can be accessed by Air through Chin\u1ee5a Achebe Airport or by Land through any of the transport services. From Onitsha, drive through Nkpor, Ogidi, Eziowelle and Abagana to get to Enugwu-Ukwu by foreigners,visitors and indegenes of Anambra State.\nGet around.\nThe people of this community can move around with their personal vehicles, tricycles popularly known as Keke which has become a more rampant means of transportation in the town, there are also shuttle buses and motorcycles also known as okada. The motorcycle riders stay at strategic points in the community, especially around Nkw\u1ecd market and Eze Eleyas junction.\nBuy.\nNkw\u1ecd Enugwu-Ukwu is a popular market situated in the middle of the village near Ring road along Enugwu-Ukwu and Agukwu nri road. People from neighbouring communities come to the market on nkw\u1ecd data to buy and sell their wares especially farm produce.\nEat.\nThere are many eateries and bukkas where varieties of dishes are sold as well as local dishes.\nDrink.\nThere are bush bars in Enugwu-Ukwu where drinks of different types and local gin are sold."}
{"id": "214954", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=214954", "title": "Nri", "text": "Nri is a rural community in Anaocha Local Government Area of Anambra State.\nUnderstand.\nNri is an ancient community in Igboland. It is believed that Agukwu nri is the ancestral home of Igbos. It is one of the historic and culturally significant communities in Anaocha Local Government Area of Anambra State, Nigeria. The community is known for its industrious and creative people, its rich cultural heritage, and its long history of resilience despite social and environmental challenges. It shares borders with several important Igbo towns, including Nawfia, Abagana, Enugwu-Ukwu. Nri community is a region noted for its vibrancy in commerce, education, and cultural life.\nClimate.\nAgukwu nri village experiences a tropical climate with distinct wet (April-October) and dry (November-March) seasons, characterized by high heat, humidity, and significant rainfall during the wet months, with temperatures generally ranging from the 70s to low 90s Fahrenheit (around 23\u00b0C to 30\u00b0C+), typical of Nigeria's equatorial/tropical zone, with forecasts often showing daily thunderstorms and high chances of rain in season.\nGet in.\nThe town can be accessed by Air through Chin\u1ee5a Achebe Airport or by Land through any of the transport services. From Onitsha, drive through Nkpor, Ogidi, Eziowelle, Abagana and Enugwu-Ukwu to get to Agukwu Nri by foreigners, visitors and indegenes of Anambra State.\nGet around.\nThe people of this town can move around with their personal vehicles. Tricycles, popularly known as Keke, have become a more rampant means of transportation in the town. There are also shuttle buses and motorcycles, which are also known as okada. The motorcycle riders stay at strategic points in the community, especially around Eze Eleyas junction and the market square.\nSee and do.\nThe New Yam Festival (Iri Ji) is the most prominent. Celebrated annually, it marks the beginning of yam harvesting, one of the town\u2019s most important crops. During the festival, the Igwe or elders bless the new yams before they are eaten, symbolizing gratitude to the gods and ancestors for a bountiful harvest. Feasting, dancing, and communal prayers are common, and residents often invite friends and family to join in the celebrations."}
{"id": "214956", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=214956", "title": "Agulu", "text": "Agulu is Anambra State in South East Nigeria. The culturally sacred and crocodile-filled Agulu Lake is a major tourist draw, making the town a mix of tradition and modernity with developing infrastructure.\nUnderstand.\nAgulu is a significant, vibrant town in Anaocha Local Government Area, known for its deep cultural roots, prominent figures in politics/academia, and economic growth.\nHistory.\nThe history of Agulu is deeply rooted in Igbo tradition and oral accounts passed down from elders. Like many towns in Anambra, Agulu traces its origin to ancient Igbo settlements, with its people believed to have descended from early migrants who settled in the fertile lands of the Anambra valley. Oral traditions suggest that Agulu, Nri, Adazi, and other neighboring towns share ancestral links, which explains their close cultural and social interactions. The 20 villages of Agulu are: Nneogidi, Okpu, Ama-Ezike, Umuowelle\nAmawbia, Agulu, Amatutu, Okpuifite, Okpuenu, Amaeze, Nneogwu, Agulu-Agu, Okpu-Agulu, Okpuani, Okpuike, Amaezike-Agulu, Okpu-Ezi, Amaikpa, Okpu-Ifite Agulu, Umuokwu, Okpuenu-Ifite.\nEach village contributes members to age-grade associations, women\u2019s groups, and youth organizations that collectively handle matters of security, development, and cultural events. For example, when festivals such as the New Yam Festival or masquerade parades are organized, each village plays its part by providing dancers, masquerades, or food items to ensure success.\nClimate.\nAgulu has a tropical climate with distinct wet (Apr-Nov, warm/oppressive) and dry (Nov-Mar, hot/muggy) seasons, hot temperatures year-round (24\u00b0C-37\u00b0C,or 75\u00b0F-98\u00b0F), high humidity, and significant rainfall during the wet season, making it suitable for farming but requiring preparation for heat and heavy rains.\nGet in.\nTo get to Agulu town, you typically drive or take a bus/taxi from nearby major cities like Enugu or Awka, usually connecting via major roads to reach the town's center, with common drop-off points near the Agulu Lake or main bridges, from where local transport (keke/okada) takes you further in. \nGet around.\nTo get around Agulu town in Anambra State, you'll rely on local transport like motorcycles (Okadas), tricycles (Keke Napep) for shorter trips, and possibly taxis or private cars, as it's a sizeable town, but expect local, often bustling, Nigerian transport systems, especially if you're near the main roads or markets.\nSee.\nthe Agulu Lake, which is one of the most famous natural landmarks in Anambra State. Located on the Agulu\u2013Nanka road, the lake is both a cultural and environmental treasure.Traditionally, the lake is believed to house spiritual forces that protect the town. Taboos once guided the use of the lake, forbidding indiscriminate fishing or pollution. Elders recount stories of how the lake avenged wrongdoing or protected the community during crises.\nBuy.\nIn Agulu town, you can buy everyday essentials and local specialties like bananas, groundnuts, bread, okpa, and fresh produce at markets like the new Solution Arena at Nwagu Junction, alongside staples from supermarkets,"}
{"id": "214966", "revid": "2253620", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=214966", "title": "Val di Noto Wine Region", "text": "The Val di Noto Wine Region is a winemaking area in south-eastern Sicily, covering parts of the provinces of Syracuse and Ragusa. It includes wine-producing areas around Noto, Pachino, Avola, Vittoria, and Chiaramonte Gulfi. The region encompasses several DOC zones and is characterised by rolling limestone hills, inland plateaus, and coastal plains, with wineries offering tastings, cellar visits, and rural accommodation.\nUnderstand.\nThe Val di Noto Wine Region occupies the rural areas of south-eastern Sicily, where vineyards form part of a broader agricultural landscape that also includes olive groves, citrus orchards and arable land. Wine production is mainly small- to medium-scale, with many wineries operating from working farms rather than purpose-built visitor centres.\nWine visits are typically arranged by appointment and are often combined with food tastings or meals. The region is primarily associated with red wines based on Nero d\u2019Avola and Frappato, though white, ros\u00e9 and sweet wines are also produced, particularly in coastal areas.\nThe limestone hills and inland plains contrast with Sicily\u2019s volcanic wine zones and allow visitors to combine winery visits with nearby historic towns such as Noto, Ragusa and Modica. A car is generally required, as wineries are widely dispersed and public transport in rural areas is limited.\nFor orientation, wine labels in the region refer to several local DOC zones (Denominazione di Origine Controllata), an Italian quality system that links wine styles to specific areas and production rules. In Val di Noto, these include Vittoria, Noto, Eloro, Riesi and Siracusa. The region is also home to Cerasuolo di Vittoria DOCG, Sicily\u2019s only DOCG designation, which represents the highest level in Italy\u2019s classification system. For travellers, these names are best understood as guides to regional identity and typical wine styles rather than guarantees of quality or price.\nGet in.\nThe Val di Noto Wine Region is accessible by air, road, and rail, though reaching wineries in rural areas generally requires a car. Coastal towns and major centres are well connected, while inland wine areas are more dispersed.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest airports are:\nFrom both airports, car rental is widely available.\nBy train and bus.\nRail and bus services connect the main towns of the region, including Noto, Syracuse, Ragusa, and Modica.\nPublic transport is suitable for reaching towns but is generally impractical for visiting wineries directly.\nBy car.\nA car provides the greatest flexibility for exploring the Val di Noto Wine Region, particularly for visiting vineyards and rural estates. The A18 motorway links Catania with Syracuse, while state roads connect inland towns. Some rural roads are narrow, winding, or poorly lit, especially in hill areas, and driving times may be longer than expected.\nGet around.\nGuided wine tours operate throughout the region and typically include transportation and winery visits. Tours usually depart from Trapani, Palermo, or nearby airports and should be booked in advance during the summer season.\nStay safe.\nAvoid drinking and driving when visiting wineries. Rural roads may be narrow and poorly lit, and summer heat can be intense."}
{"id": "214983", "revid": "2252661", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=214983", "title": "Chapleau", "text": "Chapleau is in the Lake Superior North Shore region of Northern Ontario. A small and somewhat remote township of around 1,900 people, it is the starting point for exploring some of the most pristine natural settings in the north, including the largest game preserve in the world.\nUnderstand.\nChapleau owes its existence to the Canadian Pacific Railway. When the rail line was built through the area in 1885, Chapleau was chosen as a divisional point, and the town formed around it."}
{"id": "214987", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=214987", "title": "Achi", "text": "Achi (\u963f\u667a\u6751 Achi mura) is a village in Nagano, with a population of about 6,000 people (2020). It is known as one of the best places in Japan for stargazing, owing to its low light pollution.\nUnderstand.\nTourist information.\nThere really is very little tourism information in English. The Japanese National Tourism Agency has a (very) simple article about Achi here. Achi village has a Japanese-only official website, and an automatic machine-translated version can be found here (translation quality may vary)."}
{"id": "214993", "revid": "2332920", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=214993", "title": "U.S. Highway 101", "text": "U.S. Highway 101 (US-101) is a major north-south U.S. highway on the west coast. This itinerary will take you the entire 1,535 mi (2,471 km) from Los Angeles, California to Tumwater, Washington. \nUnderstand.\nUS-101 does not serve major cities like Portland or Seattle. Those cities are instead served by Interstate 5, a more inland route. However, US-101 runs along the coast, connecting other major cities like San Francisco and San Jose.\nPrepare.\nUS-101 starts in really dense suburban areas, but after San Francisco and Santa Rosa, there is not many cities over 10,000 residents. Instead of heading inland to big cities like Redding, Eugene, or Portland, it instead stays on the coast to smaller but historical cities like Eureka, Crescent City, and Tillamook. Be prepared and stock up on food and gas before leaving a city.\nBe weary of rain, as they can make the road slippery.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nAll codes in parentheses are IATA codes, unless otherwise stated.\nGo.\nBy train.\nThe closest train route to US-101 is the Coast Starlight, running from Los Angeles to Seattle.\nDrive.\n\"Note: State highways are referenced by their postal abbreviations, e.g., CA-60 means California State Highway 60, MS-15 means Mississippi State Highway 15, etc. Attractions and cities are listed in bold are linked to their respective Wikivoyage articles for further reading and discussion. If no such article exists on Wikivoyage it is linked to its Wikipedia article. Please feel free to plunge forward to edit this or another article or create a that does not yet exist.\"\nThis itinerary will take you south-north. Exit numbers may be different going north-south.\nDay 1: Los Angeles to Salinas.\nRoute.\nFrom, US-101 starts at the East Los Angeles Interchange with, and \"(Mile 0.0)\". Immediately after, you hit the interchange with \"(Exit 3)\", which leads to, the home of the Los Angeles Dodgers baseball team. There is an exit with \"(Exit 7)\" where you can hop on at the western edge of the historic highway. Soon, you must keep right to stay on US-101 at the interchange with and \"(Exit 13)\" in, with the right three lanes leading to US-101. In the LA suburb of, you will reach \"(Exit 18)\". You will then pass by the cities and suburbs of,, and before an interchange with \"(Exit 70B)\" to the San Joaquin Valley. US-101 then rides along the coast, passing by the cities of, and before cutting inland and ending the freeway section with a couple of intersections. The is a rest stop with parking, vending machines, and direct access to. US-101 has an interchange with the \"(Exit 132)\" in the unincorporated community of Las Cruces. US-101 stays north, heading towards the cities of and before riding the coast in the cities of and before going inland towards, the county seat of San Luis Obispo County. It then runs parallel to the Salinas River, where the drive starts becoming rural. You will still find some cities, including,, and, before you arrive in, where you can stop for the day.\nDay 2: Salinas to Ukiah.\nRoute.\nGetting back on the road, US-101 shifts inland to,, and before reaching the so-called \"Silicon Valley\" with its most populated city. In San Jose, it has major interchanges with \"(Exit 377A)\", \"(Exit 384)\", \"(Exit 388B)\", and \"(Exit 396C).\" It then turns back to the coast, running up the Bay Area Peninsula with the cities of,, and before entering the cultural center of Northern California. There, you hit your first traffic lights along the route, as the road runs as Van Ness Avenue and Lombard Street after an interchange with \"(Exit 433B, southbound exit on the left)\". You can visit places like Fisherman's Wharf, Pier 39, or one that is directly along your route, the. Tolls on the golden gate bridge can cost anywhere from $9.75 with a FasTrak card to $10.75 for a toll invoice with a normal, 2-axle car. Carpools with a FasTrak Flex card cost $7.75. Across the Bay, you will reach the cities of,, and before splitting off from the coast for the rest of the day (it will rejoin the coast near Eureka). You then enter wine country with the city of, probably the last city along your path with more than 100,000 people. In Santa Rosa, there is an interchange with \"(Exit 488B)\", a major east-west highway in California. is the next city on the route.The highway then enters Mendocino County, with the historic towns of and, where you can rest for the day. \nDay 3: Ukiah to Crescent City.\nRoute.\nFrom Ukiah, US-101 has an interchange with \"(Exit 555B)\" that goes to Yuba City. US-101 then starts going towards the coast, meeting with the city of, with an interchange with the other side of \"(Exit 568)\". A few miles later, there is an intersection with that goes to Willows. The road then turns left towards the city of, with the end of the Pacific Coast Highway (CA-1). US-101 then starts following (most of) the bends of the South Fork Eel River, passing by the small towns of,, and. You then meet with the biggest cities of the day, and, with a metro population of 132,380. Pretty much your last city on the day is until you reach.\nStay safe.\nUS-101 is known to have dense coastal fog, causing low visibility and major pileups. Roads are also known to be slick, made even worse by the fact that most of the affected route is a 2-lane undivided road winding through the Redwood forests."}
