{"id": "214997", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=214997", "title": "Maipu", "text": "Maipu is a city in Mendoza Province, Argentina, and the administrative centre of Maipu Department. It forms part of Greater Mendoza and lies southeast of the city of Mendoza.\nThe city and surrounding department are particularly known for wine production and olive oil. Maipu is home to historic wineries, modern wine projects, vineyards, olive groves, restaurants, museums and other attractions connected with the region's wine and olive-growing traditions.\nFor travellers, Maipu is one of the main wine-tourism destinations in Mendoza. Wineries and other attractions are spread across several districts of the department, so it is worth planning transportation according to the places you intend to visit.\nAccording to Argentina's 2022 National Population, Household and Housing Census, Maipu Department had a population of 219,402. Source: Argentina.gob.ar\nUnderstand.\nMaipu Department was established on 14 May 1858. Its history has been closely connected with agriculture and, later, with the expansion of wine and olive production in Mendoza.\nDuring the 19th and early 20th centuries, numerous wineries and agricultural estates were established in the area. European immigrants contributed to the development of viticulture and introduced new winemaking techniques.\nOne of the most important chapters in Maipu's wine history is associated with Juan Giol and Bautista Gargantini. The company they developed in Maipu became one of the largest wine enterprises of its time, and the former Giol winery came to be regarded as the world's largest winery by production scale.\nIn 1910, one of its large 75,000-litre wine vats received first prize as the world's largest wine vessel at the Centennial Exhibition.\nWine production remains an important part of the department's economy and identity. A provincial survey using 2023 data recorded 121 registered wineries in Maipu and 42 wineries open to tourism. These figures refer to different groups: the first includes establishments registered as wine producers, while the second refers to wineries that receive visitors.\nThe wine-tourism offer is complemented by olive-oil producers, restaurants and gastronomic experiences.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe usual airport for visitors arriving in Mendoza is Governor Francisco Gabrielli International Airport, also known as El Plumerillo, in the metropolitan area.\nFrom the airport, Maipu can be reached by car, taxi, rental vehicle or public transport.\nBy car.\nMaipu is part of the Mendoza metropolitan area and is connected to Mendoza city by the main metropolitan roads.\nIf visiting wineries in different districts of the department, it is advisable to plan the route in advance and check the directions provided by each establishment.\nBy bus.\nPublic transport services connect Maipu with Mendoza city and other parts of Greater Mendoza.\nFor visiting several wineries in one day, public transport may be less convenient than a car, hired transfer, organised excursion or bicycle.\nGet around.\nThe centre of Maipu can be explored on foot, but many wineries, olive-oil producers and tourist restaurants are spread across different districts.\nThe main options for getting around are:\nDistances between wineries should be checked before setting out. Opening hours and reservation requirements should also be confirmed with each establishment.\nSee.\nFormer Giol Winery.\nThe former Giol Winery is part of Maipu's industrial and wine heritage. The company established by Juan Giol and Bautista Gargantini reached an extraordinary scale in the early 20th century and came to be regarded as the world's largest winery.\nOne of its best-known historic objects is a 75,000-litre wine vat that received first prize as the world's largest wine vessel in 1910.\nThe historic complex associated with Giol and Gargantini illustrates the scale reached by Maipu's wine industry during this period.\nDo.\nWine tourism.\nWine is one of the main reasons travellers visit Maipu. The department combines historic wineries, family-owned producers, contemporary wine projects, restaurants and tasting experiences.\nSome of Maipu's best-known wineries include:\nExploring the wineries.\nMaipu's wineries can be explored in several ways. The best option depends on the amount of time available, budget, distances between establishments and the type of experience sought.\nOrganised excursions.\nOrganised excursions can combine several wineries while taking care of transportation and reservations. They are a practical option for travellers who prefer a planned itinerary.\nBy car.\nA car makes it possible to combine establishments in different parts of the department. Anyone taking part in wine tastings should arrange a safe way to return and must not drive after drinking alcohol.\nBy bicycle.\nExploring the wineries by bicycle is a common tourist activity in Maipu's wine region. It allows visitors to combine wineries, vineyards, olive groves and restaurants while giving them more flexibility over the route.\nSeveral companies rent bicycles and provide maps, route advice, self-guided itineraries or guided tours. One of them is Maipu Bikes, based in Coquimbito, which offers bicycle rental and routes through the wine region.\nBefore setting out, check the distances, winery opening hours and reservation requirements.\nOlive growing.\nMaipu also has a long tradition of olive growing. Some olive-oil producers offer visits, tastings and regional products.\nSome tourism experiences combine wine and olive oil in the same itinerary.\nFood and wine.\nSeveral wineries have their own restaurants and gastronomic programmes. Menus commonly include regional dishes, meat, seasonal produce, olive oil and locally produced wines.\nWinery dining experiences often require advance reservations.\nEvents.\nThroughout the year, Maipu hosts activities related to the grape harvest, wine, olive oil, gastronomy, music and local heritage.\nEvents vary according to the season, so it is advisable to check the current tourism calendar before travelling.\nLearn.\nMaipu is a good place to learn about the history of Argentine winemaking.\nWinery visits can introduce visitors to different stages of the production process and the differences between traditional wineries and contemporary wine projects.\nThe Museo Nacional del Vino y la Vendimia and the Wine Museum at Bodega La Rural also provide an insight into the historical and technical heritage of the wine industry.\nWork.\nMaipu forms part of Greater Mendoza and has agricultural, industrial, commercial and service activities.\nFor longer stays, check accommodation and transport options in the city and surrounding districts.\nBuy.\nTypical products to buy in Maipu include:\nMany wineries sell their products directly to visitors.\nEat.\nMaipu's cuisine is closely connected with wine, olive oil and regional products.\nIn addition to independent restaurants, several wineries offer lunches and tasting menus.\nReservations are advisable when dining at a winery, particularly on weekends and during busy periods.\nDrink and go out.\nWine tourism is the main activity related to alcoholic drinks in Maipu. Wineries offer tastings, and some combine wine with food.\nThe centre of Maipu also has bars, restaurants and other places to eat and drink.\nWinery and restaurant opening hours may vary according to the day of the week and season.\nSleep.\nMaipu has hotels, guesthouses, rural accommodation and other lodging options.\nStaying within the department can be convenient for travellers who want to spend more than one day visiting wineries and other attractions.\nStay safe.\nMaipu combines urban areas with rural and agricultural districts.\nAs in other tourist destinations, keep personal belongings secure, pay attention to traffic and take normal precautions when travelling through less populated areas.\nRural roads may be shared with agricultural machinery and other vehicles. Take particular care when cycling or driving in these areas.\nAnyone taking part in wine tastings should arrange transportation for the return journey in advance.\nFor general emergencies, call 911.\nStay healthy.\nMedical emergencies can be reported by calling 107 or 911.\nTravellers should consider appropriate travel medical insurance and carry any regular medication they need.\nContact.\nFor up-to-date tourism information about Mendoza, its destinations, activities and tourism providers, consult Mendoza Tourism.\nThe official Maipu information page on Argentina.gob.ar also provides information about the department."}
{"id": "215000", "revid": "2183461", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=215000", "title": "Benisheikh", "text": "Benisheikh (also spelled Beni Sheik or Beni Sheikh) is a small town in Kaga Local Government Area of Borno State, in northeastern Nigeria. It lies along the important Maiduguri\u2013Damaturu highway and serves as a key transit point between Borno and Yobe States.\nAlthough modest in size, Benisheikh is well known for its strategic location and its role as a security and transportation checkpoint in the region.\nUnderstand.\nBenisheikh sits about 70 km west of Maiduguri, Borno State's capital. The town faced major issues from the Boko Haram conflict, which hurt daily life, business, and travel. Security has gotten better lately, but the area is still delicate.Most people are Kanuri, and Hausa is commonly spoken. Islam is the primary religion, and Kanuri traditions shape everyday life.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThere is no airport in Benisheikh. The nearest airport is in Maiduguri International Airport. From there, road transport is required.\nGet around.\nBenisheikh is a small town, and most places can be reached on foot. Motorcycles (okada) are commonly used for short distances within the town, depending on local security regulations.\nDrink.\nNon-alcoholic drinks are widely available, including\nSleep.\nAccommodation options are very limited.\nVisitors should not expect hotels with modern amenities.\nStay safe.\nSecurity conditions can change quickly."}
{"id": "215006", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=215006", "title": "Omura", "text": "Omura (\u5927\u6751\u5e02 \u014cmura Shi) is a city in Nagasaki on the eastern shore of \u014cmura Bay, at the western foot of the Tara mountain range.\nUnderstand.\nno population data for this page"}
{"id": "215008", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=215008", "title": "Amta", "text": "Amta (\u0986\u09ae\u09a4\u09be \u0100mt\u0101) is a town in the Howrah district of West Bengal, along the Damodar River. It is known for Melai Chandi Mandir, a Kali temple and a major pilgrimage centre in the area."}
{"id": "215034", "revid": "1298114", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=215034", "title": "Yang Highland Wildlife Sanctuary", "text": "Yang Highland Wildlife Sanctuary (or \"Hyang Mountains Wildlife Sanctuary\") a wildlife sanctuary in Java Island.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nYang Highland Wildlife Sanctuary is one of conservation area in East Java, encompassing a 14,177-Ha highlands area. It was inaugurated as a wildlife reserve on 5 May 1962.\nFlora and fauna.\nMammals:\nLeopard (\"Panthera pardus\"), Javan Langur (\"Tracypithecus auratus\"), Timor Deer (\"Cervus timorensis\"), Wild Boar (\"Sus scrofa\"), Muntjak Deer (\"Muntiacus muntjak\"), and Leopard Cat (\"Felis bengalensis\").\nBirds:\nJavan Hawk-eagle (\"Nisaetus bartelsi\"), Black Eagle (\"Ictinaetus malayensis\"), Crested Serpent Eagle (\"Spilornis cheela\"), Jungle Fowls (\"Gallus varius\"), Peafowl (\"Pavo muticus\").\nDo.\nClimbing Mount Argopuro.\n has a peak with an altitude of 3088 meters above sea level. You can go to in Krucil District of Probolinggo to start hiking, or from in Sumbermalang District of Situbondo.\nRespect.\nThere are local community activities after harvest time, including a thanksgiving ceremony at the top of Mount Argopuro."}
{"id": "215037", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=215037", "title": "Santes Creus", "text": "Santes Creus is a hamlet of Aiguam\u00farcia in Alt Camp county of Tarragona (province). The main visit is the Santes Creus monastery (\"Holy crosses monastery\"), which is part of Cister Route, that links together the three Cistercian monasteries of Santes Creus, Poblet and Vallbona. Also is a stage in the Catalan Way of St. James\nGet around.\nSmall enough to walk.\nDo.\nRuta del Cister.\nGetting There and Preparation.\nStart in Montblanc, a medieval walled town accessible by train from Barcelona (R14 line, ~2.5 hours). From Poblet Natural Park, Montblanc is just 12 km away on foot. Obtain the Cistercian Route Pass for discounted entry to all three monasteries. Pack comfortable hiking shoes, water, and weather gear\u2014summers are hot, winters cool and rainy. Accommodations range from pilgrim hostels to rural hotels in villages like L'Espluga de Francol\u00ed and Rocafort de Queralt. The full GR-175 trail takes 5-7 days; shorter car tours focus on monasteries.\nStage 1: Montblanc to Poblet (12 km, \u2191550m \u2193400m.\nBegin in Montblanc's historic center with its Gothic Santa Maria church and town walls. Head north through Conca de Barber\u00e0's rolling vineyards and olive groves into the wooded Prades Mountains within Poblet Natural Park. The trail winds past rural farms, offering views of cork oaks and almond trees. Arrive at Real Monasterio de Santa Mar\u00eda de Poblet, a UNESCO World Heritage Site since 1991. This vast 12th-century complex, still home to Cistercian monks, features a Romanesque-Gothic church with royal tombs (including Aragonese kings), a stunning cloister, chapter house, and kitchen. Join evening vespers for Gregorian chants. Stay at Hostatgeria de Poblet or nearby guesthouses.\nStage 2: Poblet to Vallbona de les Monges (25 km).\nDepart Poblet eastward across farmland and Serra del Tallat ridges with panoramic views. Pass through quiet hamlets like Blancafort and Solivella, showcasing dry-stone (sec\u00e0) architecture. The path levels into Urgell's flatlands dotted with cereals. Reach Monasterio de Santa Mar\u00eda de Vallbona, founded in 1150 for Cistercian nuns (still active today). Highlights include its 14th-century rose window, Romanesque cloister, and Gothic dormitory. Experience contemplative vespers in this intimate setting. lodging Vallbona de les Monges Monestir lodging or Transfer to Rocallaura or T\u00e0rrega for lodging amid vineyards.\nStage 3: Vallbona de les Monges to Rocafort de Queralt (22 km).\nFrom Vallbona, traverse almond groves and low hills toward Rocafort de Queralt, a wine village in Pened\u00e8s DO. Sample local cava at cooperatives. The stage is gentle, passing rural tracks with birdwatching opportunities. Explore the village's castle ruins and Baroque church. Overnight in nearby Belltall or Vallverd rural hotels.\nStage 4: Rocafort de Queralt to Santes Creus (24 km).\nDescend Gai\u00e0 River Valley through terraced vineyards and wooded slopes. Arrive at Monasterio de Santa Mar\u00eda de Santes Creus in Aiguam\u00farcia, founded 1168. This golden-limestone gem features a simple Cistercian church, elegant cloister, and royal pantheon. Less crowded than Poblet, it offers serene self-guided tours. Lodging in Santes Creus lodging section or nearby.\nStage 5: Santes Creus to Montblanc (25 km, \u2191578m \u2193549m).\nClimb gently through groves back to Montblanc, crossing Conca Plain with final vineyard vistas. End with a stroll along Montblanc's 14th-century walls and towers. Celebrate with local cuisine like escudella stew or Priorat wines.\nKey Places and Attractions.\nMonasteries: Poblet (grandest, monks' chants), Vallbona (nuns, rose window), Santes Creus (intimate cloisters). All emphasize Cistercian austerity: white habits, self-sufficiency via agriculture.\nNatural Highlights: Prades Mountains trails, Poblet Natural Park forests, river gorges for birding (eagles, vultures).\nGastronomy: Conca de Barber\u00e0 wines, cheeses, honey, olive oil tastings.\nPractical Tips.\nDifficulty: Moderate (daily 400-550m elevation). Best May-October; avoid midday heat. GR-175 markers guide the way. Free audioguides at monasteries. Combine with Poblet Natural Park hikes for wildlife. Families can drive the circuit in 1-2 days. Sustainable tourism: support local farms, respect trails. This route immerses you in Catalonia's medieval soul amid stunning inland scenery.\nBuy.\nCarquinyolis are traditional Catalan cookies that are very dry, crisp, and full of almonds. They are typically made from a simple dough of flour, sugar, eggs, and whole almonds, baked in a log and then sliced into small, crunchy pieces. Usually enjoyed with coffee, sweet wine, or hot chocolate. \nEat.\nCal\u00e7otada is a full meal, available from Christmas until mid April, based on the cal\u00e7ot, a kind of young onion grilled and eaten with a sauce. Usually the dish comprises assorted grilled meats, and some dessert after that. This is usually a group meal, but is also available for couples or families.\nConnect.\nAs of April 2026, Santes Creus and its approach roads have 4G from all Spanish carriers. There's 5G along Highway AP-2 to the south, but it hasn't reached up the valley."}
{"id": "215040", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=215040", "title": "Versailles (Kentucky)", "text": "Versailles is in the Bluegrass Region of Kentucky.\nUnderstand.\nVersailles is a small town about half an hour's drive west of Lexington. It's known for the Woodford Reserve whiskey distillery and is in the heart of Kentucky's Bourbon country.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nThe Bluegrass Parkway bypasses Versailles on its southern side, with Exit 68 (KY-33, aka Troy Pike) becoming the Main Street to the north of the parkway. US-60 connects Frankfort and I-64 with the town from the north."}
