{"id": "215201", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=215201", "title": "Dasghara", "text": "Dasghara (\u09a6\u09b6\u0998\u09b0\u09be \"Doshghor\u0101\") is a heritage town in the Hooghly district of West Bengal, best known for the Biswas zamindar family native to the town.\nGet in.\nBy bus.\nBus routes 17 (Chinsurah Court-Tarakeswar), 18A (Dasghara-Haripal Station), and 23 (Chinsurah Court-Tarakeswar) serve Dasghara.\nBy car.\nDasghara is on SH 15, 12 km from Tarakeswar.\nGet around.\nOn foot.\nPractically the only option for main attractions in Dasghara."}
{"id": "215208", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=215208", "title": "Vallbona de les Monges", "text": "Vallbona de les Monges is in Lleida (province). In the town there is the most important female cistercenc monastery in Catalonia: Santa Maria de Vallbona, and together with the Monestir of Poblet and the Monestir of Santes Creus they make up the Cistercian Route. Also is a stage in the Catalan Way of St. James\nUnderstand.\nVallbona de les Monges is in the south of the Urgell county, about 25 km from the capital, T\u00e0rrega, in one of the valleys between the Serra del Tallat and the Vall del Corb, just at the provincial limit of the Terres de Lleida and the Camp de Tarragona. Dry stone huts, cereal fields, vineyards, almond tree and olive tree fields predominate the landscape.\nThe founder of the town of Vallbona de les Monges was an abbess of the monastery, Estefania de Piquer who, in order to avoid the agreements of the Council of Trent which stipulated that female monasteries could not be located in uninhabited places or outside the walls, made a donation of the lands and buildings around the monastery. In this way the town was born and the first mayor was appointed in 1573.\nGet around.\nSmall enough to walk."}
{"id": "215229", "revid": "2432955", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=215229", "title": "Isla Salamanca Parkway", "text": "Isla Salamanca Parkway (officially, \"V\u00eda Parque Isla de Salamanca\") is a protected natural area along the Caribbean coast in the Magdalena Department in northern Colombia. It is also an important transportation corridor, linking coastal cities via a well-maintained parkway that attracts tourists who come for the ocean views and to see the mangrove swamps and inland lagoons that are a haven for native and migratory birds as well as marine mammals, reptiles, and amphibians. \nUnderstand.\nThe park covers an area of 56,200 hectares.\nHistory.\nThe long barrier island with its sprawling mangrove swamps and inland lagoons was established as part of the Colombian national parks system in 1964. The Cienega swamps (inland side of the Isla Salamanca barrier island) are designated as a UNESCO Biosphere Reserve and as a Ramsar wetland of international importance.\nLandscape.\nTourists will normally travel along the 60-km-long parkway, which runs along a coastal barrier island (Isla de Salamanca). Sandy beaches line much of the coast along with mangrove swamps. Forests line the banks of the Rio Magdalena. The sheltered lagoon side of the island has shallow lagoons with aquatic grasses.\nFlora and fauna.\nThe predominant plant life are the gnarly stands of mangrove in the park's many swamps and salt marshes. These are mostly black mangrove and white mangrove with stands of buttonwood. \nBirds are the most diverse form of fauna with some 200 identified species in the park, representing endemic and native species as well as migratory birds since the park lies at the South American convergence of flyways across the Caribbean as well as across Central America. There are many aquatic bird species who find plentiful food sources in the lagoons.\nMore than 100 fish species have been identified in the park, including both saltwater species and freshwater species.\nSnakes thrive in the mangrove swamps and wetlands throughout the park and alligators live in both the saltwater and freshwater lagoons.\nClimate.\nThe climate is tropical with very warm temperatures that average about 30 \u00b0C. Temperatures are relatively stable year-round. The park receives little rainfall with as little as 400 mm per year in the eastern park to about twice that in the somewhat wetter and more humid western side of the park. Beach lovers will enjoy over 300 days of sunshine per year.\nGet in.\nThe nearest major airport is in Barranquilla (). From Barranquilla, guided tours go through the park, often stopping at scenic beaches, birdwatching spots, or other points of interest. You could also rent a car and drive along the parkway yourself.\nThe parkways is designated as federal highway (ruta Nacional) 90, also identified as \"Troncal del Caribe\". From Barranquilla, drive east towards Ci\u00e9naga and Santa Marta. \nFees and permits.\nThere is no fee or permit required for this park. \nGet around.\nThe best way to get around is by car (or tour bus/van). There are numerous stopping points and side roads off the main parkway where you can access hiking trails, beaches, or scenic overlooks (miradors). \nBoats can be hired at various points, or arranged in Cienega. Boats are really the only way to get into the mangrove swamps or the small estuaries where birds and other wildlife can be spotted. There are small islands, such as Marchena, that can also be visited on a boat tour. A motorized boat with a local guide is the most comfortable option, but hardier or more adventurous visitors may prefer to rent a kayak and paddle through the swamps and lagoons.\nDo.\nunpaved dirt trails with boardwalks over wetlands. A popular trail is at the Los Cucos Park Administrative Center \nSleep.\nThere are hotels in nearby Cienega, as well as Santa Marta or Barranquilla. "}
{"id": "215239", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=215239", "title": "Pabell\u00f3n de Arteaga", "text": "Pabell\u00f3n de Arteaga is a small city in the central Mexican state of Aguascalientes. It is developing a growing reputation for its wineries and is the epicenter of the state's \"Ruta de Vino\" (Aguascalientes Wine Trail). Travelers with an interest in history can learn about its role in the Mexican Revolution and will also find several nearby Haciendas (most re-purposed as venues for weddings and other private events).\nUnderstand.\nThe city itself has a population of about 30,000 people. When outlying villages and farming communities are counted, the municipality has a total population of more than 47,000 (2020).\nGet in.\nThe nearest airport with commercial flights is in Aguascalientes ('). A taxi from the airport will take about 30 minutes to reach Pabellon de Arteaga and will cost about M$200 (US$10). Alternatively, you may find better flights to Zacatecas ('), which is about an hour by taxi (69 km).\nBy car, drive north from Aguascalientes on federal highway MEX-45 for 30 kilometers. \nGet around.\nTaxis are readily available. There are two sitios where they wait for fares\u02d0 on the north side of Plaza Principal (Zocalo) and a few blocks east of the plaza at the corner of Av. 16 de Septiembre and Av. Plutarco Elias (near the town letters).\nCombis (shared shuttle vans) leave every 30 minutes towards Aguascalientes.\nSleep.\nThe town lacks good hotel choices, but some wineries may offer overnight guest rooms, and a large selection of hotels is available in and around Aguascalientes, to the south."}
{"id": "215253", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=215253", "title": "Manpo", "text": "Manpo (\ub9cc\ud3ec\uc2dc) is a border city across the Yalu River from China in Chagang province in North Korea.\nUnderstand.\nIn 2019 a border crossing opened between Manpo and Ji'an, allowing Chinese tour groups to visit Manpo, with plans for other nationalities to visit as well.\nNorth Korea ceased all foreign tours into the country in early 2020, and is still closed as of January 2026 with the exception of Russian nationals. \nno population data for this page\nGet in.\nIt is possible to enter Manpo as part of a guided day trip from Ji'an in the Jilin province of China.\nSee.\nChagang province is the most restricted province in North Korea for tourists to travel to. Prior to the opening of Manpo, the only place tourists could enter was Huichon, and that was only to provide accommodation at the Huichon Hotel for tourists visiting the nearby Ryongmun Cavern or Mt Myohyang.\nAs such, Manpo is the only city in the Chagang province tourists can take travel around as part of a guided tour.\nDo.\nAs part of a guided tour, tourists may visit the Manpo Pavilion, Junggang Revolutionary Museum, Junggang Farm, Junggang School, Manpo Wool Mill, Manpo Children's Traffic Park and Manpo City Kindergarten.\nEat.\nTour groups are provided with lunch at Manpo Hotel, the main hotel for foreigners in Manpo. Patrons can expect North Korean cuisine such as cold noodles and kimchi.\nSleep.\nAlthough the Manpo Hotel exists as a lunch stop for tourists on day trips, it has not been confirmed if tourists are permitted to stay in Manpo overnight.\nGo next.\nFrom Manpo it is possible for foreign tourists to travel to Pyongyang by train. Tourists on a guided day trip would return to Ji'an."}
{"id": "215312", "revid": "2432955", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=215312", "title": "Mandalika", "text": "Mandalika is a resort area in Lombok.\nUnderstand.\nThis area is designated as a special economic zone which stretches over 1,035.67 hectares of the southern coast of the island of Lombok. \nGet in.\nMandalika is 17 km straight from Lombok International Airport and approximately 50 km from the downtown of Mataram, the province's capital city.\nDo.\nSurfing.\nAccessible via land by a long ride from Kuta or via boat from the fishing harbour of Awang.\nCan be reached by paddling about 500 m out or hiring a boat (lift) for around "}
{"id": "215319", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=215319", "title": "Narsipatnam", "text": "Narsipatnam in North Andhra serves as the gateway to the Eastern Ghats of Andhra Pradesh. Often overlooked as just a transit hub, it is the starting point for journeys into the \"Agency\" areas, including the famous hill station of Lambasingi. The town is nestled between the coast and the mountains, making it a unique mix of plains culture and tribal mountain influence.\nGet in.\nBy bus.\nNarsipatnam has a very active APSRTC bus station with direct connections to major cities.\nBy train.\nThe Narsipatnam Road (NRP) railway station is located about 40 km from the main town but is not used much. Lot more trains connect to Anakapalli and Tuni.\nEat.\nMangilal Sweets - Marwari Sweetshop but it's a historic local favourite. Samosas are quite good(10rs). They serve pakodis, mirchi bada, chat and the usual stuff.\nDrink.\nThere are three or four wine stores in town, and one functioning bar. Public drinking is pretty common. A lot of pan shops let you have a drink beside them if you buy something. A fun sub culture but keep an eye out for the police. They wouldn't do much but expect a bribe."}
{"id": "215341", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=215341", "title": "Rioverde", "text": "Rioverde is a small city in the south-central part of the state of San Luis Potosi in the Bajio region of central Mexico. The town has a picturesque colonial centro but is most visited by travelers who enjoy natural attractions, like \"Laguna de la Media Luna\".\nUnderstand.\nThe city has a population of about 60,000 (2020), but the farms and villages in the surrounding municipal areas increases its overstated population to about 100,000. \nGet in.\nThe nearest airport with commercial flights is in San Luis Potosi, which receives mostly domestic flights on Aeromexico, Aerus, and Volaris as well as limited international service on American Airlines from DFW and United from IAH. \nBy bus.\nRioverde is served by very frequent departures from San Luis Potosi. Autonaves buses leave every 30 minutes during the day and only somewhat less frequently at night. The 150-km trip costs as little as M$200.\nDo.\nPeople enjoy the outdoors in Rioverde, where there are several parks close to the city with natural attractions within easy driving distance including many waterfalls near Tamasopo, as well as deep caves and mountainous, wooded hiking trails. "}
{"id": "215357", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=215357", "title": "United States National Forests", "text": "\"Not to be confused with the United States National Park System, a collection of smaller parks that includes the best-of-the-best natural attractions.\"\nThe United States Forest Service operates 174 National Forests and National Grasslands, managing huge expanses of forests, woodlands and other natural areas for outdoor recreation and other uses.\nUnderstand.\nThere are 154 national forests and 20 national grasslands containing 193,000,000 acre, about 8.5% of the total land area of the U.S. They are owned collectively by the American people through the federal government. Most lie in the Western U.S. and Alaska, particularly in mountain ranges.\nThe United States Forest Service (USFS), a division of the U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA), manages these areas. In addition to recreation, the USFS also coordinates forestry, livestock grazing and wildlife management. Unlike areas run by the National Park Service, Forest Service lands are often working lands, seeking to balance the needs of preservation and recreation with the needs of industry and resource extraction. Federal lands that are less mountainous or less vegetated tend to be administered by the \"Bureau of Land Management (BLM)\", with its own policies and land use priorities.\nFees and permits.\nNational Forests are almost always free to drive through and make short stops along the way. The more popular ones may charge a small fee for parking passes or for specific facilities such as visitor centers. Day passes are typically $5-10, annual passes a bit more. Campgrounds are a mix of free and paid, though dispersed camping and backpacking are typically free.\nThe America the Beautiful Pass, also called the Interagency Pass, provides entry to all national parks, as well as some national monuments, national wildlife refuges, and national forests. A single pass applies for an entire group traveling together in a private vehicle, or up to four people on foot or bike. There are several pass types:\nIn 2026, the National Park Service offers free admission to all national parks on 10 days:\nRecreation.gov, operated by a contractor on behalf of the federal government, is the most widely-used reservation service for federal trails and campgrounds. Each facility uses the site a little differently: some open up reservation in large blocks at the beginning of the year, some have rolling openings 2-12 months out. A few trails and campsites are highly competitive: people wait on the site in the minutes before reservations open, and then snap up openings within seconds.\nGet around.\nForest roads.\nThe Forest Service Road System is a collection of rural and forest roads built and maintained by the USFS, designated with \"NF\" and a number. They were built with logging and wildfire in mind, but today are primarily used for recreation access.\nLess than a quarter of all Forest Service roads are maintained for passenger cars, and only about 7% are two-lane or asphalt-surfaced. Some public forest roads may be closed seasonally to prevent damage to the road, or may have other restrictions, such as only allowing high-clearance vehicles. Most ranger stations and forest websites maintain recent reports on road conditions and closures, and trip reports from recreation communities include details as well. Check these before heading out or else you might need to turn around.\nSleep.\nCampgrounds.\nForest Service campgrounds offer fewer amenities and services compared to National Parks and private campgrounds. They are also much cheaper, and many are free.\nDispersed camping.\nNational Forests generally allow \"dispersed\" camping, outside of a designated campground: a pullout along the road, a remote patch of desert, a desolate beach, or nearly anywhere else you can drive up to and pitch a tent without bothering someone. These areas are typically free, or covered by cheap annual recreation passes. You need to be self-contained, as there are no amenities like water, restrooms or trash cans.\nThe following are typical restrictions for BLM or USFS lands. Contact the local agency office to check for any needed permits or special rules:\nDestinations.\nThis is not a comprehensive list of National Forests. There are many more described in the articles for nearby cities and the states that contain them. Some forests have more recreation opportunities than others.\nRespect.\nShare the land. Expect to bump into hikers, horse riders, offroaders, rockhounds, boondockers, survivalists, hunters, loggers, and cattle, as most forests explicitly allow all of these users. Compared to national parks, there is much more of an \"anything goes\" approach to national forests. Land managers must balance all of these uses, and not everybody is happy with the balance. Local workers can have wildly different opinions than out-of-town recreationists, which makes for sensitive topics that can become politically-charged.\nTread lightly. Follow Leave-No-Trace principles in relatively undisturbed areas, and don't leave a mess in places with more human impact. Collection of rocks, plants and other natural products is often allowed, but only after obtaining a permit, typically in-person at a ranger station."}
{"id": "215365", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=215365", "title": "Ribadesella", "text": "Ribadesella is a beach resort in Asturias on the north coast of Spain. It's known as \"Ribeseya\" in Asturian, and in 2024 had a population of 5500.\nGet in.\nRibadesella is 68 km east of Gij\u00f3n and 167 km west of Santander. A-8 is the fast coastal highway, branch off onto N-632.\nAlsa buses run every hour from Oviedo taking 70 min, and every hour or two from Gij\u00f3n taking 1 hr 45 min. Both run via Villaviciosa. \nFrom Santander you usually change at Llanes, but one bus daily is direct taking 2 hr 15 min.\n is on the east riverbank.\nRibadesella is on the Oviedo-Santander line. Four regional trains a day run from Oviedo, taking 2 hr 30 min, and continue to Llanes; two of them go on to Santander. They need to be booked in the website. There is also one reinforcement train during the Descent.\n is a simple platform halt 500 m southeast of town centre.\nGet around.\nThe cave is within walking distance.\nTaxis wait by the main roundabout.\nBuy.\nCarrefour Express is by the roundabout at Palacio Vald\u00e9s 1, open daily 09:00-21:00.\nDrink.\nOver a dozen bars in town centre.\nCider is the chief drink in Asturias, though the region produces some wine.\nConnect.\nAs of Jan 2026, Ribadesella and its approach roads have 5G from all Spanish carriers."}
{"id": "215397", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=215397", "title": "Sierra Madre", "text": "Mexico's Sierra Madre is a mountain range that covers most of Mexico. Almost all Mexican states have mountainous areas that are part of the Sierra Madre. The Sierra Madre are often referred to as three major mountain ranges surrounding a large central high plain known as the \"Altiplano\". Several sub-ranges are commonly referred to locally, though all are part of the Sierra Madre.\nUnderstand.\nThe three major ranges that comprise the Sierra Madre are\u02d0\nThe Altiplano is a high-plains region that lies between the Sierra Madre Occidental and Sierra Madre Oriental. \nThe Eje Volcanico is a belt of high geologic activity that includes a concentration of active volcanoes and 8 of the country's 10 highest peaks. \nThe Sierra de Baja California is a range that runs across the length of the Baja peninsula. Geologically, it is part of the Sierra Madre Occidental (despite having the Gulf of California in between).\nDestinations.\nNational parks.\nMany Mexican national parks are heavily mountainous. Parks in the Sierra Madre mountains include\u02d0\nVolcanoes.\nA region of very high volcanic activity called the \"Trans Mexican Volcano Belt\" stretches across Central Mexico and includes areas of both the Sierra Madre Occidental and the Sierra Madre Oriental. This is not a separate mountain range, but is an area with frequent eruptions and earthquakes. It is also a concentration of Mexico's highest peaks. The 19 highest peaks in Mexico are all within this area, including three peaks over 5,000 m (16,404 ft): Pico de Orizaba (5,610 m), Popocatapetl (5,426 m), and Iztaccihuatl (5,220 m). Many of the major mountains and volcanoes of this region are part of Mexico national parks.\nInteresting volcanoes in the Sierra Madre include\u02d0\nMountain towns.\nThere are thousands of small mountain towns throughout the Sierra Madre. Many towns in the Bajio region were wealthy mining towns. The most scenic and tranquil towns earn the designation of Pueblo M\u00e1gico. Some interesting mountain towns include\u02d0"}
{"id": "215398", "revid": "2361892", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=215398", "title": "Tiwi", "text": "Tiwi is a municipality in the northeast quadrant of Albay, Philippines at the endpoint of Legazpi-Tiwi coastal road and borders the municipality of Sag\u00f1ay in Camarines Sur's Partido District. Being a neighboring municipality of Malinao, its shoreline is facing Lagonoy Gulf. It is known for its pottery industry and the geothermal power plant at the foot of Mount Malinao.\nUnderstand.\nTiwi is the home of the Shrine of Our Lady of Salvation at Barangay Joroan. The municipality is dubbed as the Pilgrim capital of Albay.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest airport is in Daraga ().\nBy bus.\nThere are buses from Manila to Tiwi, stopping also at other towns and cities along the way, and there are also UV Express vans from Legazpi and jeepneys from Tabaco.\nGet around.\nAround the town center there are pedicabs while tricycles are available heading to the outlying barangays."}
{"id": "215413", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=215413", "title": "Tequila Trail", "text": "Tequila Trail is an itinerary to tour the tequila distilleries and agave fields around the town of Tequila in Jalisco, Mexico. The tequila-producing region with its distilleries and agave fields is a UNESCO World Heritage Site. Trips start in Guadalajara. They typically begin with a train trip or a bus trip to Tequila and usually visit two other nearby tequila-producing towns\u02d0 Amatitan and El Arenal. The traditional Tequila Trail has been a popular tourist activity since the 1970s.\nIn 2025, the Jalisco Tourism Ministry introduced a new route, called the Los Altos Tequila Trail, that includes different tequila producers in the highland towns that ring the Tequila Valley. These distilleries and their agave fields are at higher elevations and produce tequilas with a sweeter flavor profile and different terroir than those produced in the lower valley. There is no overlap between the two routes, and a serious tequila aficionado will want to experience both routes.\nUnderstand.\nProducers.\nTequila is an appellation that is legally defined and regulated by an official organization called the \"Consejo Regulador del Tequila\" (CRT). They set standards that producers must follow, maintain a registry of all tequila producers, set quality standards, establish trade protections through international agreements, and promote tequila around the world. It's a big business.\nThere are more than 200 licensed tequila distilleries. The vast majority are in the state of Jalisco, and the best known of those, around the town of Tequila. Every distillery has a unique identification number that must be displayed on every bottle they sell. This is sometimes on the label on back of a bottle, but can be a sticker affixed to the bottle. Tequila aficionados look for the registration number (referred to as the \"NOM\"). With the number, they can find out what distillery produced their bottle, where it's located, and what other tequilas they sell. If you've got a bottle of a \"vanity tequila\" (in which famous celebrities promote \"their\" tequila brand), you can find out who really produces the tequila behind the celebrity. Most celebrities obviously know nothing about tequila and have never stepped foot into a distillery, but occasionally one surprises you. Dwayne \"The Rock\" Johnson touts \"his\" tequila (called Teremana), and a bit of research shows that, in fact, he \"did\" establish his own distillery that produces only his brand to his specifications. His distillery (NOM 1613) is in the Highland town of Jesus Maria, and it really does make only artesanal tequila with traditional processes, but unfortunately for the traveler, is not open to public tours.\nTastings.\nVisits to tequila distilleries usually include a tasting session that features the distillery's house brands. Most group tours also include tastings that feature tequilas from multiple distilleries. Here's what to expect and how to get the most out of the experience.\nA tasting is called a \"cata\" and the expert who presents a tasting is the \"catador\". A catador is to tequila as a sommelier is to wine\u02d0 not necessarily an expert at making the stuff, but an expert at experiencing the different types and the flavors and aromas that exemplify each of them. Tequila samples are poured in \"flights\" so they can be evaluated side by side. Typically, these flights include as many as 8 1/2 ounce samples. \nWhen you are given a tequila sample, first look at its appearance and gauge its color and clarity. Then sniff it to see what aromas jump out at you. Is there one that dominates? Can you name it? (for example, is it like limes, or fresh mown grass?) Swirl the sample and inhale deeply. Does the second impression of its aroma change your mind? Then take a sip and see what flavors you can identify. Talk to your companions at the tasting and compare notes. If someone sitting next to you identifies a different aroma or flavor, can you pick out that flavor yourself? Listen to what the catador finds in the sample when he or she tastes it. Do you taste, smell, or see the same things they mention? Take a swallow of the tequila and pay attention to how it feels as it crosses your palate and goes down your throat. You might feel a burn or maybe it comes across as very smooth to you. What does the catador and your tasting companions think about it. Do you have an overall conclusion? Good stuff, or not one of your favorites?\nIf you're serious about getting the most out of your tequila experiences, bring a small notebook and jot down impressions of each tequila you taste, otherwise, you're sure to forget a few by the end of a long day of tequila tours!\nTraditional Tequila Trail.\nThe Tequila Valley represents the \"Lowlands\" agave fields and distilleries. It is the traditional \"Tequila Trail\" experience and features major distilleries like Jose Cuervo, Casa Sauza, and Herradura, as well as expansive agave fields and smaller, Artesanal distilleries. Tours to the Tequila Valley are often in large groups that may be transported via train (\"Tequila Express\") or large, luxury motor coaches. Most tours to the town of Tequila will visit a major distillery, agave fields, and will typically include lunch or dinner at a typical Mexican restaurant in town, often accompanied by copious samples of tequila and accompanied by mariachi music. Mariachi is recognized by UNESCO as part of the intangible culture of Mexico and the state of Jalisco boasts that it is the birthplace of the vibrant musical style.\nLos Altos Tequila Trail.\nThe Los Altos region (Highlands) lie about 2 hours drive northeast of Guadalajara and surround the Tequila Valle (Lowloands), which includes the town of Tequila itself. Towns on the Los Altos route generally lie at an elevation of 5,000 to 7,000 feet (1500 to 2100 meters) while the Tequila Valley lies at just under 4,000 feet (1200 meters). Besides elevation, other factors affecting the \"terroir\" of Highlands tequilas are cooler temperatures with wider daily and seasonal temperature variations, and significant differences in the soil where the color is generally much blacker than the red color of the valley soils. The practice of purchasing agave from independent producers can make it difficult to discern terroir effects in most tequilas, but a number of small artisanal distilleries produce estate-label tequilas that are made only from agaves grown on an individual property, making it possible for a discerning palate to isolate and identify flavor characteristics of a particular producer.\nGet in.\nTrips to the Tequila Trail usually start in Guadalajara. \nBy air.\nGuadalara has a large, busy international airport (). It receives connecting flights on domestic carriers from Mexico City, Canc\u00fan, and Monterrey. More than 30 international gateways offer flights to Guadalajara with the most frequent flights coming from Los Angeles and Houston. \nFrom Guadalajara, bus and train is the most common way to reach Tequila, though a number of tour operators have van service, particularly on the Los Altos Tequila Trail.\nStay safe.\nThe tours and tasting events described in this article are intended for adults who understand and accept the potential risks of consuming alcoholic beverages. Tequila tastings are intended for adults of legal drinking age. The drinking age in Mexico is 18. Participants should be aware of their own limits and avoid excess drinking. Do not drive a vehicle after drinking. Go with a tour group or have a designated driver. Aside from the obvious risks of being involved in an accident, be aware that the state of Jalisco is stricter than most parts of Mexico when it comes to drunk driving. Police have no tolerance for intoxication and the state law defines drunkenness as over 0.04 percent (whereas 0.08 is most common in other parts of the country)."}
{"id": "215426", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=215426", "title": "Ha'il", "text": "Ha'il (Arabic: \u062d\u0627\u0626\u0644) is the capital of the Ha'il Region in Nejd, north-central Saudi Arabia. Ha'il is famous for ancient rock art and rugged mountain scenery. \nUnderstand.\nHistorically it was a stop on the camel caravan routes and the Hajj pilgrimage path from Iraq. \nIt has a population of 500,000. Ha'il is at a higher elevation (approx. 1,000 m) than Riyadh or Jeddah, making its climate slightly more temperate.\nGet in.\nBy train.\nThe North-South Railway, operated by SAR (Saudi Arabia Railways), connects Ha'il to Riyadh (approx. 4. hr 30 min) and Al-Jouf/Qurayyat. The station is about 15 km north of the city center. \"Trains only operate six times per week.\"\nBy car.\nHa'il is well-connected by modern highways:\nFrom Riyadh: Approx. 600 km (6 hours) via Highway 65.\nFrom Medina: Approx. 430 km (4 hr 30 min) via Highway 15.\nGet around.\nBy taxi.\nUber and Careem are plentiful.\nBy car.\nSeveral car rental companies have desks at the airport.\nGo next.\nAl-Ula: 4 hr 30 min-drive west."}
{"id": "215430", "revid": "2355439", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=215430", "title": "Daisen (Akita)", "text": "\"Not to be confused with Daisen (Tottori).\"\nDaisen (\u5927\u4ed9\u5e02 Daisen shi) is a city in Akita Prefecture with a population of about 75,000 (2023). It is known for hosting the National Fireworks Competition, held every August.\nUnderstand.\nTourist information.\nDaisen City Government official website (only in Japanese)"}
{"id": "215443", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=215443", "title": "Fengdu", "text": "Fengdu is a county in Chongqing Municipality.\nUnderstand.\nFengdu's county seat is a river town on the banks of the Yangtze (Chang Jiang) River, about two hours by bus or one hour by train from Chongqing City. \nGet in.\nBy train.\nTrains to Fengdu from central Chongqing mostly depart from Chongqing North Railway Station.\nThere is one railway station in Fengdu:\nA second railway station, which will be named Fengdu North Railway Station, is under construction and will be opened in 2027 as part of the new Chongqing-Wanzhou line.\nBy bus.\nBus services from central Chongqing are provided via the Chaotianmen Bus Station, the Sigongli Transport Hub and several other bus stations. There are also buses to Fengdu from other parts of the municipality such as Fuling and Wulong.\nThere is one long-distance bus station in Fengdu:\nBy boat.\nYangtze River cruise boats often make a stop in Fengdu."}
{"id": "215450", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=215450", "title": "Barrydale", "text": "Barrydale is a small town on Route 62 in the Little Karoo. Barrydale is a centre of fruit growing on the edge of the Little Karoo. \nUnderstand.\nIt was founded in 1880. no population data for this page Almost every type of fruit is cultivated here, with the harvest season beginning in December with apricots and continuing into April."}
{"id": "215487", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=215487", "title": "Winter sports in Japan", "text": "Famed for its copious snowfall and \"champagne powder\", Japan has become a winter sports Mecca drawing in visitors from far and wide. Most of them seem to pack into the same few famous resorts, but the country boasts over 500 ski areas and you're sure to find something for your taste and experience level.\nUnderstand.\nSkiing was only introduced to Japan in 1911, when Austrian Lieutenant Colonel Theodore Edler von Lerch was brought in to teach modern mountain warfare in Niigata. The Sapporo Winter Olympics in 1972 put Japan on the map, kicking off a domestic ski boom that saw over 700 ski resorts open up by the 1980s. The popping of the Bubble in 1989 put a damper on things, but the Nagano Winter Olympics in 1998 started bringing in international skiers and things really took off in the 2000s when Australian skiers discovered Niseko and investment started pouring in.\nIn practical terms, this means that Japan's ski resorts can be broadly divided into two groups: the shiny new resorts inhaling a torrent of investment and cashed-up tourists, with all the latest mod cons and price tags to match; and Bubble-era resorts from the 1980s, cheap and largely undiscovered by non-Japanese, but with aging, limited facilities.\nGeography.\nJapan's ski resorts are nearly all clustered on northern island of Hokkaido and the western Japan Sea coast, which get the most reliable snowfall. The eastern Pacific Ocean coast, where the major cities like Tokyo are all located, is sheltered by the Japanese Alps and gets little if any snow.\nWhen to go.\nThe Japanese ski season lasts from early December to late April. The northern island of Hokkaido, being colder, has the longest season, but even there you'll want to aim for January-February for the most reliable powder.\nThe peak seasons are around New Year's, when both Japanese and international visitors have their holidays, and Chinese New Year (dates vary), when Chinese skiers fly in by the planeload. January also tends to be busy thanks to Australian summer holidays.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nBeing an island nation, the vast majority of visitors will need to fly in to the country first.\nThe other option is to transit via another country. Taipei in Taiwan and Seoul's Incheon are particularly popular choices, with direct flights to regional Japan airports like Sendai.\nBy train.\nThe Japanese rail system is justly famous and you're highly likely to find yourself on a train at some point. Particularly if arriving in Tokyo, connecting to the Shinkansen (bullet train) is often the fastest and most comfortable way to get to your destination. Gala Yuzawa holds the unique distinction of being Japan's only ski resort with a dedicated Shinkansen station.\nBeware that Japanese trains are generally not well equipped carrying for bulky luggage, so you'll want to check ahead if you're planning on bringing skis or snowboards. Alternatively, consider using a courier service, which can ship your luggage to/from your resort cheaply and reliably. There are courier counters at all major airports and resorts.\nBy bus.\nMany ski resorts offer direct buses to/from nearby airports. This is typically slower than the train but far less hassle, and recommended particularly if you have luggage. Advance reservations are typically mandatory.\nThere are also long-distance buses from Tokyo direct to major ski resorts, particularly those around Nagano, with services operated by Nagano Snow Shuttle and other companies. Expect to spend upwards of 5 hours on the bus if you opt for these.\nBy car.\nDriving in Japan poses a number of challenges starting from the licensing process, and the sheer amount of snow means that this is not the best place to learn winter driving for the first time. Nevertheless, for off-the-beaten-track resorts, it may still be the best option.\nEat.\nIn Japan, expect to eat Japanese food: hearty meals like curry rice, ramen noodles and various rice bowls with toppings are particularly popular at ski resorts. The international resorts have a more varied food scene, but even the smaller resorts will usually have a token pizza place, burger joint and Indian restaurant if you're craving something different.\nDrink.\nDon't come to a Japanese ski resort looking for nightclubs, there is little in the way of an \"apr\u00e8s-ski\" scene in most of them. The notable exception is Niseko, which has become quite the party town for the young Australians who flock there.\nSleep.\nTraditional Japanese ski resorts cater mostly to locals making short trips of one or two nights, meaning they rarely have self-catering facilities and the like. However, the newer resorts catering to international visitors staying a week or longer have a better supply of European/Australian-style serviced apartments, at European/Australian prices.\nMany ski resorts in volcanic Japan incorporate a hot spring (\"onsen\"), which is a wonderful way to wind down after a day out on the slopes. You can also try a night or two in a traditional \"ryokan\" inn.\nPracticalities.\nThe prices of lift passes generally range from \u00a54,000 for older, smaller resorts to as much as \u00a516,000 per day at the largest and best equipped, like Rusutsu. Hefty discounts are often available for locals and in the shoulder season, while at some larger resorts you can also opt to pay even more for express lines at lifts.\nThe Epic Pass is valid at Hakuba and Rusutsu.\nJapanese slopes (\u30b2\u30ec\u30f3\u30c7 \"gerende\", from the German \"Gel\u00e4nde\") usually use a three-tier grading system similar to the European one:\nTree runs.\nTrue off-piste skiing is not common in Japan, but tree runs, where you ski or snowboard in semi-controlled terrain (trees that have had their low-hanging branches trimmed etc) are increasingly popular. Tree run grading varies by resort, but they're typically numeric, starting from \"1\" as the easiest.\nTree runs are considered advanced skiing and you enter them at your own risk. Avalanches are a possibility and so is getting lost if the weather turns bad. Resorts charge steep search and recovery fees if you get injured or lost.\nEquipment.\nThere are numerous ski and snowboard rental shops in every larger resort. Gear rental (skis, poles, boots, helmet) is around \u00a55000/day at the resorts, less off-resort. The choice is normally best made by convenience to the slopes or to accommodation. You can usually fit your equipment the previous day, which avoids the wait in the morning when you could be out skiing already.\nIn smaller resorts, rental boots may not be available in sizes above 28-29 cm (11 inches) or so. Confirm ahead of time if you have large feet, and remember ski boot sizing usually runs large, meaning most people fit one size smaller than their regular shoes.\nHelmets are not required by law in Japan, but you'd be a fool not to wear one, and some resorts are starting to mandate them as a condition for accessing their lifts.\nTuition.\nMost Japanese ski instructors only speak Japanese. Largest, more internationally geared resorts have English-speaking instructors, but they're often limited and charge a premium.\nWhere to go.\nThe following is a selection of some of the larger, better known ski resorts in Japan.\nHokkaido.\nAs the northernmost island, Hokkaido has the longest ski season, the most reliable \"japow\" (Japanese powder) and the most famous resorts. The flip side is that it's often quite cold (in Jan/Feb, -15\u00b0C on the slopes is normal) and prices are high.\nTohoku.\nOften overlooked by travelers, the northern side of main island Honshu has huge amounts of snowfall (Appi averages 12 meters per year) and several large resorts that are not yet entirely overrun by tourists. The flip side is awkward access: it can take a solid 4-5 hours from Tokyo to get to your destination.\nKanto.\nThe mountainous prefecture of Gunma is just north of Tokyo and hence popular with locals.\nChubu.\nThe central part of Honshu contains the spine of the Japanese Alps. In particular, the prefecture of Nagano boasts 80 ski resorts, with Niigata hosting another 50. Very popular with Japanese visitors due to comparatively easy access from the major cities of Tokyo, Nagoya and Kansai.\nChugoku.\nThe perpetually rainy (and, in winter, snowy) San'in region of Shimane and Tottori on the west side of Honshu is entirely off the radar of international tourists, but hosts a dozen ski resorts. The local season here is a bit shorter than the rest of the country, running from late December to March.\nStay safe.\nEnsure your travel insurance is in order and specifically covers snow sports. Medical care in Japan is quite good, but medical evacuations are still extremely expensive.\nOff-piste skiing is always dangerous, but off-piste skiing when there's tons of lovely fresh powder is even more dangerous.\nAvalanches (\u96ea\u5d29 \"nadare\"), tree wells (\u30c4\u30ea\u30fc\u30db\u30fc\u30eb \"tree hole\") and blizzards (\u5439\u96ea \"fubuki\") can all trap the unwary, and 30-40 skiers are killed in Japan every year, around 40% from head trauma (wear a helmet!) and another 40% from asphyxiation.\nDon't fret \"too\" much though, since the long-term average is still only around 0.7 fatalities per 1 million skiing days."}
{"id": "215488", "revid": "692", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=215488", "title": "Rusutsu", "text": "Rusutsu (\u30eb\u30b9\u30c4) is the largest ski resort in Hokkaido, Japan.\nUnderstand.\nThe ski resort extends over three mountains; West Mountain, East Mountain, and Mount Isola. The total number of courses is 37, with 19 lifts including 4 gondolas and a total length of 42 kilometres (26 mi).\nGet in.\nThe easiest way in is to fly to Sapporo's New Chitose Airport and take the direct bus to the resort (2 hours, \u00a55500). There are up to 8 services per day in ski season and you can book online for a small discount. \nDuring ski season only, there is also a single bus per day from Sapporo city (2 hours, free) and, for day trippers only, Niseko (45 min, \u00a59000 round-trip, as of Jan 2026).\nThe nearest town of any size is Kutchan, but there is no public transport and a taxi will cost you \u00a512,000 one way (as of Jan 2026).\nSee and do.\nWhile Rusutsu positions itself as an all-year resort, odds are you're here for the skiing.\nLift pass prices here are likely the highest in all Japan, with a regular day pass clocking in at a whopping \u00a516,200 (2026). Discounts available for 5-hour passes, night skiing, children, seniors, and advance bookings online.\nIn addition to the usual daily passes, there are two more unusual options. The \"25-hour pass\" costs \u00a534,700 (as of Jan 2026), but gives you access to the lifts in 1-hour blocks, with one per hour deducted as you use them, meaning you can probably get several days' worth of use from one. You can also opt for a pay-as-you-go \"point\" model, with 1 to 4 points per lift at \u00a5800 per point.\nAlong with Hakuba, Rusutsu is one of only two ski resorts in Japan that accepts the Epic Pass."}
{"id": "215490", "revid": "2182907", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=215490", "title": "Hechuan", "text": "Hechuan is a district in Chongqing Municipality about 70 km northwest of downtown Chongqing. The main visitor attractions in Hechuan are the Diaoyu Fortress and the old town of Laitan.\nUnderstand.\nHechuan\u2019s main urban area is at the confluence of the Fu and Jialing rivers. Hechuan is a district in the northern part of Chongqing, where the Jialing, Fu and Qu rivers meet. The meeting of the three rivers has played an important role in the city\u2019s development and has also shaped its riverside landscape. Hechuan is well known for its history, especially the Battle of Diaoyu Fortress during the Southern Song dynasty. Today, visitors can experience both its historical sites and its traditional riverside culture.\nGet around.\nBy bus\nPublic buses cover the main urban areas and many tourist attractions in Hechuan. They are an inexpensive way to travel around the city. \nBy taxi or ride-hailing \nTaxis and ride-hailing services are convenient for travelling between the railway station, city centre and tourist attractions.\nBy bicycle\nBicycles can be rented for exploring the riverside areas. Cycling along the rivers is a good way to enjoy Hechuan\u2019s scenery. \nDo.\nCruise boats depart from a pier in the middle of Dongjintuo Riverside Park. The fare is \u00a558 for a daytime sightseeing cruise and \u00a568 for a nighttime cruise (as of Jan 2026). A round trip to the old fishing town of Caojie (\u8349\u8857) costs \u00a598. Discounts are available for children and seniors. Tickets may be purchased on the WeChat account of the Hechuan Sanjiang Cruise Boat Company (\u5408\u5ddd\u4e09\u6c5f\u6e38\u8239\u516c\u53f8)."}
{"id": "215496", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=215496", "title": "Atotonilco", "text": "Atotonilco is a small town in the mountains that surround the Tequila Valley in Jalisco, Mexico. The town is a key destination on Jalisco's Los Altos Tequila Trail because it is the home of Patron and Don Julio, as well as several smaller artesanal distilleries. \nUnderstand.\nThe town has a population of just under 29,000 residents. There are over 100 small villages in the surrounding countryside, that when added to the town's population, make for a municipal population of about 64,000 (2020). \nThe town sits at an elevation of about 1,600 meters, giving it a cooler, more temperate climate than the valley below. Tequilas produced from agaves grown at higher elevations are regarded as sweeter in flavor than their lowland brethren. \nGet in.\nThe closest major airport is in Guadalajara (). From Guadalajara, you can rent a car to explore the Tequila area, or you can take one of the numerous tours. Most of the \"Los Altos\" tours are smaller than the tour groups going to the town of Tequila and will most often use a large van, like a Toyota HiLux rather than a motor coach.\nBy bus.\nCoordinados (Primera Plus) operates buses from the main (new) bus station in Guadalajara to Atotonilco several times per day with departures about every 40 minutes. The 90-minute trip will cost about M$150.\nGet around.\nWalking is good for getting around the downtown area, but a car is useful for getting to places on the outskirts. Taxis can usually be found downtown near the main plaza or at the bus station, or your hotel front desk can call one for you."}
{"id": "215508", "revid": "2361892", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=215508", "title": "Malilipot", "text": "Malilipot is a municipality in the east coast of Albay. It is sandwiched between the city of Tabaco and the municipality of Bacacay. Its shoreline faces Lagonoy Gulf. The town is stretching along Legazpi-Tiwi coastal road and is one of the five municipalities that has a jurisdiction over the foothills of Mayon Volcano.\nUnderstand.\nThe name Malilipot comes from the Bikol word \"malipot\" which means \"cold\". Located at the east coast of the province, it has two mountains \u2014 Mount Bulakawan and Mount Toktokan that protect the entire town against the dangerous eruption of Mayon Volcano. Barangay San Roque is the only vulnerable place in town.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest airport is in Legazpi ().\nBy bus.\nThere are buses from Manila to Malilipot, stopping also at other towns and cities along the way, and there are also UV Express vans from Legazpi and jeepneys from Tabaco. \nBy car.\nIt is 20 km from Legazpi and 547 km from Manila.\nGet around.\nAround the town center there are pedicabs while tricycles are available heading to the outlying barangays.\nBuy.\nNative bags made of Abaca are sold as souvenirs.\nSleep.\nThere are two hotels in town and both of them are in Gamboa's Orchard."}
{"id": "215509", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=215509", "title": "Nozawa Onsen", "text": "Nozawa Onsen (\u91ce\u6ca2\u6e29\u6cc9 \"Nozawaonsen\") is a rural village turned ski resort in Nagano, Japan.\nUnderstand.\nIt began as a hot spring (\"onsen\") resort, but it's now best known as a major ski destination that is particularly popular among Australian visitors to Japan.\nno population data for this page\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThere are no major airports near Nozawa. Most visitors fly into Tokyo, or perhaps Nagoya, and continue by train.\nBy train.\nNozawa is not directly served by trains. The nearest station is Iiyama on the Hokuriku Shinkansen, 1 hr 45 min from Tokyo. From Iiyama, Nozawa Onsen Liner direct express buses connect to Nozawa Onsen in 25 minutes (\u00a5600/300 adult/child, as of Jan 2026).\nGet around.\nIn ski season, the Nowaza Free Shuttle runs around the village, twice an hour on weekdays and up to 5 times per hour at peak times on weekends.\nSee and do.\nSkiing.\nYou're probably here for the skiing. Lift passes start from \u00a57500/4500 adult/child per day, as of Jan 2026.\nThere's a maintained cross-country skiing track here as well, since this is where the Nagano Olympics biathlon races were held.\nHot springs.\nIf you're in the mood for a hot spring dip, the village boasts no less than 13 free-to-use onsen.\nSleep.\nA large part of Nozawa's charm are that there are no giant resort hotels: it's all smaller, non-chain lodges and chalets."}
{"id": "215517", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=215517", "title": "Minahasa", "text": "Minahasa is a region in Northern Sulawesi."}
